// Plain words for this work, by passage key. See ./index.ts.

export const PLAIN_PYRAMID_TEXTS: Record<string, string> = {
  "egy.pt.1": "Nut, the great shining goddess, says: this is my son, my firstborn, the king, the first to open my womb. He is my beloved, and I am pleased with him.",
  "egy.pt.2": "Geb says: this is my son, the king, born of my body. The rest of the text is broken and lost.",
  "egy.pt.3": "Nut, the great one in the lower mansion, says: this is my beloved eldest son, the king, who sits on the throne of Geb. Geb is pleased with him and gave him his inheritance before the Great Ennead. All the gods rejoice and say, \"How fine the king is! His father Geb is pleased with him.\"",
  "egy.pt.4": "Nut says to the king: I have given you your sister Isis, so that she may hold you and give you back the heart that belongs to your body.",
  "egy.pt.5": "Nut says to the king: I have given you your sister Nephthys, so that she may hold you and give you back the heart that belongs to your body.",
  "egy.pt.6": "Nut-Nekhbet, the great, says: this is my beloved son, the king. I have given him the horizons so that he may rule over them like Harachte. All the gods answer that the king truly is her favourite child, and that people will serve him as courtiers for ever.",
  "egy.pt.7": "Nut, the great one in the encircled mansion, says: this is my son, the king, whom I love. I have given him the Duat (the underworld) to be chief of it, like Horus. All the gods tell Nut that her father Shu knows she loves the king more than her mother Tefnut.",
  "egy.pt.8": "A short blessing: the king of Upper and Lower Egypt lives, beloved of Ra, living for ever.",
  "egy.pt.9": "A list of the king's titles: Horus, beloved of the Two Lands; king of Upper and Lower Egypt; beloved of the two goddesses; lord of Ombos; heir of Geb whom Geb loves; beloved of all the gods. He is given life, endurance, joy, health and every happiness, like Ra.",
  "egy.pt.10": "A list of the king's titles: Horus lives, the living appearance of the king of Upper and Lower Egypt; of the two goddesses; of the two lords of Ombos; Osiris, lord of the Duat; the beloved son of Geb; the son of Nut who opened her womb. He is given life, endurance, joy and health like Ra, for ever.",
  "egy.pt.11": "Nut says: I join your beauty to this body and to this ba (soul), for the life, endurance, joy and health of the king, whose royal titles are then listed. He lives for ever.",
  "egy.pt.12": "Nothing of this spell survives; Mercer's text is only a gap.",
  "egy.pt.13": "The speaker says: I give you your head and fasten it to your bones.",
  "egy.pt.14": "The speaker says: I give him his eyes so that he may be content. A note follows: an offering of food and drink.",
  "egy.pt.15": "The speaker says: Geb has given you your eyes so that you may be content. The rest is broken.",
  "egy.pt.16": "Only the end of this spell survives: \"…eye of Horus.\" A note says to offer one jar of water.",
  "egy.pt.17": "The speaker says that Thoth has given him his head. A note says to offer a pitcher of water.",
  "egy.pt.18": "The speaker says that he has had it brought to him. A note says to offer one drink of water.",
  "egy.pt.19": "Nothing of this spell survives; Mercer's text is only a gap.",
  "egy.pt.20": "Horus says to the king: I have come looking for you. I am your beloved son, and I have pressed and opened your mouth, fitting it back to your bones. Part of the text is restored here: he is the one who defends his weeping mother. Said four times: Osiris the king, I open your mouth with the thigh, which is the eye of Horus. A note says to offer one thigh of meat.",
  "egy.pt.21": "The speaker says to the king: how good your mouth is, now that I have fitted it to your bones. I open your mouth and your eyes with the copper (or iron) hook that opens the mouths of the gods. Horus opens the king's mouth with the same tool he used to open the mouth of his father Osiris, the metal that came from Set. So the king can walk, speak for himself before the Great Ennead in the prince's house at Heliopolis, and take the crown that belongs to Horus, lord of people.",
  "egy.pt.22": "The speaker says: Osiris the king, I bring you your ba (soul), whom you love. I open your mouth.",
  "egy.pt.23": "The speaker asks Osiris to carry off everyone who hates the king or speaks badly of his name. He tells Thoth to go and seize whoever harmed Osiris and whoever speaks badly of the king, to hold him and never let him go. Said four times; a note says to pour a libation.",
  "egy.pt.24": "The speaker tells Thoth to hurry and carry off the king's enemy. The rest is broken; what survives mentions the king and Osiris.",
  "egy.pt.25": "Whoever goes, goes with his ka (spirit double): Horus, Set, Thoth, the god, Osiris and Mekhenti-irti all go with their kas, and so does the king. The hands and feet of the king's ka are in front of him and behind him. The speaker gives Osiris the king the eye of Horus to adorn his face and lets its scent cling to him. Said four times; a note says to burn incense.",
  "egy.pt.26": "The speaker tells Horus, who is in Osiris the king, to take the eye of Horus, because he is like that eye, whose scent spreads far.",
  "egy.pt.27": "The speaker tells Osiris the king to take the eye of Horus and to adorn himself with its scent.",
  "egy.pt.28": "The speaker tells Osiris the king that Horus has given him his eye to adorn his face. A note says to offer one pellet of incense.",
  "egy.pt.29": "The speaker tells the king: I have come and brought you the eye of Horus to adorn your face, purify you and bring its scent to you. Its scent drives away your sweat and protects you from the force of Set's arm. The eye of Horus pleases you and is whole, and so are you. A note says to offer three pellets of incense.",
  "egy.pt.30": "The speaker tells Horus, who dwells in Osiris the king, to adorn himself with the eye of Horus and take it.",
  "egy.pt.31": "The speaker tells Osiris the king that Horus has filled him completely with his eye.",
  "egy.pt.32": "The speaker tells Osiris the king: this is your cool water, which came from your son Horus. I have brought you the eye of Horus to refresh your heart; it is under your feet. Take the fluid that comes out of you, and your heart will not grow weary. Said four times as the king goes out vindicated; a note says to pour water and offer two pellets of natron.",
  "egy.pt.33": "The speaker tells Osiris the king to take the libation Horus offers him, in his name \"He who came from the Cataract,\" and the fluid that comes from him. Horus has had the speaker gather the gods for him from everywhere he goes, and count the children of Horus as far as the place where he drowned. He tells the king to take his natron and become divine; Nut has made him a god, and Herenpwi knows him, for he is made young in his name \"Fresh water.\"",
  "egy.pt.34": "A spell for natron (a salt used for cleansing), named here \"smin\": it opens the king's mouth, and he will taste it before the chapels. What Horus spits out, what Set spits out, and what the two reconciled gods spit out is smin. Said four times: you have purified yourself with natron, together with Horus and his Followers. Notes give the pellets of natron to offer, ending with five from Nekheb in Upper Egypt.",
  "egy.pt.35": "The speaker tells the king that as he purifies himself, so do Horus, Set, Thoth and the god, and he is one of them. His mouth is like the mouth of a newborn calf. Notes give a pellet of natron for each line and five pellets from the Wadi Natrun in the north.",
  "egy.pt.36": "The speaker tells the king that as he purifies himself, so do Horus, Set, Thoth, the god, his ka and his god; he is among his brothers the gods. Natron is on his mouth and he cleanses all his bones; he should take what is his. The speaker gives Osiris the eye of Horus to adorn his face. A note says to offer one pellet of natron.",
  "egy.pt.37": "The speaker tells the king to close his two jaws, which had been parted. A note names a ritual instrument (or bread).",
  "egy.pt.38": "The speaker tells Osiris the king: I open your mouth. A note names a sacred copper (or iron) tool of the South and North.",
  "egy.pt.39": "The speaker tells the king to take the eye of Horus, which Horus runs to fetch and brings to him, and to put it in his mouth. A note says to offer three pellets of southern incense and three of northern incense.",
  "egy.pt.40": "The speaker tells the king to take the \"shik\" of Osiris. A note says to offer shik-pellets.",
  "egy.pt.41": "The speaker tells the king to take the tip of Horus's breast into his mouth. A note says to offer a jug of milk.",
  "egy.pt.42": "The speaker tells the king to take the breast of his sister Isis into his mouth. A note says to offer an empty jar.",
  "egy.pt.43": "The speaker tells the king to take the two eyes of Horus, the black and the white, and put them in his face so they light it up. The notes list a white jar and a black jar, which stand for his right and left eyes.",
  "egy.pt.44": "The speaker wishes that the sun in the sky, the two lords, the night and the two ladies may all be kind to the king. The offering brought to him is one he can see and hear; there is an offering in front of him, behind him and with him. A note names a cake.",
  "egy.pt.45": "The speaker tells Osiris the king to take the white teeth of Horus, which furnish his mouth. A note says to offer five white cakes.",
  "egy.pt.46": "Said four times: a royal offering to the king's ka. The speaker tells Osiris the king to take the eye of Horus, which is his cake, and eat. A note names an offering cake.",
  "egy.pt.47": "The speaker tells Osiris the king to take the eye of Horus, which has been freed from Set, put it to his mouth and open his mouth with it. A note says to offer wine in a white stone jar.",
  "egy.pt.48": "The speaker tells Osiris the king to open his mouth with what fills him. A note says to offer wine in a black stone jar.",
  "egy.pt.49": "The speaker tells Osiris the king to take the liquid that comes from him. A note says to offer beer in a black stone bowl.",
  "egy.pt.50": "The speaker says the king worships Ra, the one in heaven, and that all things belong to the king, to his body and to his ka. A note says to raise a fine offering table before him.",
  "egy.pt.51": "The speaker tells the king to take the eye of Horus and taste it. A note says to offer one loaf.",
  "egy.pt.52": "A short, unclear line: \"Darkness increases (?)\". A note says to offer one cake.",
  "egy.pt.53": "The speaker tells the king to take the eye of Horus and embrace it. A note says to offer one joint of meat.",
  "egy.pt.54": "The speaker tells the king to take the eye of Horus, which was wrested back from Set after being taken from him, and to open his mouth with it. A note says to offer a white stone bowl of wine.",
  "egy.pt.55": "The speaker tells the king to take the liquid that came out of Osiris. A note says to offer a black stone bowl of beer.",
  "egy.pt.56": "The speaker tells the king to take the eye of Horus, which had been taken from him; it is not far from him. A note says to offer a copper (or iron) bowl of beer.",
  "egy.pt.57": "The speaker tells the king to take the eye of Horus and equip himself with it, with a bowl of beer. A long run of short lines follows, each matched with an object (bows, a bowstring, whips, tails, a block, a dagger): \"I bring two eyes of Horus\" from where they fell; take what I give you; he threw them to the ground. Several lines are badly broken, with mentions of Set and his heart. The rest repeatedly tell Osiris the king to take and protect the eye of Horus, which was returned to him, which Horus took back from Set who had stolen it, which Geb guards, and which Set enjoyed and saw beside him.",
  "egy.pt.58": "The speaker tells Osiris the king to take the eye of Horus and the ointment Horus put in it. A note names a trimmed garment.",
  "egy.pt.59": "The speaker tells Osiris the king to take the eye of Horus and be wise like it, and to take the eye that Horus took back from Set, who had stolen it. The notes name a garment and a tail.",
  "egy.pt.60": "The speaker tells Osiris the king: I have given him to you; take him and wrap his heart to you. A note names a royal garment of fine cloth.",
  "egy.pt.61": "The speaker tells Osiris the king to take the thigh of Set that Horus tore off. A note names a royal garment of fine cloth.",
  "egy.pt.62": "The speaker tells Osiris the king to take the water in the eye of Horus and not to part from it, and to take the eye of Horus in which Thoth saw its water. The notes name a club, a trimmed garment, another club and a sword.",
  "egy.pt.63": "The speaker tells Osiris the king to present himself to his son Horus and take him into himself; this line is partly restored. Clubs are named, and a line is lost. An added line tells Osiris the king to make himself great over him.",
  "egy.pt.64": "The speaker tells the king to take it. Another line tells Osiris the king that the one he loves is Horus. Notes name two clubs.",
  "egy.pt.66": "The speaker tells Osiris the king to take the eye of Horus. A note names a wooden club.",
  "egy.pt.67": "The speaker tells Osiris the king to put something on himself and in his hand; several words here are untranslated. A note names a club.",
  "egy.pt.68": "The speaker tells Osiris the king to take the water in the eye of Horus, to fill his hand with a club and arm himself with it. It equips him like a god; he should not part from it, so that it may protect him. A note names one club.",
  "egy.pt.69": "The speaker tells Osiris the king to take the finger of Set, which makes the white eye of Horus see. A note names a club.",
  "egy.pt.70": "The speaker tells Osiris the king to take the eye of Horus so that it may shine on the finger of Set. A note names a sceptre.",
  "egy.pt.71": "The speaker tells Osiris the king to take his enemy's hand, to hold a sceptre, and says he is mighty against the enemy's two fingers and that he lives. He is told to take the eye of Horus that Horus's sons protect, and to slay something. Thoth is told to bring it; the speaker says he has given it to the king, who belongs to him. Geb tells Thoth to bring the bow. The last line is broken. Each line is matched with a sceptre, whip or weapon.",
  "egy.pt.72": "A heading says to pour oil. The speaker tells Osiris the king: I fill your eye with ointment. Said four times; a note names a festival oil.",
  "egy.pt.73": "The speaker tells Osiris the king to take what liquid offerings are made with. A note names an oil.",
  "egy.pt.74": "The speaker tells Osiris the king to take the eye of Horus, for which Horus suffered. A note names an oil.",
  "egy.pt.75": "The speaker tells Osiris the king to take the eye of Horus, which is united with him. A note names an oil.",
  "egy.pt.76": "The speaker tells Osiris the king to take the eye of Horus, with which Horus brought and carried the gods. A note names an oil.",
  "egy.pt.77": "The speaker calls on the oil on Horus's brow to rise and open, and to put itself on the king's brow. It should make him sweet-smelling and glorious, give him power over himself, and make every spirit who sees him or hears his name afraid of him. A note names a fine cedar oil.",
  "egy.pt.78": "The speaker tells Osiris the king: I bring you the eye of Horus, which Horus took from your brow. A note names a Libyan oil.",
  "egy.pt.79": "A note says to bring an offering before his face. Said four times: Osiris the king, join the sound eye of Horus to your face.",
  "egy.pt.80": "A note names two bags of green eye paint. The speaker tells Horus, who is in Osiris the king, to take the sound eye of Horus and join it to his face, as Horus did. He tells the king: I join your sound eyes to your face so that you may see with them.",
  "egy.pt.81": "The speaker tells the cloth to wake in peace, like the weaving goddess Tait and the eye of Horus in Buto. He asks it to make the Two Lands bow to the king as they bow to Horus and fear him as they fear Set. It should sit before the king as his god and open his way among the spirits, so he can stand among them like Anubis, First of the Westerners. Said four times: forward to Osiris. A note says two rolls of linen.",
  "egy.pt.82": "The speaker says it is Thoth who comes with it, bringing the eye of Horus. A note says to set up an offering table and make a funeral offering.",
  "egy.pt.83": "The speaker says the eye of Horus is given to him and he is satisfied with it, and calls on him to come with the royal offering.",
  "egy.pt.84": "The speaker tells Osiris the king to take the eye of Horus, with which Horus was satisfied. A note says a royal offering, twice.",
  "egy.pt.85": "A note says to set the offering table on the ground. The speaker tells Osiris the king to take the eye of Horus and be satisfied with it. A note names two offerings of the broad hall.",
  "egy.pt.86": "The speaker says: make it come back to you, and sit in silence. A note says a royal funeral offering.",
  "egy.pt.87": "The speaker tells Osiris the king to take the eye of Horus and join it to his mouth. A note lists food: one loaf and one beer.",
  "egy.pt.88": "The speaker tells Osiris the king to take the eye of Horus and take care that it is not trampled. A note names one loaf of bread.",
  "egy.pt.89": "The speaker tells Osiris the king to take the eye of Horus, which frightens him. A note names one loaf.",
  "egy.pt.90": "The speaker tells Osiris the king to take the eye of Horus; Set has eaten only a little of it. A note names one mug of beer.",
  "egy.pt.91": "The speaker tells Osiris the king to take the eye of Horus, which they put out for him. A note names one mug of beer.",
  "egy.pt.92": "The speaker tells Osiris the king to take the eye of Horus and put it to his face. A note says to lift up one bread and one beer.",
  "egy.pt.93": "A note says to lift the offering before his face. The speaker tells Osiris the king, whose spirit hurries, to lift up his face, be strong and pre-eminent, and see what comes from him, and to share in it. He tells him to wash and open his mouth with the eye of Horus, and to call his ka, like Osiris, to protect him from the anger of the dead. He should receive his bread, the eye of Horus. A note says to set the offering on the ground before him.",
  "egy.pt.94": "A note says to give food: one loaf and one beer. The speaker tells Osiris the king to take the eye of Horus, which he can live on. A note names one cake.",
  "egy.pt.95": "Said four times: equip yourself with the liquid that comes out of you. A note names one mug of beer.",
  "egy.pt.96": "The speaker tells Osiris the king to take the eye of Horus, a joint of meat. A note names one joint of meat.",
  "egy.pt.97": "A note says this is placed in the left hand. The speaker tells Osiris the king that this eye of Horus is the one Horus demanded back from Set.",
  "egy.pt.98": "The speaker tells Osiris the king that Horus has put his eye in his hand.",
  "egy.pt.99": "The speaker tells Osiris the king: I give you the eye of Horus; give me your hand so I can hand it to you. A closing line says, perhaps, \"Go there.\"",
  "egy.pt.100": "A note says this is placed in his left hand. The speaker tells Osiris the king that he has fought a little, but the speaker has loved him and, it seems, avenged him. The rest is broken.",
  "egy.pt.101": "The speaker tells Osiris the king: I, your avenger, have come; seize the eye of Horus. The rest is broken.",
  "egy.pt.102": "The text is badly broken. What survives says: \"I am Horus, Osiris the king…\", \"Give me your hand…\" and \"Seize…\".",
  "egy.pt.103": "A note says this is placed in his left hand. The rest is broken; only \"Osiris the king… to you\" survives.",
  "egy.pt.104": "The text is broken; only \"Osiris the king… your left…\" survives.",
  "egy.pt.105": "A run of very broken lines. Scattered words survive: \"he who lives is living… life\", \"Father, Osiris the king…\", and a warning that he frightens you but you must not give way; fill yourself, Osiris the king. The speaker says he has come to avenge the king and gives him something, but most of the text is lost.",
  "egy.pt.106": "Horus tells the king: I am your son. I have come and brought you the two eyes of Horus; take them and join them to yourself. I have gathered and joined them for you, and they are whole. Horus has set them before the king so they may lead him to heaven and the Great God, and avenge him on all his enemies. I bring you the two eyes of Horus, which gladden his heart. Parts of this are restored from gaps.",
  "egy.pt.107": "The speaker says he gathers them for the king and tells him to take them. The rest is broken.",
  "egy.pt.108": "The speaker tells Osiris the king to join to himself the water in the eye. Said four times, with the offering lifted four times; a note names two cups of water.",
  "egy.pt.109": "The speaker tells Osiris the king to take the eye of Horus, which cleans his mouth. Said four times, with the offering lifted four times; a note names two pots of natron.",
  "egy.pt.110": "The speaker tells Osiris the king to take the eye of Horus and join it to his mouth. Said four times, with the offering lifted four times; a note names one loaf and one beer.",
  "egy.pt.111": "The speaker tells Osiris the king to take the eye of Horus, which Set trampled. Said four times, with the offering lifted four times; a note names one or two loaves.",
  "egy.pt.112": "The speaker tells Osiris the king to take the eye of Horus, which frightens Set. Said four times, with the offering lifted four times; a note names one loaf.",
  "egy.pt.113": "The speaker tells Osiris the king to take it upon himself. Said four times, with the offering lifted four times; a note names two loaves.",
  "egy.pt.114": "The speaker tells Osiris the king: I bring you what suits your face. Said four times, with the offering lifted four times; a note names two loaves.",
  "egy.pt.115": "The speaker tells Osiris the king: I set your eye in place. Said four times, with the offering lifted four times; a note names four loaves.",
  "egy.pt.116": "The speaker tells Osiris the king to take the eye of Horus and keep Horus from suffering because of it. Said four times, with the offering lifted four times; a note names four cakes.",
  "egy.pt.117": "The speaker tells Osiris the king to receive his head. Said four times, with the offering lifted four times; a note names four loaves.",
  "egy.pt.118": "The speaker tells Osiris the king to take his eye and hold on to it. Said four times, with the offering lifted four times; a note names four loaves.",
  "egy.pt.119": "The speaker tells Osiris the king to take the eye of Horus, which Horus swallowed. Said four times, with the offering lifted four times; a note names four baskets of cakes.",
  "egy.pt.120": "The speaker tells Osiris the king to take the eye of Horus and make it respected. Said four times, with the offering lifted four times; a note names four baskets.",
  "egy.pt.121": "The speaker tells Osiris the king to take the eye of Horus, which frightens Set. Said four times, with the offering lifted four times; a note names four loaves.",
  "egy.pt.122": "The speaker tells Osiris the king to take the eye of Horus and put it in his mouth. Said four times, with the offering lifted four times; a note names four northern cakes.",
  "egy.pt.123": "The speaker tells Osiris the king to take the eye of Horus; it is his cake, and he should eat. Said four times, with the offering lifted four times; a note names four cakes.",
  "egy.pt.124": "The speaker tells Osiris the king to take the eye of Horus, which frightens him. Said four times, with the offering lifted four times; a note names four baskets of bread.",
  "egy.pt.125": "The speaker tells Osiris the king to take his teeth, white and sound. Said four times, with the offering lifted four times; a note names four bunches of garlic.",
  "egy.pt.126": "The speaker tells Osiris the king to take the joint of meat, which is the eye of Horus. Said four times, with the offering lifted four times; a note names one joint of meat.",
  "egy.pt.127": "The speaker tells Osiris the king to dance for joy, because Geb is not angry with his rightful heir. Said four times, with the offering lifted four times; a note names one joint of meat.",
  "egy.pt.128": "The speaker tells Osiris the king to take the eye of Horus and embrace it. Said four times, with the offering lifted four times; a note names one joint of meat.",
  "egy.pt.129": "The speaker tells Osiris the king to take this cut of meat, the eye of Horus. Said four times, with the offering lifted four times; a note names one joint of meat.",
  "egy.pt.130": "The speaker tells Osiris the king to take those who rebel against him. Said four times, with the offering lifted four times; a note names four ribs of meat.",
  "egy.pt.131": "The speaker tells Osiris the king to take an untranslated item. Said four times, with the offering lifted four times; a note lists a pot of roast, a liver, a spleen, a limb and a breast of meat.",
  "egy.pt.132": "The speaker tells Osiris the king to take the eye of Horus and go to it. Said four times, with the offering lifted four times; a note names one liver.",
  "egy.pt.133": "The speaker tells Osiris the king to take the eye of Horus, to which Horus goes. Said four times, with the offering lifted four times; a note names one spleen.",
  "egy.pt.134": "The speaker tells Osiris the king to take the eye of Horus that is on his brow. Said four times, with the offering lifted four times; a note names one limb.",
  "egy.pt.135": "The speaker tells Osiris the king to take the eye of Horus that is on the brow of Set. Said four times, with the offering lifted four times; a note names one breast of meat.",
  "egy.pt.136": "The speaker tells Osiris the king to take the cut-off heads of the Followers of Set. Said four times, with the offering lifted four times; a note names one goose.",
  "egy.pt.137": "The speaker tells Osiris the king to take as much as his heart desires. Said four times, with the offering lifted four times; a note names one goose.",
  "egy.pt.138": "The speaker tells Osiris the king to take the eye of Horus, which Horus brought. Said four times, with the offering lifted four times; a note names one goose.",
  "egy.pt.139": "The speaker tells Osiris the king to take those who come when they are faint (the meaning is uncertain). Said four times, with the offering lifted four times; a note names one goose.",
  "egy.pt.140": "The speaker tells Osiris the king to take the eye of Horus and keep Horus from sickening over it. Said four times, with the offering lifted four times; a note names one pigeon.",
  "egy.pt.141": "The speaker tells Osiris the king to take the eye of Horus, which frightens him. Said four times, with the offering lifted four times; a note names one bread.",
  "egy.pt.142": "The speaker tells Osiris the king to take the eye of Horus; it will not be parted from him. Said four times, with the offering lifted four times; a note names two loaves.",
  "egy.pt.143": "The speaker tells Osiris the king that the eye of Horus is assigned to him. Said four times, with the offering lifted four times; a note names two baskets of food.",
  "egy.pt.144": "The speaker tells Osiris the king to take the eye of Horus, from which Horus poured out the water. Said four times, with the offering lifted four times; a note names two pots of liver.",
  "egy.pt.145": "The speaker tells Osiris the king to take the eye of Horus; Set has eaten only a little of it. Said four times, with the offering lifted four times; a note names two bowls of beer.",
  "egy.pt.146": "The speaker tells Osiris the king to take the eye of Horus, through which certain beings (the word is untranslated) come. Said four times, with the offering lifted four times; a note names two bowls of Theban beer.",
  "egy.pt.147": "The speaker tells Osiris the king to take the eye of Horus, which they put out for him. Said four times, with the offering lifted four times; a note names two bowls of beer.",
  "egy.pt.148": "The speaker tells Osiris the king to equip himself with the liquid that comes out of him. Said four times, with the offering lifted four times; a note names two bowls of beer.",
  "egy.pt.149": "The speaker tells Osiris the king to equip himself with the liquid that comes out of him. Said four times, with the offering lifted four times; a note names two bowls of a drink.",
  "egy.pt.150": "The speaker tells Osiris the king to equip himself with the liquid that comes out of him. Said four times, with the offering lifted four times; a note names two bowls of a drink.",
  "egy.pt.151": "The speaker tells Osiris the king to equip himself with the liquid that comes out of him. Said four times, with the offering lifted four times; a note names two mugs of Nubian beer.",
  "egy.pt.152": "The speaker tells Osiris the king to take the breast of Horus, which they taste. Said four times, with the offering lifted four times; a note names two baskets of figs.",
  "egy.pt.153": "The speaker tells Osiris the king to open his mouth with it. Said four times, with the offering lifted four times; the notes list jars and bowls of wine from the North, Buto, Pelusium and other places.",
  "egy.pt.154": "The speaker tells Osiris the king to take the eye of Horus, which they spat out, and to keep Horus from swallowing it. Said four times, with the offering lifted four times; a note names two jars of wine.",
  "egy.pt.155": "The speaker tells Osiris the king to take the maiden who is in the eye of Horus and open his mouth with her. Said four times, with the offering lifted four times; a note names two jars of wine of Buto.",
  "egy.pt.156": "The speaker tells Osiris the king to take the eye of Horus, which Horus fished up, and open his mouth with it. Said four times, with the offering lifted four times; a note names two jars of wine.",
  "egy.pt.157": "The speaker tells Osiris the king to take the eye of Horus; it will never be separated from him again. Said four times, with the offering lifted four times; a note names two jars of wine of Pelusium.",
  "egy.pt.158": "The speaker tells Osiris the king to take the eye of Horus and honour it. Said four times, with the offering lifted four times; a note names two baskets of fruit.",
  "egy.pt.159": "The speaker tells Osiris the king to take the eye of Horus, which Horus carried about (or perhaps swallowed). Said four times, with the offering lifted four times; a note names two baskets of cakes.",
  "egy.pt.160": "The speaker tells Osiris the king to take the eye of Horus, which Horus took from Set. Said four times, with the offering lifted four times; a note names two baskets of fruit.",
  "egy.pt.161": "The speaker tells Osiris the king to take the white eye of Horus and keep him from tearing it out. Said four times, with the offering lifted four times; a note names two baskets of fruit.",
  "egy.pt.162": "The speaker tells Osiris the king to take the green eye of Horus and keep him from tearing it out. Said four times, with the offering lifted four times; a note names two baskets of fruit.",
  "egy.pt.163": "The speaker tells Osiris the king to take the eye of Horus and keep him from carrying it off. Said four times, with the offering lifted four times; a note names two baskets of grain.",
  "egy.pt.164": "The speaker tells Osiris the king to take the eye of Horus and keep him from carrying it off. Said four times, with the offering lifted four times; a note names two baskets of grain.",
  "egy.pt.165": "The speaker tells Osiris the king to take the eye of Horus, which is like a certain fruit (the word is untranslated). Said four times, with the offering lifted four times; a note names two baskets of seedy fruit.",
  "egy.pt.166": "The speaker tells Osiris the king to take the eye of Horus, which they swallowed. Said four times, with the offering lifted four times; a note names two baskets of fruit.",
  "egy.pt.167": "The speaker tells Osiris the king to open his eyes and see with them. Said four times, with the offering lifted four times; a note names two baskets of fruit.",
  "egy.pt.168": "The speaker tells Osiris the king to take the eye of Horus and keep him from seizing it. Said four times, with the offering lifted four times; a note names two baskets of fruit.",
  "egy.pt.169": "The speaker tells Osiris the king to take the sweet eye of Horus and make it stay with him. Said four times, with the offering lifted four times; a note names two baskets of every sweet thing.",
  "egy.pt.170": "The speaker tells Osiris the king to take the eye of Horus and claim it back for himself. Said four times, with the offering lifted four times; a note names two baskets of all fresh plants.",
  "egy.pt.171": "The speaker says to Osiris the king: may it please you, be for you and be with you. Said four times, with the offering lifted four times; a note names an offering.",
  "egy.pt.172": "Said four times: a meal offered to the king. The speaker says: may Geb make an offering to the king. I give you every festival offering and every gift of food and drink you could want, so that you may be happy with the god for ever.",
  "egy.pt.173": "The speaker tells Osiris the king that Horus has come to look after him, because he is Horus's father. A note names barley.",
  "egy.pt.174": "The speaker tells the king to go to Geb. A note names two pots of natron.",
  "egy.pt.175": "The speaker says Geb has given the king his two eyes so that he may be content. A note names one offering table.",
  "egy.pt.176": "The speaker tells Osiris the king that he is his ka. A note names one loaf.",
  "egy.pt.177": "The speaker tells the Great One, Osiris the king, to take the two eyes. A note names two loaves.",
  "egy.pt.178": "The speaker tells the king to be content with them. A note names two offering tables of the broad hall.",
  "egy.pt.179": "The speaker tells the king to be content with Horus, because he is Horus's father. A note names one offering of bread.",
  "egy.pt.180": "The speaker says Nekhbet takes the eye of Horus, and Horus has given it to the king. A note names two baskets of fruit.",
  "egy.pt.181": "The speaker says Nekhbet takes the eye of Horus, which they swallow, and Horus has given it to the king. A note names two baskets of fruit.",
  "egy.pt.182": "The speaker tells the king to take the eye of Horus, which Horus seized; Horus has given it to him. A note names one basket of fruit.",
  "egy.pt.183": "The speaker tells the king to take the liquid that comes out of Osiris. A note names two jugs of a drink.",
  "egy.pt.184": "The speaker tells Osiris the king to take the water that is in him; Horus has given it to him. The note that follows is partly broken.",
  "egy.pt.185": "The speaker tells the king to take the eye of Horus and open his mouth with it. A note names two bowls of northern wine.",
  "egy.pt.186": "The speaker tells Osiris the king to take the green eye of Horus, which Horus carried off; Horus has given it to him. A note names two dishes of fresh bread.",
  "egy.pt.187": "The speaker tells the king to take the eye of Horus and honour it; Horus has given it to him. A note names two baskets of fruit.",
  "egy.pt.188": "The speaker tells the king to take the eye of Horus, which Horus owns; Horus has given it to him. A note names two baskets of fruit cake.",
  "egy.pt.189": "The speaker tells the king to take the white eye of Horus, which Horus tore out; Horus has given it to him. A note names two baskets of white fruit.",
  "egy.pt.190": "The speaker tells the king to take the green eye of Horus, which Horus tore out; Horus has given it to him. A note names two baskets of green fruit.",
  "egy.pt.191": "The speaker tells the king to take the eye of Horus, which Horus recognised; Horus has given it to him. A note names two cakes.",
  "egy.pt.192": "The speaker tells the king to take the eye of Horus, which Horus snatched away in a hurry; Horus has given it to him. A note names two pots of grain.",
  "egy.pt.193": "The speaker tells Osiris the king to take the eye of Horus and feast on it. A note names two baskets of figs.",
  "egy.pt.194": "The speaker tells Osiris the king to make this sweet eye of Horus stay with him. A note names two baskets of every sweet fruit.",
  "egy.pt.195": "The speaker tells the king to claim it back for himself. A note names two baskets of all fresh fruit.",
  "egy.pt.196": "The speaker says: may it please you. A note names two libations.",
  "egy.pt.197": "The speaker tells Osiris the king to take this hard eye of Horus into himself, so that his enemy, the one who carried him in his time, may fear him.",
  "egy.pt.198": "The speaker tells Osiris the king that Horus has filled him completely with his eye, as an offering.",
  "egy.pt.199": "The speaker tells Osiris the king to turn to his bread and take it from the speaker's hand. Said four times: may the eye of Horus flourish for you. A note names a god-given offering of bread, cake and drink.",
  "egy.pt.200": "The speaker greets incense as a divine brother in the limbs of Horus, and calls on it, as his father, to grow great and multiply in its name of \"pellet.\" Its scent and perfume are for the king. He tells the eye of Horus that it is higher and greater than the king. A note says incense.",
  "egy.pt.201": "The speaker tells Father the king to take the eye of Horus, the bread the gods feed on.",
  "egy.pt.202": "The speaker tells Father the king to take the liquid that comes from Osiris.",
  "egy.pt.203": "The speaker tells Osiris the king to take it; the eye of Horus belongs to him.",
  "egy.pt.204": "The speaker calls on the hoers (people who work with hoes) to rejoice and on people's hearts to lift, because they have swallowed the bright eye of Horus in Heliopolis. The king's little finger draws out what is in Osiris's navel. The king is not thirsty or hungry and his heart does not faint, for it is in the hands of Ha, who keeps his hunger away. He calls on the fillers of hearts to fill him.",
  "egy.pt.205": "The speaker asks the gods in charge of food and plenty to commend the king to Fetekta, Ra's cup-bearer, so that he may commend him to Ra, and Ra to the chiefs of this year's provisions, who will give him barley, spelt, bread and beer. It is his father Ra who feeds him, for he is the great bull who struck Kenset. Five portions of food are his, three in heaven with Ra and two on earth with the Ennead. He is set free and seen; he is better today than yesterday. The king says he has slept with several goddesses, and that his beloved, though without grain, gives him bread and treated him well that day.",
  "egy.pt.206": "The speaker asks the gods in charge of food and plenty to commend the king to Fetekta, Ra's cup-bearer, so that he may commend him to Ra, and Ra to the chiefs of provisions. Whatever Ra bites or nibbles he gives to the king, so that the king may sleep and be well every day.",
  "egy.pt.207": "A list of offerings: the butcher's offering, the offering of what is in Ra's eye, the bird-catcher's offering, and the cup-bearer who brings water. Let the fire be hot and the meat go with the pastry, with four handfuls of water.",
  "egy.pt.208": "A variant of the previous spell: an offering to Atum, an offering of what is in the eye of the god's boat. Let the meat go with the pastry, with four handfuls of water.",
  "egy.pt.209": "The speaker says Shu is flourishing while the king has not taken his meal, and the king is flourishing while Shu has not taken his. He asks the eastern messengers to double the king's bread.",
  "egy.pt.210": "The judge and Thoth are awake, and the sleepers and those in Kenset stir before the great heron coming from the marsh and Wepwawet coming from the tamarisk. The king's mouth and tongue are pure; the Two Enneads purify him. He loathes dung and rejects urine and will not eat them, just as Set shrinks from the two companions who sail the sky. He asks Ra and Thoth to take him with them, so he may eat, drink, live, sit, be strong and sail as they do. His shelter is a reed hut, his plenty is in the Field of Offerings, his water is wine like Ra's, and he travels round the sky like Ra and Thoth.",
  "egy.pt.211": "The king hates hunger and thirst and will not take them in; he is the one who feeds those who exist. His nurse is Iat, who keeps him alive and gave birth to him. He was conceived and born at night and belongs to the Followers of Ra before the morning star. He was conceived and born in Nun (the primeval waters), and he has come bringing some of the bread he found there.",
  "egy.pt.212": "The eye of Horus drips onto a tuft of a plant. Khenti-imentiu came and brought him food, an offering of Horus, chief of the houses, for he lives on, eats and drinks what the king does. A joint of meat and pastry is his meal.",
  "egy.pt.213": "The speaker tells the king that he did not go away dead but alive. So he sits on the throne of Osiris, sceptres in hand, ruling the living and those in hidden places. His arms, shoulders, body, back, seat and legs are like Atum's and his face is like Anubis's. He travels through the regions of Horus and of Set (or, they serve him).",
  "egy.pt.214": "The king is warned four times to beware of the sea. Messengers have come for him from his ka, his father and Ra. He should follow his sun and purify himself, for his bones are falcon goddesses in the sky; he will be beside the god and leave his house to his son and heir. Geb counts anyone who speaks ill of the king as an evil-doer, and that person grows weak. The king washes in the dew of the stars and comes down on firm cables, on the shoulders of Horus. The blessed mourn him after the undying stars carried him off. He is told to enter the house of his father Geb, who will give him what is on Horus's brow, so that he becomes a ba, a power, and Khenti-imentiu.",
  "egy.pt.215": "The speaker tells the king to send his messengers to his father Atum, and asks Atum to let him rise and hold him, since every god who becomes a star has a companion. Those who knew their fathers' speech are now undying stars. He will see Horus and Set in the palace, spit in Horus's face to heal his injury and take hold of Set's testicles to undo his mutilation; both were born of him. Horus, born of Osiris, and Set, conceived by Geb, are named and healed, and each is more powerful than his father. No seed of a god perishes, and Ra-Atum hands the king over neither to Osiris nor to Horus to judge his heart. The twin children of Atum told him to rise as a god, and he becomes an Atum to every god. Each part of his body is then given to a god: head Horus, face Mekhenti-irti, nose Anubis, teeth Sopdu, arms and legs the four sons of Horus, so he can go up to the sky and down below. He does not perish, nor does his ka, for he is a ka.",
  "egy.pt.216": "The speaker says he has come to Nephthys, to the evening boat, and to other named powers, and asks them to remember the king. As Orion is wrapped in the Duat, pure and living in the horizon, so is the king. He is content and cool in the arms of his father Atum.",
  "egy.pt.217": "The speaker tells Ra-Atum that his son the king is coming to him, an undying spirit, so that they may cross the sky together in darkness and rise in the horizon. Set and Nephthys are told to tell the gods of Upper Egypt, Osiris and Isis the gods of Lower Egypt, Thoth the gods of the West, and Horus the Souls of the East, that the king is coming as an undying spirit. Whoever he wants to live will live, and whoever he wants to die will die. He comes like the morning star and like Anubis, to count and rule hearts. Ra-Atum is asked to let him rise and to hold him, for he is his own son for ever.",
  "egy.pt.218": "The speaker tells Osiris that the king is coming, angry (it seems) with the Nine Bows, an undying spirit, to count hearts and take and subdue kas. Anyone who holds back from him will have no bread. Geb and the Ennead call him a falcon, a ba and a power. The king surpasses Osiris and is greater, fresher and more praised; Osiris's silence is over. Set and Thoth, his brothers, did not know how to weep for him. Isis and Nephthys are told to embrace and unite. Again and again the king comes as an undying spirit, and the Westerners, Easterners, Southerners, Northerners and those in the underworld all belong to him.",
  "egy.pt.219": "The speaker names Osiris to each god in turn: Atum, Shu, Tefnut, Geb, Nut, Isis, Set, Nephthys, Thoth, Horus and the Great and Little Enneads. This is Osiris, whom you made endure and live. As he lives, the king lives; as he does not die, perish or get judged, neither does the king; as he judges, so does the king. Set and Thoth are told he lives to punish them. The same refrain follows a list of Osiris's names and places: Heliopolis, Busiris, the Divine Hall, Orion, Buto, Hermopolis and others, with lines about provisions as his daughter and the eye he has eaten. Finally the speaker tells Osiris that his body, flesh and bones are the king's, and where one goes the other goes.",
  "egy.pt.220": "The two doors of the horizon open. The king has come to the crown of Lower Egypt and to the uraeus (royal cobra), Great One and Great-in-magic, pure and in awe of her. The speaker hopes she will be pleased with him, his purity and his words: \"How beautiful your face is, peaceful, new and young, for a god, father of the gods, fathered you!\" It is Horus who has fought to protect his eye.",
  "egy.pt.221": "The speaker calls on the crown of Lower Egypt and the uraeus, under several names, to make the king feared, respected and loved as she is, to put his sceptres at the head of the living and the spirits, and to make his blade firm against his enemies. He says she came from him as he came from her; two goddesses gave birth to her and adorned her, for she is like Horus who fought to protect his eye.",
  "egy.pt.222": "The speaker tells the king to stand on the earth that came from Atum, the spittle of Khepri, and rise high above it to see his father Ra. The king has come to his father under many names: Ra, Ndi, Pndn, Dndn, Sema-wer, Sekhen-wer, Sopdu, Sharp of Teeth. He is asked to let the king seize the sky and horizon, rule the Nine Bows, equip the Ennead and hold the shepherd's crook so the Two Lands bow. Nephthys praises him as equipped like Set of Ombos, more powerful than the southern gods, and Isis praises him as equipped like the young Horus, more powerful than the northern gods. He puts away his impurity for Atum in Heliopolis and rises with him. He rises and sets with Ra and with the night and day boats, purifies himself in the horizon, and is master of himself with no one in his way. Atum is asked to let him rise and to hold him, for he is his son for ever.",
  "egy.pt.223": "The speaker tells the king to wake, turn round, get up and sit before a thousand loaves and a thousand beers, with roast meat and ribs from the slaughtering bench and bread from the hall. As a god is given offerings, so is the king given his bread. He has come to his ba, Osiris, mighty and protected by the Ennead. The speaker asks him to come close, not to go far from the tomb or from him, gives him the eye of Horus, and tells him to take his bread from his hand. Said four times: \"I will be your door-keeper.\" A closing note: an offering to him in all his ranks and places, given by Geb.",
  "egy.pt.224": "The speaker tells the king to wake and turn round: he has come to rule the regions of Horus, Set and Osiris. A royal offering is made \"in all your ranks.\" He wears loincloths and sandals, slaughters an ox and travels in a boat, in all his ranks and places. His sceptre is at the head of the living and his staff at the head of the spirits, like Anubis, First of the Westerners, and Andjeti, First of the Eastern nomes. How fortunate he is, a spirit among his brothers the gods; how changed his state! He is told to protect his children and to beware of his limit in the earth. Said four times: clothe your body and come before them.",
  "egy.pt.225": "A variant of the previous spell. The speaker tells the king to wake and turn round: he has come to rule the regions of Horus and Set and to speak in the regions of Osiris. A royal offering: his son is on his throne. He wears loincloths and sandals, slaughters an ox, travels in a boat, and his sceptre and staff lead the living and the spirits. The old one has come and protects his son. The speaker asks that his body be clothed so he can come to him, and that the eye of Horus please him.",
  "egy.pt.226": "This spell says one snake is encircled by another, as a young calf born on the pasture is encircled. It tells the earth to swallow what came out of it, and tells the beast to lie down and slither away. A servant of the Ennead has fallen into the water. The snake is told to turn over so that Ra may see it.",
  "egy.pt.227": "The spell says the head of the great black bull was cut off. It tells a serpent and a scorpion to turn over and slither into the ground: \"I have said this to you.\"",
  "egy.pt.228": "This spell says face falls on face and face sees face. A black and green knife goes out against the creature until it has swallowed what it licked.",
  "egy.pt.229": "This spell says that this is the fingernail of Atum, pressed on the backbone of the serpent Nehebkau, which ended the strife in Wenu. It tells the snake to fall and slither away.",
  "egy.pt.230": "This spell tells the snake to put its poison glands in the ground and its ribs in the hole; two kites stand by, and its mouth is shut. The king tells Ra that he has bitten the earth, Geb, and the father of the one who bit him; the creature bit him and came against him, though he did not attack it. If it bites the king he will make it one piece; if it looks at him he will make it two. Male and female serpents bite each other. Heaven, earth, mankind, the blind-headed god and the scorpion itself are protected by magic. These are the two knots of Elephantine in the mouth of Osiris, which Horus tied over the backbone.",
  "egy.pt.231": "The spell tells the snake that its bone is a harpoon point it will be speared with. Hearts are stopped, and the nomads are at the spear and are thrown down. That is the god Hemen.",
  "egy.pt.232": "This spell is mostly repeated sounds (\"Mti, Mti… Tiw, his mother\"). It ends by asking that the desert be watered: let there be water, not sand.",
  "egy.pt.233": "This spell says the snake that came out of the earth has fallen, and the flame that came out of Nun (the primeval waters) has fallen. It tells it to fall and slither away.",
  "egy.pt.234": "This spell tells the snake on its belly, in its bush, that a face is against it, and to go down on its backbone. It is to give way before the two-headed serpent.",
  "egy.pt.235": "This spell begins with untranslated names (Kwtiw, Imhw). It accuses the creature of having raped the two keepers of the stone door-post of a place with an untranslated name.",
  "egy.pt.236": "This spell names the snake with a long, untranslated name, calling it son of Hifget: \"that is your name.\"",
  "egy.pt.237": "This spell speaks of spittle that has not dried up or flowed away into the house of its mother, and tells the serpent to lie down.",
  "egy.pt.238": "This spell tells someone named Iki-nhii that his father's bread is his, and his own bread belongs to his father and to him. It then lists jewellery and oil and names an ox, the renowned one, for whose deed this is being done. The meaning is obscure.",
  "egy.pt.239": "This spell says the white crown has gone out and swallowed the Great One; its tongue swallowed him, yet the tongue was not seen.",
  "egy.pt.240": "This spell says the cobra belongs to the sky and the centipede of Horus to the earth. Horus was a herdsman when he trod on things; the king treads on the path of Horus, though he does not know the unknown one. The creature in its bush and hole is told to lie on its back; Horus's meat-cook is told to escape into the earth, and the beast is told to slither away.",
  "egy.pt.241": "This spell names \"spitting of the wall\" and \"vomiting of the brick,\" and says that what comes out of the creature's mouth is thrown back on itself.",
  "egy.pt.242": "This spell says the flame is out: the flame-snake is not found in the house of the one who owns Ombos. It is a biting snake that has slipped back into the house of the one it will bite, to stay there.",
  "egy.pt.243": "An obscure spell that mentions pairs of sceptres and cords, \"like bread that is held back from you.\" It ends by asking the creature whether it is really here or there, and tells the slave to go away.",
  "egy.pt.244": "The speaker tells Osiris the king that this is the hard eye of Horus; he should take it so that he is strong and Set fears him. A note says to break two red jars.",
  "egy.pt.245": "The speaker tells Nut that the king is coming to her. He has thrown his father to the ground and left Horus behind him. His wings and feathers have grown like a falcon's; his ba has brought him and his magic has equipped him. Nut opens his place in the sky among the stars; he is the lone star, companion of Hu. He looks down on Osiris as he rules the spirits, standing far from him; he is not one of them and never will be.",
  "egy.pt.246": "The speaker tells the gods to see the king standing among them with two horns like wild bulls, for he is the black ram, son of a black sheep, born of a white sheep and nursed by four ewes. They should beware of the furious red-eyed Horus, whose might no one can withstand. His messengers announce in the East that this is the one who, as Dwen-anwi said, will command the gods; the gods fall silent with hands over their mouths. The king is told to stand at the horizon's doorway, open the sky's double doors and stand at the gods' head like Geb, while they come and go in fear. They see him like Min. His brother and kin stand behind him. He does not perish, and his name lives among people and gods.",
  "egy.pt.247": "The speaker tells the king that his son Horus has done this for him. The great ones tremble at the sword in his hand as he comes out of the Duat. He is greeted as the wise one, made by Geb and fathered by the Ennead; Horus is pleased with his father, and the gods of East and West are pleased with what happened in the arms of Nut. The king, who has seen, heard and been there, is told to get up from his side, he who hates sleep and is weary. His fine bread is made in Buto, and he takes his power in Heliopolis. Horus ordered this for his father; the lord of storm held back Set's spittle when Set was to carry him.",
  "egy.pt.248": "The speaker says the king is great; he came out from between the thighs of the Ennead. Sekhmet conceived him and Shesmetet bore him as a sharp-eyed, long-striding star who brings Ra supplies for his journey every day. The king has come to his throne above the two protective goddesses and shines as a star.",
  "egy.pt.249": "The speaker asks the two fighters to announce to the honoured one that the king is a plant springing from the earth. His hand is cleansed by the one who prepared his throne. He is at the nose of the Great One, and has come from the Isle of Flame after putting truth there in place of falsehood. He guards the linen and protects the cobras on the night of the great flood. The king appears as Nefertem, the lotus flower at Ra's nose; as he rises from the horizon each day, the gods purify themselves when they see him.",
  "egy.pt.250": "The Great One, chief of the wise, says that the king is chief of the kas, who unites hearts, holds the divine book, knows, and stands at Ra's right hand. The king comes to his throne and comes into being as this one. He says what is in the heart of the Great One at the Feast of Red Cloths, and so the chief of the abyss of Nun is troubled.",
  "egy.pt.251": "The speaker asks the gods in charge of the hours, who go before Ra, to prepare the king's way so he can pass the fierce border guards. The king goes to his throne with bowed head behind the god, armed with a sharp, strong antelope horn like a throat-cutting knife; it drives suffering away and punishes those in darkness. The king has punished them and crushed their heads, and no one will resist his arm in the horizon.",
  "egy.pt.252": "The speaker tells the gods in the Duat to lift their heads, for the king has come and is becoming a great god. They are to bring him in with trembling and adorn him. He judges those who live in Ra's land and speaks to the pure land where he lives. He is mighty, holds a sceptre, sits with Ra's rowers, and commands the good, which is then done, for he is the Great God.",
  "egy.pt.253": "The speaker says that whoever was purified in the Field of Reeds is pure: Ra was purified there, and so is the king. The king's hand is in Ra's hand, Nut takes his arm, and Shu lifts him up.",
  "egy.pt.254": "The great cobra burns incense to the bull of Nekhen, and her fiery breath is turned on those around the shrine. The king tells the lord of the horizon to make room for him, or he will curse his father Geb so that the earth falls silent. He eats bit by bit whoever he meets on his way. The pelicans and the Enneads announce that the earth will be dammed, the riverbanks joined, roads and stairways closed. The stars tremble before the bull of heaven. The Beautiful West comes to meet him with lovely hair, saying she bore him and has protected him, and sends him to the Field of Offerings. His body decays in the earth, but he sees Ra in his bonds and freed from them. He passes the head-cutting she-monkeys in peace, for he has put his head back on his neck. The cobra goddess widens his place in Busiris, Mendes and Heliopolis and sets up his fields. His strength is the strength of Ra's eye. He frees himself from those who took his food, breath and life: their hearts fall into his fingers, their entrails go to the birds, their blood to the beasts, their houses to fire and their farms to the flood. He rejoices as the unique bull of heaven, and his throne is what Shu gave him before Set.",
  "egy.pt.255": "The horizon burns incense to Horus of Nekhen, and its fiery, poisonous breath is turned on those around the shrine and those who wear the crown. The speaker tells \"the ugly one\" to leave his place and lay his rank on the ground for the king. If he does not, the king will come like the Great One, send heat from his eye around them, a storm on wrongdoers and a flood over the ancients, strike away Shu's arms from under Nut, and put his arm on the wall that protects them. The Great One stands up in his shrine and lays down his rank for the king, since the king has taken command (Hu) and knowledge (Sia).",
  "egy.pt.256": "The speaker says the king has inherited Geb and Atum and sits on the throne of Horus the eldest. His eye is his strength, and what was done to him is his protection. The fiery breath of his cobra is like that on his brow; he has put fear in their hearts by slaughtering them. The gods saw it and bowed before him, saying: his mother leads him, his home town tows him; let go your rope.",
  "egy.pt.257": "There is an uproar in the sky; the first gods say they see something new: a Horus in the sun's rays. The lords of forms and the Two Enneads serve him as he sits in the All-lord's place. He takes the sky and splits its firmness, and is led on the paths of Khepri. He rests from life in the West, followed by those in the Duat, and rises renewed in the East; the one who judged the quarrel bows to him. The gods are told to serve him as one older than the Great One. He takes command (Hu), eternity is brought to him and knowledge (Sia) is at his feet. They are to shout for joy: he has won the horizon.",
  "egy.pt.258": "The speaker says the king is Osiris in a dust storm. He hates the earth and has not entered Geb to be destroyed, nor slept in his earthly house to have his bones broken. His wounds are wiped away; he has purified himself with the eye of Horus and the two mourners of Osiris, and let the fluid of his wound run onto the ground at Qus. His sister, the lady of Pe, wept for him. He goes to the sky on the wind and no one stops him; he is on his own, the eldest of the gods. His bread comes from on high with Ra's, and his offering from Nun. He comes and goes with Ra and visits his houses. He takes and frees kas, covers and ends evil, spends day and night calming the two butchers in Wenu, and nothing stops his feet or holds back his heart.",
  "egy.pt.259": "A near repeat of the previous spell. The king is Osiris in a dust storm; he hates the earth, has not entered Geb to perish, and his bones are not broken. His wounds are wiped away with the eye of Horus and by the two mourners of Osiris, and his sister, the lady of Pe, and the two nurses wept for him. He goes to the sky with Shu and Ra, unhindered, on his feet, the eldest of the gods, with no seat in the divine court. His bread and offering come from on high and from Nun; he comes and goes with Ra. He covers and ends evil, takes and frees kas, frees the two butchers in Wenu, and nothing stops his feet or his heart.",
  "egy.pt.260": "The speaker tells Geb, bull of Nut, that the king is a Horus, his father's heir, the fourth of four gods who brought water, washed and rejoice in their fathers' power. The king wants to be vindicated for what he has done. Since Tefen and Tefnut have judged him, the Two Truths have heard him and given their verdict, and Shu has spoken for him, he has gone round Geb's thrones and risen to what he wished. His hidden limbs are joined, and he goes out as a true spirit, ending strife and punishing revolt, bringing Truth with him. His refuge, protection, power and strength are in his eye. The gods of every direction are told to respect and fear him, and to come to him as friends, not foes. He hates walking in darkness, where he would see those who are upside down. He will come out today with Truth and will not be handed over to the gods' flame.",
  "egy.pt.261": "The speaker says the king is a heartbeat, son of Shu's heart, wide-spreading and dazzling. He is a flame moving before the wind to the ends of the sky and earth. He travels through Shu and strides through Aker, and kisses the red crown. Those in the arbour open their arms to him. He stands on the eastern side of the sky, where what goes up to the sky is brought to him, and he splits the storm.",
  "egy.pt.262": "The king asks a series of gods not to disown him, because they know him and he knows them: the god, Ra, Thoth, Horus the pre-eminent, the one in the Duat and the bull of heaven. Each is given an odd title such as \"the passing one,\" \"the solitary\" or \"this star.\" Then: the king comes and rises up, not by himself but because a messenger and a divine word lift him. He has passed his house; the great sea's fury has missed him; his fare is not taken in the great ship, and no palace can keep him from the path of the stars. He has reached the heights of the sky and seen his cobra in the evening and morning boats of the sun. The blessed have borne witness for him, and the hailstorm of heaven carries him to Ra.",
  "egy.pt.263": "The two reed floats of the sky are set out for Ra and for Harachte to cross to the horizon, and for the king to cross with them. The king is well and lives with his ka, wearing his loincloth, with his weapon on his arm and his sceptre in hand. Four eldest spirits with side-locks, who lean on their sceptres in the east of the sky, are brought to speak the king's good name to Ra and announce him to Nehebkau so that he is welcomed. The Field of Reeds is flooded so he can cross the Winding Waterway to the eastern side of the sky. His sister is Sothis and his mother is the morning star.",
  "egy.pt.264": "The two reed floats of the sky are set out for Horus, for Shesmeti and for the king to cross to the horizon and Harachte. The canal is open, the Winding Waterway is flooded and the Field of Reeds is full. The king will cross to the eastern sky, where the gods will give him birth new and young at the hour of the coming days. Ra will summon him and Nehebkau will feed him, like Horus of the horizon. He stands like the star under the sky's belly and judges like a god. Four gods on sceptres speak his name to Ra and Horus of the horizon, so that he is freed from his bonds. Ra has not given him to Osiris; he has not died, but become a spirit in the horizon and everlasting.",
  "egy.pt.265": "The two reed floats of the sky are set out for Ra, for Harachte and for the king to cross to the horizon. The canal is open, the Winding Waterway is flooded and the Field of Reeds is full. The king will cross to the eastern sky, where the gods are born, and be born with them like Horus of the horizon. Four guards with side-locks, standing on their sceptres in the east, are to speak his good name to Ra and Nehebkau. His sister is Sothis and his mother the morning star; he is the star with Ra under the sky's belly. A refrain repeats: the king is vindicated; praise to him and his ka.",
  "egy.pt.266": "The two reed floats of the sky are set out for Ra, Harachte and the king to cross to the horizon. The canal is open and the waters are full. Four friendly guards with side-locks, sitting on their sceptres in the east, are to speak the king's good name to Nehebkau; the king and his ka are praised and vindicated. Ra has taken him to the eastern sky, like Horus of the Duat and like a shining star. His sister is Sothis and his mother the morning star, and he sits between them. The sky and earth will never be empty of him. The text is then broken; what survives asks for a boat to be brought and says he will be third in Heliopolis.",
  "egy.pt.267": "The speaker says Osiris's heart, legs and arms belonged to him, and so the king's belong to him. A stairway to the sky will be laid down for him, and he goes up on the smoke of the great incense-burning. He flies like a goose and lands like a scarab on the empty throne in Ra's boat. He tells whoever sits there, who does not know the reed thicket, to get up so that the king can take his place and row Ra's boat across the sky. He pushes off from the earth as its navigator, sceptre in hand. He goes up to the sky and parts from the earth, from wife and office.",
  "egy.pt.268": "The king washes; Ra appears and the Great Ennead shines. The king puts humanity off like a limb and takes the crown from the Ennead. Isis nurses him and Nephthys suckles him. Horus takes him by the fingers, purifies him in the jackal's lake and cleans his ka in the lake of the Duat, rubbing them down with what is near Ra in the horizon. He brings them to the great palace. The king leads the undying stars and crosses to the Field of Reeds, rowed by those of the horizon and the sky. He is mighty; his arms do not fail him and his ka comes to help him.",
  "egy.pt.269": "The fire is laid and shines, and the incense on it shines. The incense's scent comes to the king and his to it; the gods' scent comes to him and his to them. He asks to be with the gods, live with them and be loved by them. Incense pellets come from the lap of Horus. Those who have climbed and lifted themselves up like Shu have come. The king climbs on the hips of Isis and Nephthys. His father Atum takes his arm and assigns him to the quick, wise, undying stars. He asks his mother Ipy to give him her breast so he can suck her white, sweet milk. In the land where he goes he will never thirst or hunger.",
  "egy.pt.270": "The king greets the ferryman of the sky, of Nut and of the gods, whose face looks behind him, and asks to be ferried across in the boat that carries the gods. He comes to his side and his temple as a god does. No one accuses him, living or dead, nor over a goose or an ox. If the ferryman will not take him, he will spring onto the wing of Thoth, who will carry him across.",
  "egy.pt.271": "The speaker says it was the king who flooded the land after it rose from the ocean, pulled up the papyrus, and reconciled and united the Two Lands; his mother, the great wild cow, will be united with him. The two djed-pillars stand and the broken steps fall. The king climbs the ladder his father Ra made for him; Horus and Set take his arms and bring him to the Duat, each warned to guard against the other. The god's face is shown to him, and he takes his seat on the great throne beside the god.",
  "egy.pt.272": "The king addresses the lofty door of Nun and asks that it be opened for him. He is small, yet he is chief of the Followers of Ra, not of the demons.",
  "egy.pt.273": "The sky darkens, the stars grow dim and the bones of the earth gods shake when they see the king rise as a ba, a god who lives on his fathers and feeds on his mothers. His honour is in the sky and his might in the horizon, like his father Atum, who made him mightier than himself. His cobras are on his brow. He is the bull of heaven who decided to live on the gods, eating them when they were full of magic. Named helpers lasso, guard, bind and behead the gods for him, and Shesmu cooks them in his evening pots. He eats their magic and swallows their spirits: the great ones for breakfast, the middle ones for supper, the little ones at night, and the old ones for his fire. He goes round both skies and the Two Lands and eats whoever he meets bit by bit. He is older than the eldest, served by thousands, and Orion gives him his title. He has broken backbones, taken the gods' hearts, eaten the crowns and swallowed the wisdom of every god, so his rank cannot be taken away. His life is eternity, he does as he wishes, and no evil-doer can destroy his place among the living.",
  "egy.pt.275": "The king says he comes to the falcons, though their houses are barred to him, wearing a cloak of ape-skin. He opens the double doors and goes to the edge of the horizon, lays a garment on the ground, and becomes like the Great One in Crocodilopolis.",
  "egy.pt.276": "This spell tells the snake in its hole, the opponent, that what it does will be turned against itself.",
  "egy.pt.277": "This spell says Horus falls because of his eye, and the bull (Set) collapses because of his testicles. It tells the creature to fall and collapse.",
  "egy.pt.278": "The spell says Babi has risen against the chief of Letopolis, whom that spittle protected; the spittle protects everyone the speaker loves. It tells the serpent it is loosed, and asks it to let the king be protected.",
  "egy.pt.279": "The king says he has trodden the mud of the waterways, and that Thoth protects him when it is dark.",
  "egy.pt.280": "This spell calls out to a \"doer\" and a \"passer\" whose face is behind them, and warns them to beware of the great door. The passage breaks off there.",
  "egy.pt.281": "This spell tells someone to punish the serpent, named with an untranslated name, and calls on two lions. The speaker asks for meat now, one pot, and tells the serpent to go.",
  "egy.pt.282": "This spell speaks of a foreign country at the river mouth and quotes a complaint: \"This land belongs to me, the lord of Heknu.\" It names the renowned ox-god of Heknu, against whom this was done. The meaning is obscure.",
  "egy.pt.283": "This spell says the king wags his left thumb at the creature, signalling with it to Min and his thunderbolt. It tells the robber not to rob.",
  "egy.pt.284": "This spell says the serpent that Atum bit has filled the king's mouth as it coiled. The centipede and the householder struck each other. That lion is inside this lion, and two bulls fight inside the ibis.",
  "egy.pt.285": "This spell tells the snake that its two drops of poison are going back to its poison glands, and to spit them out. It calls on a rain god to send rain so the serpent loses courage and the speaker's heart is safe, and a storm so the lion drowns and the king's heart is at ease.",
  "egy.pt.286": "This spell addresses creatures that gurgle like baby crocodiles and slip away, using several untranslated names. It says the red water flowers praise something, which belongs to the one who raised them. It ends: \"Hail, we two!\"",
  "egy.pt.287": "This spell says \"Nni, his mother\" twice, then asks whether the creature is really here, and tells the lion to go away.",
  "egy.pt.288": "This spell tells two serpents to go away, face on the road. It tells the king's eye not to look at them, and says the serpent will not have its way with the king.",
  "egy.pt.289": "This spell says a bull has fallen because of a serpent and the serpent because of the bull. It tells it to fall and slither away.",
  "egy.pt.290": "This spell says face falls on face, and a black, coloured knife goes out against the creature until it has swallowed what it seized.",
  "egy.pt.291": "This spell tells the \"white hole\" that its honour has been wiped out and stolen by the one who escaped the worm.",
  "egy.pt.292": "This spell tells a serpent that it has been seized, perhaps by its neighbour.",
  "egy.pt.293": "This spell tells the hidden serpent to go back and hide, so the king does not see it, and not to come where the king is, or he will speak its name, Nemi son of Nemit. A holy servant once fell into the Nile, so it should flee. It tells the beast to lie down.",
  "egy.pt.294": "The speaker says the king is Horus, who comes out of the acacia tree and was told to beware of the lion. He has come out of his jar after spending the night in it, and appears in the morning.",
  "egy.pt.295": "This spell says the lynx springs on the neck of one serpent and again on the neck of the serpent with raised head. It asks who will remain, and answers: the king.",
  "egy.pt.296": "This spell asks a serpent where it is going, tells it not to go but to stand by the king, for the king is Geb. It then asks another serpent whether its father should die. The meaning is obscure.",
  "egy.pt.297": "This spell tells the snake that the king's hand has come upon it violently: it is the lynx from the house of life. She strikes its face and scratches its eyes, so it falls in its own dung and urine. It tells it to fall, lie down and slither away, so that its mother Nut may see it.",
  "egy.pt.298": "This spell says Ra rises with his cobra on his head against the serpent that came out of the earth and is under the king's fingers. The king cuts off its head with the lynx's knife, pulls its teeth and drains its poison with the four strings of Osiris's sandals. It tells the beast to lie down and slither away.",
  "egy.pt.299": "This spell says the cobra belongs to the sky and Horus's centipede to the earth. Horus wore a sandal as he went against the bull of the hole, the fighting serpent. The king will not be beaten, for his protecting sycamore and his refuge are Horus's. He eats whoever he meets on his way bit by bit.",
  "egy.pt.300": "The king asks Herti, ferryman of the boat that Khnum made, to bring the boat to him. The king is Seker of Rosetau, on his way to Seker's place. He says it is \"our brother\" who brings the boat for the bridge-builders of the desert.",
  "egy.pt.301": "The speaker gives offerings to pairs of gods who shelter the gods: Nun and Naunet, Amun and Amaunet, Atum with Shu and Tefnut, who created and begat the gods. They are to tell their father that the king has given them their due, and not to stop him crossing to the horizon. The king knows that god's name: Nehi, lord of the year, Horus over the stars, who keeps Ra alive daily and will renew the king. The king brings Horus his great left eye as a healer, whole with its water, blood and breath, and tells him to put it on his brow as fine oil and shine among the gods, so the cobra goddess will love him. He then addresses the great reed float, likened to Wepwawet and to Sobek. He asks to be purified in the jackal's lake where the gods are purified, and says he has become a soul like Horus, lord of the green stone.",
  "egy.pt.302": "The sky is calm and Sothis lives, because the king, son of Sothis, lives. The Enneads have purified themselves for him. His house in the sky and throne on earth will never perish. People bury themselves and the gods fly up; Sothis has made the king fly to the sky, and Nut opened her arms to him. The two chief souls of Heliopolis spent the night weeping for the god. The king's throne is beside Ra, who will give it to no one else. He rises to Ra with a falcon's face, goose wings and claws like fangs. No one on earth or in heaven speaks against him; he has wiped away the charge. Wepwawet has made him fly; he flaps like a goose and a kite, and flies away from humankind.",
  "egy.pt.303": "The speaker asks the gods of the four directions to set out for the king the four pure reed floats they set out for Osiris, so he could cross to the sky with his son Horus, whom he raised to rise as a great god. He asks whether the king is Horus son of Osiris, the eldest son of Hathor, the seed of Geb. Osiris has decreed that the king rise as a second Horus, and the four souls in Heliopolis have written it in the register of the two Great Gods in the sky.",
  "egy.pt.304": "The king greets the daughter of Anubis at the windows of the sky, friend of Thoth at the ladder's rail; an ostrich on the bank of the Winding Waterway; and Ra's four-horned ox, asking each to open the way or bow its western horn so he can pass. Asked whether he is a pure westerner, he says he comes from the falcon city. He greets the Field of Offerings and the honoured ones in it, saying he will honour them.",
  "egy.pt.305": "Ra and Horus set up the ladder before Osiris as he goes to his spirit, one on each side, with the king between them. Asked whether he is a god of pure places, the king says he comes from a pure place. Horus tells him to stand, Set tells him to sit, and Ra says to take his arm. The spirit belongs to the sky and the body to the earth. The buried receive a thousand loaves and beers from Khenti-imentiu's table. If an heir is poor for want of a will, the king will write with his big finger, not his little one. The passage breaks off there.",
  "egy.pt.306": "The gods say how lovely it is to see the king go up to the sky, with his fame on him, fear of him all round and his magic going before him. Geb has done for him what was done for Geb. The Souls of Buto and Hierakonpolis and the gods of sky and earth make a support of their arms for him, and he climbs the sky as a ladder. Atum said the sky and earth should be his, as Geb had discussed. The kingdoms of Horus and Set and the Field of Reeds worship him as Sopdu under his trees. If someone killed him, he still remains, the last bull of the wild bulls, chief of the spirits for ever.",
  "egy.pt.307": "The king tells the god and Ra that a Heliopolitan like them is in him. His mother and father are Heliopolitans, and he was born in Heliopolis when Ra ruled the Enneads and Nefertem ruled people, the unequalled heir of Geb. Any god who raises his arm against him when he turns to worship will have no bread or cake among the gods, send no message, and the doors of the sun boats and the destroyer will not open to him. The king comes as the large-headed wild bull of the highlands from Heliopolis, who has given birth to the god.",
  "egy.pt.308": "The king greets Horus and Set in their regions, Iaru in the Field of Reeds, and the two harmonious goddesses, daughters of the four gods in the great palace, who came out naked at his voice. He has looked at them as Horus looked at Isis, as Nehebkau at Serqet, as Sobek at Neith, and as Set at the two goddesses.",
  "egy.pt.309": "The speaker says the king is a servant of the gods behind Ra's house, born of the gods' wish in the prow of Ra's boat. He sits before Ra, opens his boxes, breaks open and seals his letters, sends out his tireless messengers, and does what Ra tells him.",
  "egy.pt.310": "The king says that if he is bewitched, slandered, beaten or stopped on the road, the same will happen to Atum. He is Horus, coming after his father Osiris. He asks the ferryman whose face looks both ways to bring the boat; asked which boat, he says the one that flies up and lands.",
  "egy.pt.311": "The king asks Ra to look at and recognise him, as one of those who know him. He knows the offering ritual when his lord goes out, so that the doors of the horizon open for the morning boat, and he knows the throne hall from which Ra boards the evening boat. He asks Ra to commend him to the four raging winds around him, two-faced and two-mouthed, so they do not raise their arms against him. He will not be blind in darkness or deaf without Ra's voice. He asks Ra to take him along to drive away storms, clouds and hail, and promises endless praise.",
  "egy.pt.312": "The speaker says the bread flies to his houses, the houses of the crown of Lower Egypt.",
  "egy.pt.313": "The phallus of Babi is drawn and the sky's double doors open, then lock. The way runs over flames under what the gods make, through which every Horus slips, and the king slips through too. They make a way for him, for he is a Horus.",
  "egy.pt.314": "This spell tells the ox to get back; it will be killed, and the earth god's fingers will be on its horns. It tells it to fall and slither away.",
  "egy.pt.315": "The king addresses three kinds of ape. His death depends on his own wish and his blessedness comes from himself. He will give the same homage they give and sit among them.",
  "egy.pt.316": "The king tells two beings, Hemi and Sehed, that he has not given them his magic. He will sit beside the revered one of Heliopolis, and asks them to take him to the sky.",
  "egy.pt.317": "The king says he has come out today at the head of the flood. He is a crocodile god with green feathers, alert face and raised brow, springing from the Great One. He comes to his waterways in the flooded land of Mehet-weret and the green fields of the horizon, to make the plants green and bring green to the great eye. He takes his throne in the horizon and appears as Sobek, son of Neith. He eats, urinates and has sex, and is lord of the seed women receive from their husbands, wherever he wishes.",
  "egy.pt.318": "The king says he is the serpent, the leading bull, who swallowed its seven cobras, which became its seven neck bones, and who commands seven Enneads. His mother is Henut. He has come to swallow and take myrrh, his nose and nails full of it. He tells the gods he has taken their necks, and they should serve him, who will give them their courage.",
  "egy.pt.319": "The king says he is the bull of the two lights in his eye. His mouth is safe because of his fiery breath and his head because of his horns, as lord of the South. He leads the god and rules the Ennead, makes lapis lazuli grow and plants sprout. He has tied plant cords, united the skies and rules the southern and northern lands like the gods of old. He built a divine city and is the third at his rising.",
  "egy.pt.320": "The king says he has ordered the night and sent the hours on their way. The stars rise and proclaim him as Babi. He is the son of a mother who did not know she had borne him, the fierce-faced lord of nights. The lords and subjects are told to humble and hide themselves before him, for he is Babi, lord of night, the bull without whom life would end.",
  "egy.pt.321": "The king asks the ferryman whose face is behind him to bring the object that was on Osiris's back, so he can climb to the sky on it and serve Ra as a courtier.",
  "egy.pt.322": "The speaker says sky and earth are open; two sets of double doors open for Horus and Set. He tells them to turn for the king, who has passed over them like Atum. The king is Khaitau, who lives among the Nega mountains.",
  "egy.pt.323": "The king has purified himself with Ra in the Sea of Reeds. Horus dries his skin and Thoth his feet. The speaker asks Shu to lift him high and Nut to give him her arm.",
  "egy.pt.324": "The king greets Horus's doorkeeper at the gate of Osiris and asks him to announce his name to Horus, for he comes with spittle for his aching temple, which hurts at the start of each month and goes bald every half month; he asks whether it will be cooled with the magic once made for the Great One. He greets the everlasting hippopotamus that he killed with Horus's club, and the braying ass he killed with a tail from Osiris's sea. He greets Khnum, made harmless though he built the king: he is a plant trodden under the king's foot that cannot straighten, and one of the two pillars of the great palace. Parts of the text are restored.",
  "egy.pt.325": "The double doors of the sky open at daybreak for Horus of the gods, Harachte, Horus of the East, Horus of Shesmet, and the king himself, so each can go up and purify himself in the Field of Reeds. The king is clean; he takes his lasting bones and stretches out the undying limbs that were in his mother Nut. He asks Ra for his arm; Shu will draw him up to his companions after feeding him the milk of two black cows. He tells three beings, Hepat, Hennu and Semennu, that Nut's body aches with the divine seed in her, and the king is that seed. He is purified and wears his divine robe, so he can stand among them as a god, and asks them to take him and keep him with them.",
  "egy.pt.326": "The king addresses the lovely collar on Ra's neck, beloved of Horus: if it goes to the sky, he will go to the sky too.",
  "egy.pt.327": "The king says he was the beloved messenger who brought Horus back his eye, Set his testicles and Thoth his arm. The Two Enneads tremble, for they are the messengers the king loves, who should bring him to food, and they do.",
  "egy.pt.328": "The speaker says the king is the exalted one at the front with raised brow, the star the gods bow to and the Two Enneads tremble before. His own hand will lift him up.",
  "egy.pt.329": "The speaker says the king is the exalted one at the front with raised brow, the star the gods bow to and the Two Enneads tremble before. His face sees his own rising, and he is a nose that breathes.",
  "egy.pt.330": "The king goes up to the sky on an object (untranslated) that was present at the separation of sky and earth, after the sky's sole was grasped by a raised hand.",
  "egy.pt.331": "The king goes up to the sky on an object (untranslated) that was present at the separation of sky and earth, after the sky's sole was grasped. He is a nose that breathes, and his face sees his own rising.",
  "egy.pt.332": "The king comes out of the jar. He went up as his own warm breath and came back. He has gone to the sky and returned to the earth; he has walked on the green plants under Geb's feet and treads again on Nut's paths.",
  "egy.pt.333": "The king purified himself on the mound of the earth where Ra purified himself. He set up a stand and the ladder, and those in the great sky will take his arm.",
  "egy.pt.334": "The king greets Ra, who crosses the sky and sails through Nut and has crossed the Winding Waterway. The king has grasped his tail, for he is a god, son of a god. He is a golden flower that comes from the ka. He has crossed Buto like Herti and sailed through Kenmut like Shesmu in his oil-press boat. He asks the god to let him live as Fetekta lives.",
  "egy.pt.335": "The speaker says how beautiful the king looks with Ra's horns, an apron like Hathor's and a falcon's feather, as he rises to the sky among the gods.",
  "egy.pt.336": "The king greets the ox of oxen as it makes the ascent. He seizes it by the tail and the hindquarters; a Great One is before it and behind it. He asks the Great One among the gods to take him, since he belongs to him. His heart is whole and the parts of his body are young.",
  "egy.pt.337": "The sky speaks and the earth shakes in fear of Osiris as he ascends. The milk cows and nurse cows are told to gather round him, weep, praise and mourn him as he goes up to the sky among his brothers the gods.",
  "egy.pt.338": "The king tells hunger not to come to him but to go to Nun and the flood. He is full, thanks to the bread of Horus made by his chief maid, which restores him. Shu and Tefnut keep him from thirst and hunger, and the four sons of Horus, Hapi, Duamutef, Qebehsenuf and Imsety, will drive the hunger and thirst out of him.",
  "egy.pt.339": "The king's hunger and thirst are in the hands of Shu and Tefnut. He lives on the morning bread that comes on time, and eats what Shu and Tefnut eat.",
  "egy.pt.340": "The king comes to Nekhekh and asks him to fall back before him as the east wind before the west, and follow him as the north wind follows the south. A note says to set down an offering.",
  "egy.pt.341": "The face of Horus is opened by Aker and Aker's by Horus. Abundance has held out her arm to the king, and he has taken up fowling. Everything the marsh produces belongs to her son Hab, and the king has eaten with him today.",
  "egy.pt.342": "The speaker tells Isis, Asbet and Nephthys that it is the king, and to come and see their son. He has passed through Athribis and the region of the crown. His bag and basket are woven from plants, and he comes bringing what is wanted and given.",
  "egy.pt.343": "Bedshet comes and the brazier burns. Those with ready hands stand to give the king an offering.",
  "egy.pt.344": "The king greets the Great Flood, cup-bearer of the gods and leader of people, asking him to make people and gods kind to him so they give him offerings.",
  "egy.pt.345": "The king asks Wer-kaf, Horus's cup-bearer, head of Ra's dining hall and Ptah's cook, to give generously; the king eats as much as he gives.",
  "egy.pt.346": "Kas are, were and will be in Buto; the king's ka is in Buto, red as flame and alive as Khepri. Be cheerful: a meal for butchers. The speaker asks his lady to give the king love and respect in the hearts of all the gods.",
  "egy.pt.347": "The king's mouth is in incense and his lips in myrrh. He is told to go down from his ka's field to the Field of Offerings. His meal is like the divine boat's. His life will be longer than the year goddess's and his food more than the flood's. He asks his ka to bring food so they can eat together. Part of one line is missing.",
  "egy.pt.348": "The king greets the Great Flood, cup-bearer of the gods and leader of people, asking him to make the gods kind to him, so they refresh him, love him and keep him well.",
  "egy.pt.349": "The king asks Wer-kaf, Horus's cup-bearer, head of Ra's dining hall and Ptah's cook, to give generously; the king eats as much as he gives, a generous helping of meat.",
  "egy.pt.350": "The king addresses the wide-striding one who sows emerald, malachite and turquoise as stars: if she is green and well, he will be green like living plants.",
  "egy.pt.351": "A vulture became pregnant with the king in the night; he is on the horn of the pregnant cow. If she is green and well, he will be green like living plants.",
  "egy.pt.352": "A vulture became pregnant with the king in the night; he is on the horn of the pregnant cow. He is her papyrus shoot, green as the turquoise stars, and green with her like living plants.",
  "egy.pt.353": "The king has come out of Buto, red as fire and alive as Khepri.",
  "egy.pt.354": "An offering of the butcher and of the cup-bearer, who is told to bring water. The rest of the text is broken.",
  "egy.pt.355": "The sky's double doors open. The speaker tells the king his head is joined to his bones and his bones to his head; the great bolts are drawn back and a brick is taken out of the great tomb for him. He has a jackal's face and a lion's tail and sits on his throne commanding the spirits. He comes to the speaker as Horus did after avenging Osiris. The speaker says he is his Anubis-priest. The king puts his hand on the land and his arm over the great region where he travels among the spirits, and is told to rise like Osiris.",
  "egy.pt.356": "The speaker tells Osiris the king that Horus has come looking for him. Horus has had Thoth turn back the Followers of Set and bring them to him, and has made Set afraid, for Osiris is older and better. Geb saw this and put him in his place, bringing him his sisters Isis and Nephthys. Horus has made the gods join and avenge him. Geb has put his foot on the enemy's head; Horus struck him and took back his eye to give to Osiris, making him glorious and mighty. Nut made him a god despite Set and spread herself over him. Horus has put Set under him to carry him and tremble beneath him, so that Osiris holds him and he cannot escape. Horus has avenged him, so he may be content.",
  "egy.pt.357": "Horus says: may Geb make an offering to Osiris the king. Geb has given him his two eyes and had Horus hand them over. Isis and Nephthys found him, and Horus had them protect him. Horus is glad to be in his arms. He is told to come close to Horus and not go far. Horus has struck and tied up Set for him, made Set afraid and put him underneath to carry him, and Set's followers dare not resist. Horus has opened his mouth. He should not groan; Geb has brought Horus to count the gods' hearts, and none escapes. Horus has quickly avenged him and taken his eye back from Set; he should keep this sweet eye. Isis has cared for him, Horus's heart is glad, and Horus will avenge what Set did to him.",
  "egy.pt.358": "The speaker tells the king he is Shu's eldest son, and the two lords of Nun have loosed his bonds.",
  "egy.pt.359": "Horus wailed for his eye and Set for his testicles. The eye of Horus leapt across the Winding Waterway to escape Set and landed on Thoth's wing, where Thoth saw it. The king asks the gods who cross on Thoth's wing to the eastern sky to speak with Set about the eye to let him cross with them. He greets the ferryman of the Winding Waterway, \"face-behind,\" and asks him to announce him to Ra. The king is going to the far palace of the lords of kas, where Ra is worshipped each morning; Ra commends him to the ferryman to be carried across. He is looking for the injured eye of Horus. The gods wash his face, with the four sons of Horus on his right and other gods on his left. His seat and helm know him, and he finds his seat empty in Ra's golden boat.",
  "egy.pt.360": "The king addresses the lofty, untouched gate of Nut. He is Shu, who came out of Atum, and asks Nun to open the gate for him, for he comes as a soul, divine.",
  "egy.pt.361": "Nun has commended the king to Atum, and Pega to Shu, so that the double doors of the sky are opened for him, despite nameless people. He asks to be taken by the arm to the sky so he does not die on earth among humans.",
  "egy.pt.362": "The king calls on his father Atum in the darkness to bring him to his side, so he can light the lamp for him and protect him, as Nun protected the four goddesses Isis, Nephthys, Neith and Serqet on the day they guarded the throne.",
  "egy.pt.363": "The king asks the Way of Horus to prepare its tent and arms for him. Ra is coming, so it should ferry him across as it ferries the plant it loves. If it stretches out its arm to the West or the East, it should also stretch it to the king, as it did for its eldest son, the ape.",
  "egy.pt.364": "The speaker tells Osiris the king to rise. Horus has come, claimed him back from the gods, loved him and fitted his eye to him so he can see. The gods have tied on his face; Isis and Nephthys have healed him. Horus is close; Osiris is his ka, and he should listen to Horus. Geb has brought Horus, who has found him and rejoices. Horus has set him at the head of the gods, given him the crown and his hard eye so his enemies fear him, and rounded up the gods so none can escape. Nephthys has gathered his limbs and made them well. He has been given to his mother Nut as his grave and tomb, and she has embraced him. Horus has joined his limbs so he stands without staggering, and has quickly avenged him. He is the mightiest god; Horus's children carry him and all the gods follow him. He lives, moves every day, and is glorious and mighty for ever.",
  "egy.pt.365": "The speaker tells the king, great in power, to rise quickly, sit at the head of the gods and do what Osiris did in the prince's house at Heliopolis, now that he has his rank. Nothing will stop his steps in the sky or on earth, for he is a spirit born of Nut and nursed by Nephthys. He will stand in his place and do as he did before, a spirit above all spirits. In Buto and Hierakonpolis he will find those he must resist. He does what Osiris does, as the one on his throne, dressed as the great wild bull, and nowhere will he be opposed.",
  "egy.pt.366": "The speaker tells Osiris the king to stand up: his mother Nut bore him and Geb wiped his mouth. The Great Ennead avenges him and puts his enemy under him, telling the enemy to carry one greater than himself. His sisters Isis and Nephthys come and heal him, whole and great as \"Great Black\" and fresh as \"Great Green.\" He is great and round like the circle of the world. Isis and Nephthys protected their lord in Asyut and worship and care for him so he will not leave or be angry. His sister comes rejoicing in her love for him; he places her on his phallus and his seed enters her, sharp like Sothis. Horus the sharp comes from him, and as \"Horus the son who avenges his father\" he avenges him.",
  "egy.pt.367": "The speaker tells Osiris the king that Geb has brought Horus to avenge him and bring him the gods' hearts, so he will not lack or groan. Horus has given him his eye so he can take the crown before the gods, and has gathered and put his limbs together in good order. Thoth has seized his enemy and beheaded him and his followers, sparing none.",
  "egy.pt.368": "The speaker tells Osiris the king that Horus is in his arms and will avenge him; Horus is glad to be with him again, and Osiris holds him tight so he will not leave. Horus keeps him from sickness, puts his enemy under his feet, and gives him his children to carry him. His mother Nut spreads herself over him, makes him a god and shields him from all evil; he is the greatest of her children. Geb is pleased with him, loves and protects him, has given him back his head, and has had Thoth care for him so that what was against him ended.",
  "egy.pt.369": "The speaker tells Osiris the king to stand, for Horus has raised him and Geb has let Horus see his father in him. Horus has given him the gods to brighten his face and his eye to see with, and put his enemy under him to carry him so he returns to his former state. The gods have tied his face back on. The children of Horus have struck his enemy bloody and punished him until he stinks. Horus has fitted his mouth to his bones, opened it, and restored his two eyes, so his face will never lack sight.",
  "egy.pt.370": "The speaker tells Osiris the king that Horus has made the gods join and befriend him. He should go to Horus and not leave him. Horus is used to him and cannot part from him, and has given him life; he should accept Horus's word and listen, for it will not harm him. Horus has gathered all the gods and none escapes. Osiris has joined Horus's children, who love him. Horus has done this for his own ka in him, so he may be content.",
  "egy.pt.371": "The speaker tells Osiris the king that Horus has set him in the gods' hearts, given him the white crown, found him and rejoices. He should go out against his enemy, for he is greater. Horus has made the enemy carry him, rescued him and avenged him. Geb has seen his character and put him in his place. Horus has laid the enemy under him, for Osiris is older and was born first. He is the father who begot Horus, and Horus's heart is glad because of him as First of the Westerners.",
  "egy.pt.372": "The speaker tells Osiris the king to wake. Horus has had Thoth bring his enemy and put Osiris on his back so he cannot resist. He should mount and sit on him so he cannot escape, then get down and do him harm, for he is stronger. Horus has broken his enemies' legs, brought them cut up, and driven out their kas, so Osiris can do to them what he wishes.",
  "egy.pt.373": "The speaker tells the king to get up, take his head, join his bones, gather his limbs and shake the earth off his flesh. He should take his bread that cannot mould and his beer that cannot sour. He stands at the doors that hold people back; a god comes out, takes his arm and brings him to the sky, to his father Geb. Geb rejoices, gives him his arm, kisses and embraces him, and sets him at the head of the spirits and undying stars. The hidden ones worship him, the great gather and the watchers stand before him. Barley and spelt are harvested for his monthly and half-monthly feasts, as Geb ordered. He should rise; he will not die.",
  "egy.pt.374": "The speaker tells the king that he is great and has been ferried over, and his name is announced to Osiris. His stride is great enough to cross the sky. The earth gods do not seize him and the stars do not turn him away. The two doors of the sky open for him to go out like Horus and like the jackal who hid his forms from his enemies, for he has no human father or mother.",
  "egy.pt.375": "The speaker says the king is the one whom Tw will protect and Tshii will rescue. Tshii's messenger is told to bring his message while it is fresh, and not to come against the king, son of a Great One, as a castrating knife.",
  "egy.pt.376": "A short spell: \"The castrating knife! Shining, shining; triumphant, triumphant.\" It asks the sailor to take off his clothes as a sail for the sun's boat.",
  "egy.pt.377": "This spell tells the serpent that it will land as \"Fortress\" and capsize as \"Igai,\" for it is the snake on its belly that lives on the hearts of the gods of Heliopolis. It tells it to give way and go.",
  "egy.pt.378": "This spell says the cobra belongs to the sky and Horus's centipede to the earth. Horus's sandal trod on the serpent that threatened the child Horus, finger in his mouth. The king is also the child Horus; if the snake threatens him he will tread on it, but if it is sensible he will not. It is the hidden one, as the gods call it, because it has no legs or arms to follow its brother gods. The spell then asks two pairs of beings, the unlucky and the rising, who tie the god's knot, to protect the king so he will protect them.",
  "egy.pt.379": "A short spell: \"Your water is in the sky; your thousands are on earth.\" It ends with an untranslated name.",
  "egy.pt.380": "This spell calls out to a \"doer\" and a \"passer,\" telling it its foot is behind it and to beware of the \"great Great.\" The passage breaks off there.",
  "egy.pt.381": "This spell says the great centipede comes down after charming the householder, and the householder is charmed by the centipede.",
  "egy.pt.382": "This spell tells two serpents to go away from the king. Horus goes round behind his eye. It tells the \"reverse-serpent\" to rot in the earth.",
  "egy.pt.383": "This spell asks the serpent where it is going and tells it to stand by the king. It asks whether its father should die. A holy servant once fell into the Nile, and the creature is told to come here.",
  "egy.pt.384": "This spell tells the snake that the king's hand coming against it is the hand of the great goddess in the house of life. Whoever she seizes does not live, and whoever she strikes never puts his head back on. It tells it to fall and slither away.",
  "egy.pt.385": "This spell says Ra rises against the snake, and Horus bends his Nine Bows against the spirit coming out of the earth, its head cut off and tail clipped. It names several serpents, including Djedi, son of Serqet, and tells them to turn over and heed Geb, or the branding iron over their heads will heed them. It calls on the earth to seize it. If the king moves his arm against it, it dies; if he lets it go, it still will not live. Shu stands on its bonds. The king's fingers are the lynx's fingers. It should spit, fall, flee and turn over; Horus would have struck it dead and Set would have cut it to pieces.",
  "egy.pt.386": "The king comes to Iutiu and asks to be let through \"the divided opening\"; if he is driven back, he will drive Iutiu back. Horus fell because of his eye and Set suffered because of his testicles. The serpent with raised head in the bush is told to fall and slither away.",
  "egy.pt.387": "This spell says a Great One and a holy servant of the Ennead have fallen, and tells the beast to lie down.",
  "egy.pt.388": "This spell says Horus has risen and escaped the fighting serpent; the king is that Horus. It tells someone to hurry, since he has no messenger and his boy has been taken, and to say that Horus smashed the mouth of the serpent \"Fowling-with-the-phallus\" with his foot.",
  "egy.pt.389": "This spell tells the god in the hole to lie on his back before the king, who is the great mistress. Whoever the king sees will not live, and whoever his face falls on will not have his head put back. It tells the serpent in the bush to slither away and turn over.",
  "egy.pt.390": "The king and his ka are pure. He is as well as the bodily health of Horus and of Set, and his health lies between them. He strung the bow like Horus and drew the string like Osiris. Whoever the creature is, Horus or Set, it will be thrown on its head or back. The king's foot and hand placed on it are those of the lynx goddess in the house of life. He strikes its face so its spit runs out. Part of the text is broken. It ends by telling the serpents to lie down and slither away.",
  "egy.pt.391": "The text is partly broken. It tells something twice to lie down on its side and to escape and go away, then asks for the king to be rescued and protected. It ends: your message is ready, your will is received, and what is before you is at rest.",
  "egy.pt.392": "A short spell: the king's water is in the sky and his people are on earth. It ends with words that may mean \"the heart is sad.\"",
  "egy.pt.393": "This spell tells the fighting serpent that its protecting sycamore is its grain and its grain its sycamore, that its tail shall be in its mouth, and tells the great bull to turn round. A line is broken. It warns the serpent to protect itself against the earth and against Geb.",
  "egy.pt.394": "A short spell: a lion is behind a lion for life's sake, and two bulls are inside the ibis.",
  "egy.pt.395": "Said twice: this spell tells the earth to protect itself against the earth, and the serpent to protect itself against Geb, the father who begot Osiris.",
  "egy.pt.396": "A one-line spell telling a serpent that there is a smell of the plough being drawn through the earth.",
  "egy.pt.397": "The text is partly broken. It asks whether someone is a certain being, says he is bubbling up, and asks Shu to put his arms around the king.",
  "egy.pt.398": "This spell tells the hoer not to hoe the earth, and to protect himself from the enemy. The king was conceived and born of a being called Dammiu, who went to his mother with him.",
  "egy.pt.399": "A short spell: \"Your water is in the sky; your people are on earth.\" It ends with an untranslated name.",
  "egy.pt.400": "The eye of Horus drips on the tuft of a plant. The king asks the two Horuses, chiefs of the houses and great lords of food in Heliopolis, to give him bread and beer and refresh his table and slaughtering bench. If he is hungry or thirsty, so will the two lions and the goddess of el-Kab be. He tells Hedjenut not to bring the smell of her plant to him.",
  "egy.pt.401": "The king has come from Buto, red as flame and alive as Khepri. He has seen the great cobra and his face has fallen on her. Hu bowed his head to him as he crossed his lake with his cobra following.",
  "egy.pt.402": "The king's place with Geb is widened and his star with Ra is raised, so he can walk in the Field of Offerings. He is Ra's eye, conceived in the night and born every day.",
  "egy.pt.403": "The king greets the gods of green trees and fields, the flower-opener on his sycamore and the lord of the green fields, and asks them to rejoice today, for he will live among them and on what they live on. He asks the bulls of Atum to refresh him more than the red crown on his head, the flood up to his chest and the dates in his fist.",
  "egy.pt.404": "The king says he juggles with the juggler, said four times, who was over the officials of Buto. He is greater than Horus adorned in red, the red crown once on Ra's head. His green eye paint is the papyrus of the god's flaming eye, and he is green like him.",
  "egy.pt.405": "The king calls on Ra by several untranslated names and says: \"The king is you and you are the king.\" Praise to the king and his ka. He asks Ra to make him well and refreshed, since he does the same for Ra. He is Ra's eye on Hathor's horn, which brings round the years, conceived in the night and born every day.",
  "egy.pt.406": "The king greets Ra in all his beauty and places. He asks him to bring Isis's milk, Nephthys's flood, the waters of the lake and ocean, life, health and happiness, bread, beer, clothing and food to live on. He hopes the patient brewers will look after him so he can sit at his meal. He wants to see Ra set out as Thoth when his boat heads to his fields in the sky.",
  "egy.pt.407": "The king is pure and receives his pure place in the sky, in the bow of Ra's boat; he and his places will last. The sailors who row Ra round the horizon will row him too. His mouth, nose and ears are opened so he can judge words, separate the two quarrellers and give orders even to one greater than himself. Ra purifies and protects him from harm.",
  "egy.pt.408": "The speaker calls on \"Born-in-the-night\": the king is born. The two women who conceived by day are asked to be patient and give birth to him who lives in the egg city, and having borne him, to feed him. The king's heart is glad like the chief of the Duat, and the gods rejoice to see him young again. The feast of the sixth day of the month will be his breakfast and the feast of the seventh his supper; cows will be slaughtered for him at the Wag festival, for he is the bull of Heliopolis.",
  "egy.pt.409": "The king is the bull of the Ennead, lord of five meals, three in heaven and two on earth, brought to him by the evening and morning boats of the sun from the god's house. He hates offal and rejects urine and will not drink it. He lives on sweet wood and the incense that is in the earth.",
  "egy.pt.410": "The king addresses Osiris of Busiris, the djed-pillar. The king will be his companion, and finds him sitting in the fortress where the gods live and to which the lords of kas are drawn. The rest of the text is broken.",
  "egy.pt.411": "Most of this spell is lost. What survives asks for something to be brought to the king and for him to be placed on the side of life and joy (this part is restored).",
  "egy.pt.412": "The Great One has fallen on his side and stirs; Ra lifts his head, for he hates sleep and weariness. The speaker tells the king's flesh not to rot, decay or smell, and not to step in the fluid of Osiris's corpse. He will walk the sky like Orion and be sharp like Sothis; he is a soul and honoured. His soul stands among the gods like Horus, and the gods dread him as they dread the crowns. He takes the arm of the undying stars. His bones and flesh will not perish, for he is one of the gods. Buto and Hierakonpolis sail to him; mourners weep and priests robe for him; Ra welcomes him. The sky's doors open after he has gone down like Anubis on his belly. The great maiden of Heliopolis gives him her arm, for he has no human mother or father; his mother is the great wild cow of el-Kab, who will nurse him and never wean him. He is told to turn from his left side to his right. He appears crowned, mace in hand, judging the gods' words. He belongs to Ra's servant stars, will be reborn each new moon, and the sky will never be empty of him.",
  "egy.pt.413": "The speaker tells the king to rise: his water, plenty and the milk in his mother Isis's breasts are his. The children of Horus raise him. The Great One fell asleep. He is told to wake, take his head, join his bones, shake off his dust, sit on his firm throne and eat his leg of meat and ribs in the sky among the gods.",
  "egy.pt.414": "The speaker tells the king to take his garment of light and his veil and clothe himself in the eye of Horus from the weaving goddess Tait, so it wins him respect and recognition among the gods and he can take the crown with it, among the gods and with Horus, lord of people.",
  "egy.pt.415": "The king greets the weaving goddess Tait, who was at the edge of the great nest that united the god with his brother. He asks her to protect his head so it does not come off, gather his bones so they do not separate, and put love for him in every god who sees him.",
  "egy.pt.416": "The speaker says this is a whole, sound garment that Horus made for his father Osiris.",
  "egy.pt.417": "A Great One slept on his mother Nut. His mother Tait clothed him and carried him to the sky as \"Kite,\" the darling she found, her Horus. The speaker tells Isis that this is her Horus, and asks her to take his credentials to Ra in the horizon.",
  "egy.pt.418": "The speaker greets fine oil that was on Horus's brow, which Horus put on his father Osiris's head. The king has put it on his own head in the same way.",
  "egy.pt.419": "The speaker greets the king on his day as he stands before Ra rising in the east, adorned with his rank among the spirits. Arms, feet and hands move for him in mourning. Isis took his arm and led him into the shrine; the earth is decked and his mourners weep. Anubis gives him thousands of loaves, beers, ointments, perfumes, garments and oxen, and geese are killed for him. Horus has destroyed the evil in him in four days and Set has undone what he did in eight. The doors open; he is told to rise, shake off his dust and sail with the spirits, with a falcon's wings and a star's brightness. His heart will not be taken. He wears an undamaged crown, crosses the sky to the Field of Reeds and lives in the Field of Offerings among the undying stars, following Osiris.",
  "egy.pt.420": "The speaker tells the king to be pure and burn incense for Ra. How beautiful his purity is today! Today he should take his place among the gods and among those in the god's booth.",
  "egy.pt.421": "The speaker tells the king that he climbs up and reaches the radiance; he is the brightness on the eastern side of the sky.",
  "egy.pt.422": "The speaker tells the king that he has gone to become a spirit, mighty as a god and enthroned like Osiris, with his soul in his body, his power behind him and his crowns at hand. A god's followers chant that a god comes, the king comes to Osiris's throne. Isis speaks to him and Nephthys mourns; spirits bow and kiss the ground at his feet. He goes up to his mother Nut, who shows him the way to Ra; the sky's doors open and Ra, waiting, leads him in and seats him on Osiris's throne. The eye of Horus tells him his soul and power have come, for Horus has avenged Osiris. He stands avenged, in the form of Osiris, doing what Osiris did among the undying stars, while his son sits on his throne at the head of the living, growing barley and spelt for him. Ra gives him life, health and eternity. His name lives and lasts on earth, and he will never perish.",
  "egy.pt.423": "The speaker tells Osiris the king to take this libation from Horus, in his name \"He who came from the cataract,\" and his natron, so he may be divine. Nut has made him a god. He should take the fluid that comes from him. Horus has had the speaker gather the gods from everywhere he went and count Horus's children as far as the place where he drowned. Herenpwi knows him, for he is young again as \"Fresh water.\" Horus is a soul, for he recognises his father in him.",
  "egy.pt.424": "The speaker tells the king that his going is the going of Horus, whose runners and envoys hurried to announce him to the one who raises his arm in the East. He is told to rejoice: his arms are like Wepiu's and his face like Wepwawet's. A royal offering is made so he can occupy the regions of Horus and pass through those of Set, sit on his firm throne and speak to the Great Ennead in Heliopolis. Mekhenti-irti, his herdsman, protects him, and another (name broken) protects him from spirits. He should take his daily divine offering: thousands of loaves, beers, oxen, geese, sweet things and cloths. His water, plenty and natron are brought by his brother Nekhekh.",
  "egy.pt.425": "The speaker tells Osiris the king that he is avenged: \"I have given you all the gods, with their inheritance, their food and all their things. You shall not die.\"",
  "egy.pt.426": "The speaker tells Osiris the king that he has risen as king of Upper and Lower Egypt, because he has gained power over the gods and their kas.",
  "egy.pt.427": "The speaker asks Nut to spread herself over her son, Osiris the king, hide him from Set and protect him. She has come to shelter her son; she should protect this Great One.",
  "egy.pt.428": "The speaker asks Nut to fall upon her son, Osiris the king, and protect him, this Great One among her children.",
  "egy.pt.429": "Geb tells Nut that she has become mighty, as she already was in her mother Tefnut's womb before she was born. He asks her to protect the king with life and well-being; he shall not die.",
  "egy.pt.430": "The speaker tells Nut that her heart was mighty when she was in her mother's body, in her name of \"Nut.\"",
  "egy.pt.431": "The speaker tells Nut that she is the daughter who gained power over her mother and rose as king of Lower Egypt. He asks her to make the king mighty in her womb; he shall not die.",
  "egy.pt.432": "The speaker tells the great lady who became the sky that she has grown mighty and victorious and filled every place with her beauty. The whole earth lies under her and she holds it in her arms. He asks her to set the king in her as an undying star.",
  "egy.pt.433": "The speaker tells Nut: \"I have made you fertile as Geb, in your name of 'Sky,' and joined the whole earth to you everywhere.\"",
  "egy.pt.434": "The speaker tells Nut, high above the earth, that she is above her father Shu and has mastery over him; he loved her and put himself under her, and everything is hers. She has taken each god with his boat and taught them as \"She of a thousand souls,\" so they will not vanish from her like stars. She is asked not to let the king leave her.",
  "egy.pt.435": "Nut, \"the Granary,\" says she has proclaimed the name of Osiris the king with his royal titles: Horus, beloved of the Two Lands; king of Upper and Lower Egypt; beloved of the two goddesses; golden falcon; Geb's beloved heir; beloved of all the gods. He is given life, stability, health and joy like Ra, and lives for ever.",
  "egy.pt.436": "A note says to pour a libation. The speaker tells the king that his water and plenty are his: the fluid from the god, from Osiris, so his hands are washed and his ears opened. He should wash, as his ka washes, and let his ka sit and eat bread with him for ever. He goes as Osiris's successor. The scent of grain pleases his nose, and his feet, teeth and nails enjoy his feast. He crosses as the great bull to Ra's beloved fields. He is told to rise; he will not die.",
  "egy.pt.437": "The speaker tells the king to wake for Horus, stand against Set, and rise like Osiris and like Anubis, before whom the Ennead trembles. The year's beginnings will be celebrated for him, and he purifies himself at the new moon. The earth is told to hear what Ra says as he makes the king a spirit at the head of the gods, like Horus son of Osiris. The earth answers that the doors of Aker and Geb are open; he goes out at Anubis's call to judge the gods and set bounds to the Bows. He passes Abydos; a gate in the sky opens and the gods rejoice and take him up as a soul. He crosses the Winding Waterway as a star; the Duat takes his hand toward Orion. He eats the gods' food and wears the incense of Dedun from Nubia. Ra calls him out of the sky and makes him the morning star in the Field of Reeds. The crowns gather his scattered limbs. Royal and Anubis offerings are made: thousands of antelopes, loaves, beers, sweet things and oxen, and the trees bow to him.",
  "egy.pt.438": "The speaker tells the king, his father, that he will do this for him, since the king has no human father or mother: his father is the great wild bull and his mother the young cow. He is to live and never die, like Horus of Letopolis after the great pit of Heliopolis was opened for him. The chief chair-bearers of Khenti-imentiu give him water at the start of each month and half-month, so he can provide for the great and lead the small. He gets his ribs of meat from Khenti-imentiu's slaughtering bench, as befits his rank among the honoured.",
  "egy.pt.439": "The speaker says the king is Satis, the burning one who has taken both lands. He has gone up to the sky, found Ra standing, and sat down beside him. Ra does not let him throw himself on the ground, knowing the king is greater than he is. The king is more of a spirit, more excellent and more lasting than the rest. He has triumphed over the lady of offerings, stood with Ra in the north of the sky, and taken both lands as king of the gods.",
  "egy.pt.440": "The speaker tells Horus, keeper of the staff of truth, that if he wants to live he must not shut the sky's double doors or block its way once he has taken the king's ka to the sky. There the ka will join the god's noble and beloved ones, who lean on their sceptres, guard Upper Egypt, dress in fine cloth, eat figs, drink wine and anoint themselves with oil, so that it may speak for the king to the Great God and help him climb up to him.",
  "egy.pt.441": "The speaker tells the king that the earth has been hoed and an offering made for him as he goes on the gods' road. He should turn and see this offering, made by the king and by the First of the Westerners, as he goes to the undying stars in the north.",
  "egy.pt.442": "The Great One has fallen on his side and been thrown down. Ra takes his arm and the Two Enneads lift his head. He has come back as Orion; Osiris has come as Orion, lord of wine at the Wag feast, called \"good\" by his mother and \"heir\" by his father, conceived by the sky and born of the Duat. The king is born with Orion and will live by the gods' command. He rises with Orion in the east of the sky and sets with him in the west. Their third is Sothis, who leads them along the sky's fine paths in the Field of Reeds.",
  "egy.pt.443": "The speaker tells Nut that two eyes have come out of her head; she has taken Horus and Set with their crowns. She has counted her children and is asked to claim the king for life too, so he does not perish.",
  "egy.pt.444": "The speaker tells Nut that she has risen as king of Lower Egypt, having gained power over the gods with their kas, inheritance, food and possessions. Whoever she keeps will live; if she lives, the king will live.",
  "egy.pt.446": "The speaker tells Osiris the king that his mother Nut has spread herself over him to hide him from all evil. She has guarded him, and he is the greatest of her children.",
  "egy.pt.447": "Whoever goes, goes to his ka: Osiris, Set, Mekhenti-irti and the king have all gone to their kas. The speaker tells the king he will not be in need, for his mother Nut, protector of the great and the fearful, is coming. She protects him, gives back his head, gathers his bones and puts his heart in his body. He will lead those before him and command those after him, make his house prosper and keep his children from sorrow. His purity is that of the gods who have gone to their kas and suffer no hardship.",
  "egy.pt.448": "The speaker asks Thoth to heal the king so he may live and what is against him may end, and to give him the eye of Horus.",
  "egy.pt.449": "The speaker tells Horus, who is in Osiris the king, to take the eye of Horus.",
  "egy.pt.450": "Whoever goes, goes to his ka: Osiris, Set, Mekhenti-irti and the king. The speaker tells the king that he has gone to live and not to die, to be a spirit at the head of the spirits and mighty at the head of the living, mighty and honoured. His mother Nut comes, so he will not be in need. She protects him, gives back his head, gathers his bones and limbs and puts his heart in his body. He will lead and command and protect his children from sorrow. His purity is that of the gods who have gone to their kas.",
  "egy.pt.451": "The speaker tells the king to wake, rise and stand so that he, his ka, his soul and his power may be pure. His mother Nut, the great protector, comes to purify and protect him and keep him from need. He and his ka, power and soul are pure. Geb says his bones are joined and he should take his head; Atum says the evil in him should be wiped away.",
  "egy.pt.452": "The speaker tells the king to stand and be pure with his ka. Horus purifies him in the sky. His purification is that of Shu, Tefnut and the four spirits of the houses, who rejoice in Buto. His mother Nut purifies and protects him. Geb tells him to take his head, for his bones are joined; Atum says the evil in him is wiped out and destroyed.",
  "egy.pt.453": "The speaker tells the king to stand, put on the eye of Horus and let it cling to his flesh, so he can go out in it and the gods see him adorned, and take the great crown among the Ennead of Heliopolis. He is told to live, for the eye of Horus is brought to him and will never leave him.",
  "egy.pt.454": "The speaker tells Osiris the king that he has taken every god, their lands and possessions, in his arms. He is great and curved like the circle that surrounds the islands.",
  "egy.pt.455": "The waterways are full and the canals flooded by the purifying water from Osiris. The speaker calls on the sem-priest, the prince, the great ones of the palace and of Heliopolis and the Great Ennead to sit and watch the king, Osiris, being purified with two kinds of natron. This is the spittle from the mouths of Horus and Set, which purified each of them and poured to the ground the evil the other had done to him. In the same way the king is purified, and the evil that Nwtknw did to him is poured out.",
  "egy.pt.456": "The speaker greets the Great One, son of a Great One. Palace staff run and work for him, the windows of the sky open and the steps of light are shown to him. He is greeted as the sole one who will live for ever; Horus, the long-strider, comes, who wins power over the horizon and the gods. He is greeted as a soul in his red blood, named by his father and the gods, who took his place when the sky was separated from the earth, so he can stride across the sky and through both Egypts. Whoever knows this saying of Ra and uses these charms of Harachte will be Ra's intimate and Harachte's friend. The king knows and uses them, and will be taken up to the sky in Ra's following.",
  "egy.pt.457": "The fields are watered and the canals flooded for the king today, when his spirit and power are given to him. He is told to rise, take his water, gather his bones and stand as a spirit at the head of the spirits. He should rise for bread that cannot mould and beer that cannot sour, which make him strong and pre-eminent, and give some to the one who came before him. He is glorious, and so is his successor.",
  "egy.pt.458": "The text is badly broken. What survives says the keeper stands before the king so that his new-moon, monthly, half-monthly and sixth-day feasts are celebrated. Arms are given to him, the dance comes down to him, and the great mourning post speaks to him; the rest is lost.",
  "egy.pt.459": "The speaker tells the king to take his pure water from Elephantine, his natron from Iru and the Oxyrhynchus nome, and his incense from Nubia. He sits on his firm throne, with a jackal's front and a falcon's back, eating meat from the slaughtering benches of Osiris and Set and the god's bread from the hall. He strikes and directs with his sceptres, commands the gods and takes the arm of the undying stars. He goes up in the Thinite nome and down in the great valley, and is told to rise.",
  "egy.pt.460": "The speaker tells the king that his cool water is the flood that came from him. He should be still and hear what is said: he shall be a spirit at the head of the spirits, mighty at the head of the living, and sit beside Khenti-imentiu. His two cakes come from the hall and his two ribs from the god's bench. He should rise and take the fresh bread and beer sent from his house.",
  "egy.pt.461": "The speaker tells the king that he rises as the morning star and sails as the master of the heavenly ocean; those in Nun fear him and he commands the spirits. Isis and Nephthys mourn him, and the great mourning post strikes away evil, as for Osiris in his suffering. He is warned to guard himself against the great sea. He should sit on his firm throne and command the hidden ones. The sky's doors open so he can row to the Field of Reeds, grow barley and reap spelt, and so secure his living like Horus, son of Atum.",
  "egy.pt.462": "The speaker tells the king, great in waking and great in sleep, that sweetness is too sweet for him. He should rise; he will not die.",
  "egy.pt.463": "The sky's double doors, which hold people back, are open for the king. Mourners lament for him, and the undying stars stand up for him. His air is incense and his north wind is incense smoke. He is great in the Thinite nome, the lone star that rises in the east of the sky and does not give himself up to Horus of the Duat.",
  "egy.pt.464": "The speaker tells the king, very high among the undying stars, that he will never go down.",
  "egy.pt.465": "The king asks the gods of the horizon at the end of the sky, as surely as they want Atum to live and to have their ointment, linen and cakes, to take his arm and place him in the Field of Offerings. Once they have made him a spirit and mighty among the gods, he will prepare a great meal and offering for them. He sails the sky, leads those in the settlements and takes the crown like Horus, son of Atum.",
  "egy.pt.466": "The speaker tells the king that he is the great star, Orion's companion, who crosses the sky with Orion and the Duat with Osiris. He rises in the east of the sky, renewed and young again. Nut bore him with Orion, and the year adorned him with Osiris. Arms, the dance and a meal are given to him, and the great mourning post laments him as for Osiris. He is told to sail on, arrive and protect himself from the great sea.",
  "egy.pt.467": "The speaker reminds Ra that he once wished for a mighty, honoured son with strong arms and a long stride, and says the king is that son. He shines in the East like Ra and goes in the West like Khepri, living on what Horus, lord of the sky, lives on. He purifies Ra, mounts his throne, takes the oar and rows Ra across the sky, a golden star. He flies away from humankind, belonging to the sky, not the earth, rising like a cloud, a heron, a falcon and a swarm of grasshoppers that hides the sun. He has not insulted the king or Bastet, and did no wrong as chief of the chair-bearers. Whatever is done for Ra's son, good or ill, will be done for the king.",
  "egy.pt.468": "The Great One and the king have woken beside their kas after sleeping, and the gods and mighty ones are awake. The king is told to rise; the Great Ennead of Heliopolis has given him his great position at the head of the Ennead, like Geb, Osiris and Horus. Like Anubis who hides his form, he takes a jackal's face. Horus avenges him and satisfies him with offerings at the monthly feasts. Isis and Nephthys mourn him; Horus has avenged him as he avenged Osiris. Osiris lives and the king lives; his name lives among the living. The fear of him is the eye of Horus, the white crown cobra of el-Kab, which puts his fear in all eyes, and the red crown protects him as it protected Horus. The two cobras on his brow lift him and lead him to Nut. He will be a soul like the Souls of Heliopolis, Hierakonpolis and Buto. Thoth then says: a royal offering of bread, beer and two cakes from Horus, to satisfy him for ever.",
  "egy.pt.469": "The king purifies himself, takes his oar and sits in the bow of the Two Enneads' boat, rowing Ra to the West. Ra sets his seat above the lords of kas and writes his name above the living. The double doors in the sky open, and he goes through in his panther-skin kilt with his sceptre in hand. He is whole and content with his name, living with his ka, which drives off evil before and behind him like the throw-sticks of the lord of Letopolis. He sees what the stars do and is happy with them; he is a star indeed, and will never suffer.",
  "egy.pt.470": "The king says he knows and does not forget his mother, the white crown of el-Kab and the red crown of Buto, lady of many lands. He asked her to give him her breast, and she told him to suck it so he would live again and be small again; he would go up to the sky as a falcon with feathers like a goose's. He asks Hedhed to bring the boat, for he is the great wild bull, and asks the bull of offerings to bow its horn and let him pass. Asked where he is going, he says to the sky, with life and joy, to see his father Ra; he is sent to the high places of Set, to the tall sycamore east of the sky where the gods sit. He is the living falcon who has explored the sky, the great helmsman with a long stride. He washes in the Field of Reeds, dresses in Khepri's field and finds Ra there. Wherever Ra goes, east, west or any fine place, he finds the king.",
  "egy.pt.471": "The king says he is a god, son of a god and a god's messenger. He comes to purify himself in the Field of Reeds and goes down to the field of Kenset. The Followers of Horus wash and dry him and recite for him the chapters of the right way and of those who go up for life and joy. He goes up to the sky and boards Ra's boat, commanding the gods who carry him. Every god will rejoice at his coming as they rejoice when Ra rises in the east, in peace.",
  "egy.pt.472": "The sky thunders and the earth trembles before the king, who is a magician full of magic. He comes to glorify Orion, put Osiris at the head and set the gods in their places. He asks Ma-haf, bull of the gods, to bring the boat and set him on the other side for life and joy.",
  "egy.pt.473": "The two reed floats of the sky are set out by the morning and evening boats for Ra and for Horus of the horizon to cross to each other, and they are let down for the king so he can cross to both. He rises on the eastern side of the sky where the gods are born, and is born again like Horus of the horizon. He and his ka are vindicated; his sister is Sothis and his mother the morning star. He finds the spirits, \"well-equipped by their mouths,\" sitting by a lake with their drinking bowls. They ask him whether he has no eyes and how he came to this most sacred place; he answers that he is such a spirit too, and has come. The ferrying is described again. He joins them, walks with them in the Field of Reeds, grazes in the malachite field, and eats, lives, dresses, anoints himself and drinks as they do. He sits like the one in the great palace and commands each spirit.",
  "egy.pt.474": "Isis and Nephthys say how lovely and fortunate it is to see Osiris the king go up to the sky among the undying stars, with his renown on his head, fear of him all around and his magic going ahead. He goes to his mother Nut and climbs her as a ladder. The gods of sky and earth join to carry him, and the Souls of Buto and Hierakonpolis come to him. Geb, having spoken with Atum, says that everything is his: the Field of Reeds and the regions of Horus and Set, and so it was done. The one who said he would kill him has not; the king will kill him and holds his own as the surviving wild bull. Said four times: he remains in life and joy.",
  "egy.pt.475": "The king addresses the ferryman who brought the boat to Horus to get his eye back and to Set to get his testicles back. The eye of Horus leapt up and fell on the eastern side of the sky; he asks whether the ferryman will leap with it. The king goes to serve Ra as a courtier in place of the gods who have gone to their kas. He has come and risen to the heights of the sky for life and joy, and the palace of the Great Ones does not keep him from the stars' path. The morning boat calls him, and he bails it out. Ra makes him lord of life and joy.",
  "egy.pt.476": "The sky purifies itself for Ra and the earth for Horus, and every god between them purifies the king, who worships the god. He asks the keeper of the way at the great gate to vouch for him to the two great gods, for he is the plant, son of Ra, that holds up the sky, rules the earth and will judge the gods. He will sit among the stars of the Duat, who will carry him like Ra, serve him like Horus, raise him like Wepwawet and love him like Min. He tells the scribe to break his writing kit, pens and papyrus rolls, and asks Ra to remove the scribe from his post and put the king in his place for ever, happy with the staff of office.",
  "egy.pt.477": "The sky shakes and the earth quakes. Horus and Thoth come and raise Osiris from his side and set him among the Two Enneads. Set is told to remember Geb's words and the gods' threat in Heliopolis, because he struck Osiris down. When Set denied it and said Osiris had defied him and come too close, Osiris's names, including Orion, arose from that. Osiris is told to rise; Set has risen after hearing the gods' threat. Isis and Nephthys hold his arm and hand as he walks between them. Atum gives him the sky, the earth, the Field of Reeds, the regions of Horus and Set, the cities and the nomes. Thoth is told to sharpen his knife to cut off the heads and hearts of anyone who blocks the king's way to Osiris. The king comes to Osiris, lord of the sky, again and again to wipe his face, dress him as a god, serve as his priest, cut up his enemy, and fix his hands on the sceptres as Geb ordered. Sothis, his loving daughter, leads the king to him. Horus has not put the king among the dead but among the gods; what Horus did for Osiris he has done for the king.",
  "egy.pt.478": "The speaker greets the Ladder of the god and the Ladder of Set and tells them to stand up, along with Horus's ladder made for Osiris to climb to the sky and serve Ra. He reminds Geb that he came looking for his brother Osiris after Set threw him down, and Horus came with his helmet on, facing his father Geb. The king is Horus, Geb's son; as Geb gave the ladders to the god, he should give them to the king so he can climb to the sky and serve Ra like the gods who have gone to their kas. The eye of Horus is on Thoth's wing beside the ladder; the king is that eye, a cobra going to the sky. The gods are asked to welcome him as Horus welcomed back his eye. Any spirit or god who raises an arm against him will have no hoed earth, no offering and no meals in Heliopolis. Any who open their arms to him will be on the ladder. His bones and limbs are joined, and he has sprung up to the sky on the fingers of the lord of the ladder.",
  "egy.pt.479": "This spell says the double doors of heaven stand open for Horus of the gods, Horus of the East, Horus of the Šsm.t-land, Osiris and the king, so each can rise at dawn and wash in the Marsh of Reeds. It then repeats that each of them has indeed risen and been purified there. Rē‘ is asked to make Nut's body pregnant with the spirit; the earth will rise under the king's feet, Tefnut will take his arm, Seker will purify him and Rē‘ will give him his hand. The king will rank above the leader of the Ennead and takes his place in the sky, calling on Hnni and ’Ipȝti to take him with them, alive for ever.",
  "egy.pt.480": "The speaker says how beautiful and uplifting it is to see the king rise to heaven as his father Atum did, with his fame, magic and terror around him, after Atum has gathered the cities and districts for him; Geb, prince of the gods, said so. The regions of Horus and Set and the Marsh of Reeds praise him under the names of several gods. A ladder is brought, set up and raised for him, and those who climb come; the king climbs up on the hips of Isis and Nephthys. Atum takes his arm and makes him chief of the tireless, imperishable gods. The gods had said he would never lead them again, but he does, like the surviving bull among the wild bulls.",
  "egy.pt.481": "The king calls on the ferryman \"Look-out, His-face-behind-him\" to carry him across. The two reed-floats of heaven, once laid down for Rē‘, are laid down for the king so he can cross to Rē‘ at the horizon. He will go to his place in the north-eastern sky among the imperishable stars, who stand on their sceptres, and stand among them. The moon is his brother and the morning star his mother. He asks to be given an arm so that he may live.",
  "egy.pt.482": "The speaker, as the king's son, tells his father, Osiris the king, to turn from his left side onto his right and face the fresh water and warm bread he has brought. The doors of heaven are open for him. The gods and Souls of Buto, moved by the wailing of Isis and Nephthys, dance and mourn for him, beating themselves and tearing their hair, and tell Osiris to get up and see what his son Horus has done: he has struck and bound the one who struck and bound him, and his great sister gathered his body after finding him on the shore of Ndi.t. The gods are asked to take him in; he will rise to heaven as Wp.wȝ.wt, led by Horus, and be given heaven, earth and the Marsh of Reeds.",
  "egy.pt.483": "The libation has been poured; Wp.wȝ.wt and Horus are awake. The king, as Osiris, eldest son of Geb, before whom the Great Ennead trembles, is told to rise; he will be purified at the new month and feasted at the year's beginnings, and mourned as the one at Abydos. Geb has declared him a god, the earth announces that the gateway is open, and Anubis speaks his rank. He goes up to his firm throne in heaven, crosses the Winding Watercourse, is called by Rē‘ and befriended by Set. Ddwn gives him incense, he has plenty of green plants, his scattered limbs are gathered, and trees bow to him as he circles the sky.",
  "egy.pt.484": "The text is badly broken. What survives has the king as the Great One who has gone up to heaven, and Nut saying her son has come in peace, unharmed, and that she will not let him fall or leave her. The king is the mound rising from the sea, whose hand no one on earth has grasped, and Shu bends the earth under his feet. Someone separates him from one brother and joins him to another. He lives on natron offerings and on the loaf behind the god, and the gods are told twice that it is he who has broken the order.",
  "egy.pt.485": "Much of this spell is lost. What survives says the doors of heaven are open, and any god who takes the king up to the house of Horus in heaven will be given oxen and meat, while any god who refuses will get no respect, clothing or cake and will not go up on the day of judgement. The king comes to Rē‘, the golden calf of heaven, and asks Horus not to leave him without a boat, and Geb to give him an arm up to his mother Nut. Geb, in a lion-helmet and with angry eyes, searches foreign lands, finds Osiris lying on his side and tells him to stand and be protected against Set. Someone says they have bound Set. Horus and Nut are asked to lift the king and rejoin his bones, so that every limb of his is a god's as he rises as the great star in the east.",
  "egy.pt.486": "The king greets the waters brought by Shu, in which Geb bathed. He says he was born in Nun before sky, earth, the sky's supports, unrest and fear existed. He belongs to the great company born first in Heliopolis, who are never seized, condemned or executed; no enemy has won against him, he has not grown poor, his nails are not long and none of his bones are broken. If he goes down into water, Osiris lifts him; if into the earth, Geb lifts him; and Rē‘ gives him an arm to wherever a god should be.",
  "egy.pt.487": "The son says to his father, Osiris the king, that he has become a spirit on the horizon and lasts for ever, giving commands as head of the living. He tells him to turn from his left side to his right and take the bread his son and heir is giving him.",
  "egy.pt.488": "The speaker tells the king that Horus has given him freedom to move and he shines as the only star in the sky. His wings have grown like a falcon's and he is broad-chested, a falcon seen descending after crossing the sky. He sails the sky by Rē‘-Harachte's waterway and Nut gives him her arm; the rest is broken.",
  "egy.pt.489": "Only fragments survive. They mention someone wishing the king to speak, someone \"who sees the tribute of the land\", and the words \"see\" and \"order\".",
  "egy.pt.490": "Only a few words survive: the king is called the bull-herd, and someone is told \"you two have given birth to…\"; the rest is lost.",
  "egy.pt.491": "The text is badly broken. It says that when the king dies his ka will gain power; something descends into the earth as two serpents and the speaker goes down on their coils. The king knelt in Nun and sat somewhere now lost. Horus gives him the bread he fed his followers with, and he eats it with them.",
  "egy.pt.492": "Only the opening words \"To say:\" survive; the rest of the spell is lost.",
  "egy.pt.493": "The king greets those who look after food and rule the green field near the lord of splendour, and asks them to let him eat the grain grown there. Those in charge of the offerings of the oldest gods lead him to plenty, so he may eat with his mouth and pass waste like Śerḳet. He gives out food like the long-winged one in the Marsh of Reeds. He has breath in his nose and seed in his phallus like the mysterious one. He sees Nun appear, will be honoured and great, there will be a six-day feast, and he will eat of the pregnant cow like those in Heliopolis.",
  "egy.pt.494": "Rē‘ sat down to eat bread and the Two Enneads gave water; the flood stood at the bank. The king says to the Flood that he has come so it will give him bread when he is hungry and beer when he is thirsty.",
  "egy.pt.495": "The king addresses the Great Ennead in Heliopolis, asking that his meal be like that of the chief of the palace, with two of his meals in the Ddi.t and three on the horizon. The end is broken.",
  "egy.pt.496": "The king greets Food, Abundance, Corn and Flour, and the gods who set Rē‘'s meal before him, and says he will eat Rē‘'s portion seated on the throne of splendour. He says he is she of Tentyra and comes from Tentyra; Shu is behind him, Tefnut in front and Wp-wȝ.wt guards his right. They make Rē‘'s field of food keep him alive so he can eat once it is gathered for him.",
  "egy.pt.497": "The king is told to get up, sit, and shake off the dust; to remove the arms from behind him as Set did. The eye of Horus will come to him at the start of the ten-day week because he longs for it. The rest is lost.",
  "egy.pt.498": "The speaker tells Osiris the king to wake, get up, sit and shake off the dust. He brings him the eye of Horus, which will stay with him. Stand up for this cut of meat from the hall and take your bread from my hand, he says; I am your son, born of you. The text breaks off.",
  "egy.pt.499": "A short spell against a snake: it tells the spittle that has not been let fall to get back. The serpent is lying stretched out; the speaker tells himself to guard, stand firm and strike.",
  "egy.pt.500": "A short protective spell: it says a heart is there, and one who will shut himself in. It orders the great hidden one, come out of a hidden body part, to get back. A man sees it; guard yourself and block the way.",
  "egy.pt.501": "Much is lost. The speaker asks for three meals, one in heaven and two on earth; a lion-helmet and something green are mentioned.",
  "egy.pt.502": "Almost nothing survives: only the scattered words \"four\", \"a point\", \"darkness\", \"be not\" and \"come\".",
  "egy.pt.503": "The king says the doors of heaven and earth, the windows of the sky and the steps of Nun are open, and the steps of light are revealed by the everlasting one. He says this as he goes up to heaven to anoint himself with the best oil, dress in the best linen and sit on the throne of \"Truth which makes alive\". He will be beside the imperishable stars of the north, never setting, never tiring, never pulled out of the water. If Mnt.w rises high or hurries away, he will do the same.",
  "egy.pt.504": "The king says the sky is pregnant with the juice of the vine and Nut has given birth to her daughter, the morning star; he rises too, with Sothis as the third. He has washed in the lakes of the singers and of the jackal and tells the thorn-bush to get out of his way to the south side of the Marsh of Reeds. The canal is open and the Winding Watercourse is flooded. The two reed-floats of heaven are laid down for several forms of Horus so they can cross to Rē‘ at the horizon, and he asks that they be laid down for him too, as Horus of the gods, so he can take his throne in the Marsh of Reeds. He calls himself a Great One, son of a Great One, born of the Two Enneads, who has worshipped Rē‘ and Horus so that they will be kind to him.",
  "egy.pt.505": "The king says he comes from Buto dressed in the finery of Horus and the clothes of Thot, with Isis before him and Nephthys behind. Wp-wȝ-wt opens the way, Shu lifts him, the Souls of Heliopolis build him a stairway to the top, and Nut gives him her arm as she did for Osiris. He asks the ferryman whose face is behind him to take him to the Marsh of Reeds, saying he comes from ȝwȝr.t with the uraeus-serpent as his companion. His four spirits, Ḥpi, Dwȝ-mw-t-f, ’Imś.ti and Ḳbḥ-śn.w.f, will row, two on each side, and he will steer. He finds the Two Enneads, who give him their arm; he sits between them to judge and gives orders to those he finds there.",
  "egy.pt.506": "The king lists a string of names and things he says he is: St.ti, a lake, Swnt the chest of heaven, a spirit-king of Lower Egypt, the hidden ’Imn of this land, Praise, Appearance, a two-faced Hathor symbol, the four sons Hpi, Dwȝ-mu.t.f, ’Imś.ti and Ḳbḥ-śn.w.f, the one who spreads the wings, and a living soul who has freed himself by halting the actions of those who would act against him. He does good to those who do good, his lips are the Two Enneads, he is the great spoken word, and he has been delivered from every evil. Finally he asks men and gods to put their arms under him and raise him to heaven, as Shu lifted the sky, to the great seat among the gods.",
  "egy.pt.507": "’Iḥmti is told to announce to everyone that the canal is open, the Marsh of Reeds and the Winding Watercourse are flooded, and the reed-floats are laid down so Horus and Rē‘ can cross to each other. ’Iḥmti then commends the king to his father the moon, his mother the morning star, and the four black-haired youths who sit on the eastern side of the sky in the shade of the fortress Ḳȝti. The spell ends by saying the king's father is great, and the king is great like him.",
  "egy.pt.508": "The king rises to heaven in place of Rē‘, and the goddesses of Buto and el-Kâb rejoice. He uses her splendour as steps to climb to his mother, the living uraeus on Rē‘'s head, who pities him and offers him her breast. Heaven speaks, earth quakes and the gods of Heliopolis tremble at the offering made before him. The goddesses of Bubastis, el-Kâb and Buto have nursed, raised and helped him. He arrives to feast on figs and wine from the divine vineyard, with his cook beside him, smelling sweet like Horus. Geb takes his arm and leads him through the gates of heaven; Satis washes him with four jars from Elephantine. Asked where he comes from, he says he comes to the Ennead in heaven and on earth for bread, to a boat, and to his two vulture mothers, who give him their breasts and never wean him.",
  "egy.pt.509": "Heaven speaks, the earth quakes and Geb trembles as the offering is made and the king goes up to heaven, crossing the sky and the foaming sea and breaking the walls of Shu. He rises with wingtips like a great bird, his entrails washed by Anubis and the embalming of Osiris done at Abydos. Among the imperishable stars, with Sothis as his sister and the morning star as his guide, he is led to the Marsh of Offerings. He sits on a firm throne with lion knobs and bull's-hoof feet, sceptre in hand; the gods come to him bowing, and the Two Enneads call him a prince of princes and place him among the gods.",
  "egy.pt.510": "The speaker says it is not the king but Osiris, his son Horus, who asks to see the god in his new form; when he speaks of \"statues\" and \"his beloved son\", they ferry Horus across. The doors of heaven are open at daybreak for several forms of Horus and for the king to go down and purify himself in the Marsh of Reeds. He becomes king, crosses the waters, and ’Imt.t takes his arm, because he is a pure priest, son of a priest. He is washed with four jars from the divine lake and dried by Isis. The priest of Rē‘ tells the door-keeper to let him in, the four gods of the lake declare him just to Geb and Rē‘, and Geb announces him to Rē‘. He leads the gods, steers the divine boat and takes hold of heaven and its stars; gods bow, spirits lay down their weapons. He calls himself a wild bull, the rain, a many-coiled serpent, the scribe of the divine book who makes things exist, and the eye of Horus, stronger than men and gods. Horus carries him and Set lifts him up, and he satisfies both gods.",
  "egy.pt.511": "Geb laughs and Nut smiles as the king goes up to heaven; the sky rejoices, the earth shakes, the storm roars and he roars like Set. The doors of heaven are opened for him; he stands on Shu and holds up the stars. He crosses the sky with Sothis, having washed in the lakes of the Dwȝ.t, and a cow guides him to the great seat the gods made. Isis will conceive him and Nephthys bear him, and he will sit on the great throne while gods and stars come bowing. He holds up the sky with his right arm and the earth with his left, breathes the wind of life and thrives among the gods. He meets the door-keeper of Osiris and dislikes crossing without paying. He leaves his record among men and love among gods, saying \"Speak what is true; a god hates a lying word.\" The god is told: let him be tested, do not speak against him; he is your son and heir.",
  "egy.pt.512": "The speaker says his father made himself a new heart after the old one was taken from him as he rose to heaven and crossed the Winding Watercourse. Anubis comes to meet him and Geb gives him his arm. The king is told to get up, take his four jars, and purify himself in the Lake of the jackal, with incense in the Lake of the Dȝ.t, and before his bush in the Marsh of Reeds. He is to sail the sky, live in the Marsh of Offerings among the gods, sit on his firm throne with mace and sceptre, lead those in Nun, command the gods, and travel his ocean like Rē‘. The spell ends: lift yourself up and hurry to your spirit.",
  "egy.pt.513": "When the father-king goes up to heaven among the gods, he hears the words of the blessed dead. Rē‘ finds him on the shores of the sky and gives him his arm, while the gods say \"He comes who should come\" and \"He comes who knows his place.\" He is told to take his seat in Rē‘'s boat and sail with the tireless, imperishable stars, receive the tribute of the evening boat, become a spirit in the Dȝ.t and live the sweet life of the lord of the horizon. The gods following Atum ask who did this for him; the answer is that someone greater, north of the ocean of Nut, heard his plea and received him before the Great Ennead.",
  "egy.pt.514": "The text is badly broken. It mentions Nḫi and a serpent, someone before Letopolis with his living ones at his neck, and says twice that a place is for a son. Geb has called someone; the rest is lost.",
  "egy.pt.515": "The king asks the two legs of Horus and two wings of Thot to ferry him over and not leave him without a boat, and asks for everlasting bread and beer. He stands by the two obelisks of Rē‘ on earth and his two holy signs in heaven, and travels on the reed-floats with the libation jar of Rē‘. When he reaches the field of life where Rē‘ is born, he meets Ḳbḥ.wt, daughter of Anubis, who refreshes his heart with her four jars, purifies him and censes him. He takes his food from the Great God's granary, is clothed in imperishable stars and presides over the two palaces.",
  "egy.pt.516": "The king speaks to Nwrw, ferryman of the marsh of Pȝ‘t, saying he is the herdsman of the ferryman's cattle and his potter on earth, who will break the jar, the child of Nut. He has brought the ferryman a house he made for him on the night of his birth, and it is a jar. He calls him Bes, who knows neither father nor mother, and asks to be ferried quickly to the field where the gods were begotten and where they celebrate their New Year days.",
  "egy.pt.517": "The king addresses the ferryman of the Marsh of Reeds, who carries the just across. He says he is just before heaven, earth and the island between Nut's thighs that he has swum to. He is a pygmy, a dancer of the god who delights him before his great throne, and the ferryman has heard in houses and streets that he was summoned to life and judgement. The two on the Great God's throne call him to life and joy. So he asks to be ferried to the field, the Great God's fine seat, where the honoured dead are given food and sent fowling, and says he is one of them.",
  "egy.pt.518": "The king asks ’Iw, ferryman of the Marsh of Offerings, to bring the boat. He says he is the son of the Morning Boat, Osiris's yearly messenger, bringing a message from Geb that the year's harvest is good. He has gone down with the Two Enneads and is the measuring line that lays out the Marsh of Offerings. He found the gods standing in their robes and white sandals, and they threw them off, saying their hearts were not glad until he came. Osiris is asked to stand and commend him to those north of the Marsh of Offerings, as he once commended Horus to Isis, so that they give him food in the fields and drink at the springs.",
  "egy.pt.519": "The king tells Ḥr.f-ḥȝ.f, Osiris's doorkeeper, that Osiris has ordered his boat be brought so the king can cross with a woven green band, an eye of Horus, to bandage Osiris's injured finger. The doors of heaven and the lower region are open, the pools and marshes are flooded, and the Two Enneads are asked to take him to the Marsh of Offerings, where he holds the sceptres and leads Rē‘'s servants. The four long-haired youths of the east prepare the reed-floats for Rē‘ and for him. He greets the morning star and asks for the two fingers it once gave to Nfr.t, saying he is a son of Khepri. He asks it to take up its harpoon, whose points are sun-rays, with which he cuts off the heads of enemies. The children of Nut come down with garlands, and the king went to the great island where the swallows, the imperishable stars, alight and give him the tree of life. He asks the morning star to ferry him to its great field of food, let him eat and drink what it does, seat him for his truth, raise him as Horus took his father's house from Set, and make him a prince among the northern stars.",
  "egy.pt.520": "The king addresses four beings with curls on their foreheads, temples and backs of their heads and braids in the middle, and tells them to bring the boat so Ḥḳrr and Sees-behind-him can ferry him to the imperishable stars. If they do not ferry him at once, he says, he will tell their name to the \"wicked men\" he knows and pluck out their braids like lotus flowers from a pond.",
  "egy.pt.521": "The spell calls the messenger who crosses the sea a gander, a goose and a bull that bring themselves. The king flies like a cloud or a heron and goes off to his fathers at Pdw-š, taking bread that cannot go mouldy and beer that cannot go sour. He eats his bread alone, gives none to anyone behind him, and saves it from the knm.t-bird.",
  "egy.pt.522": "The king tells the ferryman Sees-behind-him that he has come to life bringing the eye of Horus, and asks for the boat called the \"work of Khnum\"; he asks the four sons Ḥpi, ’Imś.ti, Dwȝ-mut.f and Ḳbḥ-śn.w.f to bring it from the Winding Watercourse. He asks the devourer, the ḳrr-serpent and Nekhbet to open the way. He greets the good one in peace and asks for love in return, and tells the evil one that if it keeps away from him, he will keep away from it.",
  "egy.pt.523": "The sky has made the light stronger so the king can rise to heaven as the eye of Rē‘ and stand by the left eye of Horus, where the gods' words are heard. He will stand among the spirits, the imperishable stars, as Horus stood among the living and Osiris stands among the spirits.",
  "egy.pt.524": "The king is pure with the cleansing Horus gave his eye; he is Thot, who avenges the eye, not Set, who seized it, and the gods are told to rejoice. Crowned with the white crown, the eye of Horus, he has a jackal's face, a falcon's arms and wings like Thot's, and asks Geb to let him fly to heaven. He has crossed the borders of the dead and passed Osiris's messengers; no god can hold him. He is the son of Khnum and has done no evil, and asks Rē‘ to open his way. He searched for the eye at Buto, found it at Heliopolis and snatched it from Set's head where they fought. Horus is asked to give him his arm and take back his eye, which comes with the king for ever.",
  "egy.pt.525": "Rē‘ purified himself and Horus adorned himself so that blindness and sleeplessness would end, before any god existed. The king goes down into the lake of Kns.t and is washed in the Marsh of Reeds by the Followers of Horus, who recite the chapters \"of those who ascend\" and \"of those who raise themselves up\". He is told to board the boat of Rē‘ rowed by the gods, who rejoice at his rising as they do when Rē‘ rises in the east.",
  "egy.pt.526": "The king has washed in the Lake of Reeds where Rē‘ was washed. Horus dries his back and legs. Shu is asked to take him to heaven and Nut to give him her arm.",
  "egy.pt.527": "Atum created in Heliopolis by masturbating: he took his phallus in his fist to arouse himself, and the twins Shu and Tefnut were born. They place the king between them, among the gods in the Marsh of Offerings. The spell is to be said four times: the king goes up to heaven and down to earth, for everlasting life.",
  "egy.pt.528": "The king asks Swnt, who crosses the sky nine times a night, to take his arm and ferry him over the sea. He gets into the god's boat, rowed by the Ennead. The \"chapter of Bdw\" and the \"chapter of natron\" are recited for him, incense stands before the Great Ennead and Bdw sits in the great palace.",
  "egy.pt.529": "The doorkeeper of heaven is told to notice this ascending messenger of a god: when he leaves by the western gate of the sky, bring him to the southern gate; when he rises by the eastern gate, bring him to the northern one.",
  "egy.pt.530": "The king greets the ladder set up by the Souls of Buto and Nekhen and asks it to give him its arm, so he can sit between the two great gods, take a leading place, be helped as far as the Marsh of Offerings, and sit among the stars in the sky.",
  "egy.pt.531": "The king calls on the two kites on Thot's wing, Whnnw.ti and Dndnw.ti, to carry him to the other side. He comes for life as the swift messenger of Horus.",
  "egy.pt.532": "The spell addresses the mooring-post of the morning boat. Isis and Nephthys come, one as a bird and one as a kite, and find Osiris where his brother Set struck him down in Ndi.t. Through his names Anubis, jackal of the South and Ḥr-hȝ.ti, they stop his body from rotting, leaking or smelling, and they keep several forms of Horus from rotting; Set will never escape carrying him. Osiris the king, Geb's eldest, is told to wake for Horus and stand against Set. The New Moon is celebrated for him; he travels to the sea, is a spirit on the horizon and lasts in Mendes; the Souls of Heliopolis and Rē‘ take his arm, the Two Enneads raise his head and make him chief of the palaces. He is told: you live, you live, get up.",
  "egy.pt.533": "Most of the text is lost. What survives says the king is the blood that comes from Rē‘ and the sweat that comes from Isis.",
  "egy.pt.534": "Horus speaks, asking Geb to make an offering, and drives away anyone protected by Horus, Set, Osiris, Isis, Nephthys, Thot and other gods. He says he has dedicated this house to the king, a hall purer than the sky, with an obelisk at its door and double doors sealed with two evil eyes. He then forbids Osiris, Horus, Set, Mḫnti-’irti, Thot, Isis and Nephthys, and any enemies, from entering if they come with ill intent: the king is told not to open his arms to them, each is called by an insulting name and sent off to some distant place. But if the king comes with his ka, he is to be welcomed, free to go down to the gods or up to heaven. The speaker consecrates the pyramid and temple and all it holds to the king and his ka. Anyone who lays a finger on it will have Isis and Nephthys against him, be judged by the Ennead, be left without support and be cursed as one who eats his own body. A few words are lost.",
  "egy.pt.535": "Isis and Nephthys speak. They come as two birds, a ḥȝ.t-bird and a kite, searching for their brother Osiris, the king, and weep for him: Isis sits with her hands on her head and Nephthys holds her breasts in grief, while Anubis lies on his belly and Osiris is wounded. They declare that the king has no rot, sweat, leakage or dust. The stars follow Ḳbḥ.wt, chief of his attendants. He is told to free Horus to punish Set's followers, cut off their heads and legs, take out and count their hearts as \"Anubis counter of hearts\", and drink their blood. His eyes are his two serpents and he is like Wepwawet and Anubis. The great houses of Heliopolis make him first; spirits, stars and the dead fear and honour him. The Souls of Heliopolis give him life, and he is told to come and live his life there in peaceful years.",
  "egy.pt.536": "The king is told his water and plenty are his, flowing from Osiris, and the doors of heaven and Nut are open to him. Isis says \"Welcome\" and Nephthys \"Come in peace\" when they see their brother. He is told to get up, untie his bandages, shake off the dust and sit on his firm throne, purified with jars from the natron lake given by Horus of Nekhen, who also gives him his jackal spirits. Anubis orders him to enter as the morning star and pass through the regions of Horus of the South and North, and men will build him a stairway with their arms. He comes to Geb, who is asked to do for him what was done for Osiris at the flood-time feast, when his bones were counted, his sandals mended and his nails cleaned, while people from both palaces come bowing.",
  "egy.pt.537": "The king is told to rise and sit on the throne of Osiris, his body complete like Atum's and his face like a jackal's. Rē‘ praises his words. He will not cease or perish. Nut takes hold of him, Geb takes his arm and his fathers welcome him. He has his body again; he rises as Horus of the Dȝ.t, chief of the imperishable stars, sits on his firm throne by his canal in the sky, and lives like the beetle and endures like the dd-pillar for ever.",
  "egy.pt.538": "A short spell telling a lowing ox to get back: its head is in Horus's hand, its tail in Isis's hand, and Atum's fingers are on its horns.",
  "egy.pt.539": "The king's body is matched, part by part, with gods and sacred things as he rises to the sky, the refrain repeating \"when he ascends and lifts himself to the sky\": his head like a vulture's, his face like Wepwawet's, his nose like Thot's, his tongue like truth in the boat of truth, his heart like Bastet's, his belly like Nut's, his phallus like Ḥapi's, his feet like the sun's morning boats, and so on. A few parts are broken. He is a god's son, beloved of Rē‘, begotten, conceived and born of Rē‘, with magic in his body; he is the great sceptre in Heliopolis and the child Horus. Any god who does not build him a stairway will get no cake, shade, washing or meat and no offerings; the king says it is not he but magic that says this. Any god who does build it, or gives up his seat in the boat, will get offerings, and any god who takes his arm to the house of Horus will be justified before Geb.",
  "egy.pt.540": "The king comes to his father Osiris, bringing him his ka. Several gods open Osiris's mouth, Horus doing so with his little finger, as he did for his father. The king says he is Horus, the beloved son. Osiris is purified and dried, and the king brings him a thousand alabaster vessels and a thousand garments to clothe him.",
  "egy.pt.541": "The four children of Horus, Ḥȝpi, Dwȝ-mu.t.f, ’Imś.ti and Ḳbḥ-śn.w.f, are told to guard the life of their father, Osiris the king, among the gods, and to strike Set and protect the king from him before dawn. Horus is strong and will avenge his father, and the father has made them honour him.",
  "egy.pt.542": "Horus has come to claim his father, Osiris the king, and has issued a royal death decree in the places of Anubis. Thot is told to spare none of those who wronged the king and to hurry and announce their sentence.",
  "egy.pt.543": "Osiris the king is told that the one who killed him has been brought to him; he must not let him escape, but execute him and cut him into three.",
  "egy.pt.544": "The children of Horus are told to go to Osiris the king, hurry, get underneath him, none of them holding back, and carry him.",
  "egy.pt.545": "Osiris the king is told his killer has been brought, to cut him up and execute him. The four children of Horus are told to carry and lead their father, and he is told to endure, open his mouth and stand up.",
  "egy.pt.546": "Nut speaks: she asks for Osiris the king to be brought and given to her so she can embrace him.",
  "egy.pt.547": "The son says to his father, Osiris the king, that he is coming to him, and asks him to come near.",
  "egy.pt.548": "The earth opens its mouth for Osiris the king, and Geb says he is great as a king and mighty as Rē‘; the Two Enneads say \"Come in peace\". The eastern door of heaven opens for him. Great Nut, long-horned and full-breasted, takes him in her arms, nurses him without weaning him, and takes him to heaven instead of throwing him down. He boards the boat like Rē‘ and steers towards the Marsh of Reeds, with Rē‘ taking his arm, Atum raising his head, and Isis and Nephthys holding the boat's ropes. Ḳbḥ.w.t sets him among the herdsmen of the calves.",
  "egy.pt.549": "A short spell telling Bȝbwi, the red-eared one with coloured hindquarters, to get back and to pass the cut of meat over its own mouth.",
  "egy.pt.550": "A short spell telling Km-wr to get back and slide away in Babylon (Hri-‘ḥȝ), where others fell.",
  "egy.pt.551": "The speaker tells a barrier to open as it tilts, orders Rw-ḥȝ.t and Pḥ.wi to get back, and asks to be let pass as a god passes. Part of the text is unclear.",
  "egy.pt.552": "The king says he is alive for ever.",
  "egy.pt.553": "Geb has raised the king and his spirit is kept safe; his jar remains. He is greater than Shu and Tefnut, a spirit nursed at Nephthys's left breast; Osiris gives him the spirits and the eye of Horus. Four ways before Horus's grave lead to the god at sunset; Seker purifies him and leads him to his throne in the sky. He is told to rise, sit and eat bread and beer with his ka for ever, to go as Osiris's representative to his feasts, and to cross like a great bull to the green fields of Rē‘. His water and plenty are his; all the doors of heaven, sky and tomb are open, and Isis and Nephthys greet him. He is to shake off the dust, wipe his face and loosen his bandages, which are really Nephthys's locks, travel south and north, and sit on his firm throne, purified by four jars. Sky and earth weep and tremble and mourners wail as he rises as the morning star. He comes to Geb to sit on his father's lap and be treated as Osiris was when his bones were gathered, while both palaces come to greet him.",
  "egy.pt.554": "The king is the son of the great wild cow, who conceived and bore him, keeps him under her wing and carries him over the lake and canal. He has the headband of a household chief and his sceptre in hand to strike and rule, as befits the honoured lords, for he is one of the Followers of Rē‘ behind the morning star. No evil is to come to him or to his name.",
  "egy.pt.555": "The king comes from Buto to the gods of Buto, adorned as a falcon. He rises as king like Wepwawet, having taken the white and green crowns, with mace and sceptre. Isis is his mother, Nephthys his nurse, Śḫȝ.t-ḥr suckles him, with Neit behind and Śrḳt-ḥtw before. Boats are tied up on the south shore of the Winding Watercourse for him, the hungry, thirsty son of Atum, and Thot is asked to carry him on the tip of his wing to the north shore. He is well, body and clothes sound, as he rises to heaven like Montu.",
  "egy.pt.556": "The text has many gaps. What survives says the father, Osiris the king, is raised high like Wepwawet and told, as Anubis, to get up on jackal's feet and arms. Someone rows before him and brings him the double crown. The Winding Watercourse is flooded, and he calls on Ḥm and Śmti to ferry him to the eastern sky, where the gods are born, at this hour on the third day, so he can stand there like the star under the belly of the sky. The four gods on the sceptres of heaven are told that he has not really died but has become a glorified spirit and has come to them. The end is lost.",
  "egy.pt.557": "Most of the text is lost. What survives tells the king to go back to his house: his heir is on his throne and is growing barley for him.",
  "egy.pt.558": "The king is greeted as Ḥḥ. Km-wr sets his course, he lands as the eldest god lands, and the one with long curls burns incense for him in Heliopolis. He is told he lives and is content, spreading life behind him as he goes.",
  "egy.pt.559": "Osiris says to the king, twice, \"Come in peace.\" The marshes and riverbanks are flooded for him because of the royal offering. The chief of the west takes his arm at the edge of mount ḫb.t. Osiris is to be rewarded for placing him among the princes as a support.",
  "egy.pt.560": "The ground is hoed, the offering is made and the earth of Tbi is broken, and the god's two districts shout before the king as he goes down into the earth. Geb is told to open his mouth for his son Osiris: behind him is food, and before him is the snaring of birds.",
  "egy.pt.561": "Almost all the text is lost. Only a few words survive: \"command\", \"of the boat of the evening\" and \"Thy face is like\".",
  "egy.pt.562": "The spell tells Tefnut that her arms hold the earth high under the sky, and asks her to take the king's hands for life and eternity and set him somewhere, as a distinguished one (part is lost). He sits as chief of the Two Enneads and judges the gods as king and deputy of Horus, who avenged Osiris. His body is like a god's, and the gods' bodies like his. He comes in peace to Horus, whose eye is kept fresh with them and not given over to Set's anger.",
  "egy.pt.563": "The double doors of heaven are open for Horus of the Gods, Horus of the Šsm.t-land, Horus of the East and Horus of the Horizon, and for the king each time, so each can go up and purify himself in the Marsh of Reeds; the spell then repeats that each did go up. The king is pure, has put on his garment and goes up to heaven, lasting like the earth. Nut is told the king is the divine seed in her and to receive him as she received her divine son. Hpȝt and Hnni are asked to take him with them, and Ḥftn.t, mother of the gods, to take his hand and draw him to heaven as she drew Osiris.",
  "egy.pt.564": "Rē‘, Shu and the king all purify themselves in the sea of reeds. Shu is asked to lift the king up to heaven and Nut to give him her arms, so he may fly, rejoicing.",
  "egy.pt.565": "The king is purified as he comes to heaven; he outlasts men and rose with Rē‘ at dawn. Two companions are with him, one behind giving water and one in front giving sand. He leans on Shu's arms as Rē‘ does, and finds the two spirits who rule this land. Nut rejoices, Npnp.t and others receive him and give birth to him, and he is freed from the evil in him. Nephthys gives him her breast, Dwȝ-wr shaves him and Sothis washes his hands on the day of his birth. He does not remember his first mother; it is Nut who bore him, with Osiris.",
  "egy.pt.566": "Horus is asked to take the king away and Thot to carry him on the tip of his wing, like Seker in his boat. Horus does not spend the night stuck behind the canal, Thot is not without a boat, and neither is the king, for he has the eye of Horus.",
  "egy.pt.567": "Rē‘, Horus and the king are purified in the Marsh of Reeds, so the king can rise with him. Nut is asked to give him her arm, and the spell ends: rejoice, he flies, he flies!",
  "egy.pt.568": "The king, like Mḫnti-’irti, has gone to his ka in heaven. A ladder called \"That which mounts to heaven\" is made for him to climb, his boat is brought by the imperishable stars, and the bull of heaven lowers its horn so he can cross on it to the lakes of the Dȝ.t. He will not fall to the ground: he grips the two sycamores in the middle of the far sky, which carry him across and set him on its eastern side.",
  "egy.pt.569": "The king tells the god he knows and will not forget his name, \"Limitless\"; his father's name is \"Thou art great\" and his mother, \"Satisfaction\", gives birth to him every morning. He warns that if the god stops him coming to where the god is, then the births of Limitless, Śerḳet, Śȝḥ, Sothis and Wepwawet will be stopped, Horus will lose his two regions, the god's crew of stars will not row him, and men will no longer come to the king, die, or eat bread. He says he is Śkśn, Rē‘'s messenger, and will not be kept out of heaven; the tree at heaven's door has stretched out its arms to him and the ferryman is with him, for he is one of the gods. He comes to Rē‘ to row him and serve him as a courtier, loving him with body and heart.",
  "egy.pt.570": "The sky is washed and bright and a god is born on the arms of Shu, Tefnut and the king. The king asks Wbn, Khepri, Nun, Atum and others to hear his words, be glad for him, a Great One son of a Great One, and take him with them. His mother calls him beautiful and Osiris calls him heir. He has not swallowed the eye of Horus or a limb of Osiris, so no one can say he must die for it. He lives on Atum's offering bread, protected by Nḫb.t, and is passed from one god to another. He has escaped his day, half-months, months and year of death as Set did. His hands hold up Nut like Shu, his bones are firm and he is a light-scattering star, so heaven and earth will never be without him. He is the fourth among the imperishable stars, by order of Horus. He takes the white crown, is the serpent of Set that was stolen and returned, and is the eye of Horus that was neither chewed nor spat out. He says Rē‘'s body is in him. A puzzling exchange between a baboon and knm.wt follows. He belongs to the first company, born before anger, noise, strife, and before the eye of Horus was torn out and Set's testicles torn off; he is blood from Isis and Nephthys and Rē‘'s deputy, and will not die. Geb and Thot are asked to help, and he rises in the east like Rē‘.",
  "egy.pt.571": "The king's mother in the lower sky conceived him and his father Atum gave him birth before the sky, earth, people, gods and death existed. He escapes the day of death as Set did and belongs to the gods of the lower sky, who do not perish because of their enemies or die for a king or any death; nor does he. He is an imperishable star, and Rē‘ has taken him to heaven to live like the one who sets in the west and rises in the east. He is passed from god to god and acclaimed as a star under Rē‘'s protection. Horus has set him on his shoulders and given him to Shu, and Rē‘, the Great God, is asked to give him his arm and staff so he may live for ever.",
  "egy.pt.572": "Isis says how beautiful it is to see this god rise to heaven, his fame over him, his terror around him and his magic before him. Atum did for the king what was once done for Atum: he brought him the gods of heaven and earth, who put their arms under him and made him a ladder to climb; the doors of heaven opened. Atum united the districts for him and Geb gave him the regions of Horus and Set and the Marsh of Reeds. The king is ’Iȝḥś, Ddwn and Sopdu. To those who said he would die, the spell says he will not: he lives for ever as the surviving bull at the head of the wild bulls.",
  "egy.pt.573": "The king tells Ḥsmnw, Eastern Horus, the Eastern Soul and Harachte to wake in peace: the god sleeps in the evening boat and wakes in the morning boat and is overseen by no one. He asks Rē‘, his father, to take him to Nut, open heaven's doors to him, seat him beside him on the horizon and commend him to several gods who will find him a place by the lake, set up his throne in heaven and make food grow for him on earth. He is one of the four gods ’Imś.ti, Ḥȝpi, Dwȝ-mw.t.f and Ḳbḥ-śn.w.f who live on truth and guard Upper Egypt. He flies from men like a bird, a falcon, a kite, freed from what tied his feet and hands on earth.",
  "egy.pt.574": "The king greets the sycamore that shelters the god, under which the gods of the underworld stand, whose tips are scorched and whose inside is burned. It is told to gather those in Nun and among the Bows, and the Great Mooring-post rests its head on its arm in mourning for Osiris. Osiris stands with his shadow over him and his diadem drives off Set; his dread is felt in heaven and on earth and in Buto. The king comes to Horus, heir of Geb, and the Two Enneads say everything belongs to him. He is among the gods in heaven and rejoices; day is day, night is night, Rē‘ is Rē‘, and he lasts for ever. A short part near the end is lost.",
  "egy.pt.575": "Sḥpw announces again and again that the son and beloved of Rē‘ is coming, and in turn Horus, Set, Geb and the Souls of Heliopolis and Buto each say they made him come. People standing beside the king on earth call to Rē‘ as he rises in the east, the south and the middle of the sky, asking him to give the king his hand and take him along. Messengers hurry ahead with the news.",
  "egy.pt.576": "Osiris was laid on his side by his brother Set, but he stirs; Rē‘ lifts his head, and he hates sleep and weariness. The refrain says the king does not rot or stink and is not bound by the gods' anger. Osiris and the king wake in peace, their heads lifted by Rē‘ and their smell like a serpent's. The king is the seed of Osiris and of Rē‘, called \"Horus chief of spirits\", the star that crosses the sea. He leaves his house dressed like Horus and Thot, born of Heliopolitan parents. He is one of the four sons of Geb, anointed, dressed in fine cloth, living on figs and wine, and asks the gods to share their food and goods with him, take his arm, rejoin his bones and seat him on his throne. He comes to his mother Nut, asks her to raise the sky and turn the stars for him and to make him smell like Osiris; Nun is to lift his arm so he can rise to serve Rē‘. Horus chief of spirits is told to wake in peace and to enter the king's name in his register.",
  "egy.pt.577": "Osiris rises pure and mighty, lord of truth and of the year, on New Year's day. Atum and every god in heaven, on earth and in every direction, district and city is satisfied with the great word Thot speaks about Osiris. Anubis, counter of hearts, moves Osiris the king from the earth's gods to heaven's. His year and hour know him. Atum calls \"Come, my child\", and the gods welcome their eldest brother. Heaven conceived him and the Dȝ.t bore him, and the king with him. Both hold up the sky on their right side and lean on the earth on their left, alive because the gods have decreed it. The king rose in the east, comes down as a green bird, and is purified in the lakes of the goose; a few words are lost.",
  "egy.pt.578": "Osiris the king is told not to hurry to the lands of the East but to the lands of the West, by the road of the Followers of Rē‘. His messengers run ahead to announce him to Rē‘. He holds those he does not know in his arms like herdsmen holding calves and does not let them slip. He goes out glorified, whip and sceptre in hand; enemies fall on their faces and the imperishable stars kneel. They know him as Anubis, and he stands as chief and eldest son of the gods, heir on the throne of Geb.",
  "egy.pt.579": "When Osiris the king leaves his house, it is Horus going to look for him. His messengers rush to tell Rē‘ that he has come as Geb's son, crossed the Winding Watercourse and the canal, and settled on the eastern side of the sky in the palace of the horizon. He gives the gods his arm, and they praise and greet him as they greet Rē‘.",
  "egy.pt.580": "The son speaks to the one who struck his father and killed someone greater than himself. He tells his father, Osiris the king, that he has struck down and killed the killer like an ox, and that Osiris now rides on his back. He has cut off the killer's head, tail, hands and feet, and the body is shared out among the gods: forelegs to Atum, thighs to Shu and Tefnut, sides to Geb and Nut, shoulder blades to Isis and Nephthys, shoulders to Mḫnti-’irti and Hrti, spine to Neit and Śerḳet, heart to Sekhmet, the rear to the four sons of Horus, and head, tail, hands and feet to Anubis and Osiris; the leftovers go to the Souls of Nekhen and Buto. Osiris is told to eat the red ox that Horus prepared for him.",
  "egy.pt.581": "The king's cave is the broad hall of Osiris, where the north wind blows and lifts him up. Gods come with water, wine and vases, and he stands and sits like Anubis. Aker stands up for him. People tremble at the rising flood, but the marshes laugh, the banks turn green, offerings come down, faces brighten and the gods rejoice. Runners call on the gods to free the king from his bandages, and the gods say \"Set is guilty; Osiris is justified.\" When the floods cover the land, others go to their caves, but he follows his spirit to Knm-’iwnw.",
  "egy.pt.582": "The king asks Horus to recite for him the great good word he recited for Osiris, which makes him great and powerful. He has his power within, his ba behind and his sharpness on him, so he can rest in heaven like a mountain. He will fly like a cloud or heron, with feathers sharp as knives; Śȝḥ and Sothis take his hand, and offerings are made. He sits on his firm throne with a glittering sceptre; when he raises his hand people stand and when he lowers it they sit. He has a jackal's face and judges like Sebek and Anubis. When he calls, the blessed dead greet him and ask who did this for him: it was his mother, the great wild cow with long feathers and hanging breasts, who lifted him up to heaven among the gods so that he too might be a spirit.",
  "egy.pt.583": "The text is badly broken. What survives asks Rē‘ to turn so the king may see, says Rē‘'s red crown and uraeus are the king's, and calls the father Shu and height. The arms of Horus and Thot are behind and before him, the two Great Gods support him and prepare his place in heaven, and he is told to rise to his feet.",
  "egy.pt.584": "Much of this is restored or lost. The king takes his seat and his oar in the bow of the Two Enneads' boat and rows Rē‘ to the west. Rē‘ writes his name above the living, sets his seat above the lords of kas, puts him on the shore of the Winding Watercourse and over the stars. The double doors in the sky are open for him, and he is pleased with his name.",
  "egy.pt.585": "Almost all the text is lost. Only scattered words survive, including \"stars\", \"they prepare\", \"upon the forehead\", \"lakes\", \"great\", \"It is N.\", \"truth\" and \"this\".",
  "egy.pt.586": "The king shines like Rē‘, driving away the dawn and setting up Truth behind Rē‘, shining every day for those on the horizon. The upper gates of heaven are open. Atum is great, son of a great one, and the king is a bright star among the gods. His mother tells him that Šśȝ weeps and mourns for him. Part is unclear; then His-back-behind-him is asked to bring the ladder made by Khnum so the king can climb to heaven and serve Rē‘ as a courtier.",
  "egy.pt.587": "The king greets Atum and Khepri, who made himself, and then the eye of Horus, which Horus decorated with his own hands. Addressing a city, the spell says it does not listen to West, East, South, North or the middle of the land, but only to Horus, who decorated, built and settled it; it does whatever he says and carries to him its waters full of birds, its trees, food and gifts, now and to come, wherever he wishes. Its doors stand firm and open to no one but Horus, who made them and saved them from Set's harm. Nut is told to go, as Geb ordered, in her name of \"City\". The king then says he is Horus and the whole address is repeated for him: he built and settled the city, so it must listen only to him, bring him everything, open its doors only to him, and not listen to the wrongdoer.",
  "egy.pt.588": "Osiris the king is told that his mother Nut has spread herself over him, made him a god even against his will, and protected him from all evil. He is the greatest of her children.",
  "egy.pt.589": "Osiris the king is told that he is the ka of all the gods; Horus has avenged him and he has become Horus's ka.",
  "egy.pt.590": "Osiris the king is told he has been avenged and lives, moving every day with nothing out of order in him. He settled things for his father, so his father did the same for him, like a vulture spreading herself over her son.",
  "egy.pt.591": "Horus, Set, Thot, the god and the king each put on their apron as they walk about their land. Horus is told to take back his eye, which was recognised as his in the house of the prince of Heliopolis, and the king is told his ka has recognised him despite his enemies.",
  "egy.pt.592": "The spell speaks to Geb, son of Shu, presenting Osiris the king and asking him to heal him. Geb is the eldest son of Shu, the only Great God, to whom Atum gave his inheritance and the whole Ennead. He is called \"wise mouth\", prince of the gods, judge at the head of the Ennead and stronger than any god, and he has come to protect Osiris the king from his enemy. He is asked to let his son live and prosper, to turn every evil away from him for good, and, as the ka who feeds and keeps all the gods alive, to make the king live too. Eyes go out from his head like the white and red crowns; Horus follows him out of love, and he rises as king, powerful over all the gods.",
  "egy.pt.593": "The king is told to stand and give Horus his arm, for Horus raises him; Geb has wiped his mouth. The Great Ennead avenged him and put Set under him to serve him, keeping Set's spit off him. Nut throws herself over him and protects and raises him as the greatest of her children. Isis and Nephthys hurry to him; Isis found him whole and great. He is told to embrace all things in his arms. Horus has brought Set and bent him under him, and has stayed with him and avenged him. Isis and Nephthys guarded him in Siût and worship him. Isis comes to him gladly, his seed enters her, and from it comes Horus, called \"Horus who was in Sothis\", who avenges his father.",
  "egy.pt.594": "The king has gone up to the gateway, rising as king, high like Wepwawet, holding himself up without tiring.",
  "egy.pt.595": "The speaker greets the king and says he has come to him on his day, after the night, and given him Nwtknw. He has brought the king's heart and put it back in his body, as Horus brought the heart to his mother Isis and she brought it to her son Horus.",
  "egy.pt.596": "Those who lie in graves, in hidden places, have risen. The king is told to wake and get up; his arms are there to serve him.",
  "egy.pt.597": "The king is told to come and clothe himself in the healthy eye of Horus, which was in Tȝi.t.",
  "egy.pt.598": "This is the eye of Horus that he gave to Osiris, given back so he can fit it to his face; it is also the sweet-smelling eye of incense and flame that Horus spoke of before Geb. The spell ends with a list: one pellet of incense, three pellets of incense, a bow.",
  "egy.pt.599": "The king says he is Geb, the \"wise mouth\", prince of the gods, whom Atum set at the head of the Ennead and whose words please all the gods. Atum tells the gods to gather and unite for him as they did for Atum in Heliopolis, and do everything for his good for ever. Geb is asked to give offerings of meat, bread, drink, cakes and birds to all the gods who will keep this pyramid and temple standing as the king loved it. Any gods who keep the pyramid and temple sound and lasting will be honoured and strong, and will receive royal offerings, linen and oil, and wear the white crown among the Two Enneads.",
  "egy.pt.600": "The king reminds Atum-Khepri how he rose as a hill, shone as the bnw in the temple in Heliopolis, and spat out Shu and Tefnut, then put his arms around them so his ka would be in them. He asks Atum to put his arms likewise around the king, his temple and his pyramid, so that the king's ka lasts in them for ever, and to protect them from all evil as he protected Shu and Tefnut. He calls on the Great Ennead of Heliopolis, Atum, Shu, Tefnut, Geb, Nut, Osiris, Isis, Set and Nephthys, not to stand apart while Atum protects the king against all gods and all the dead. He tells Horus that the king, his pyramid and temple are Osiris, and asks him to stay close. Thot has put the gods under him, and Horus has given him the gods to cheer him like temples.",
  "egy.pt.601": "The king asks the Great Ennead of Heliopolis to make him, his pyramid and his temple last for ever. Then, god by god, he asks that just as the names of Atum, Shu, Tefnut, Geb, Nut, Osiris (in Abydos and as First of the Westerners), Set, Horus, Rē‘, Mḫnti-’irti and Wȝd.t endure in their cities, so may his name, his pyramid and his temple endure for ever and ever.",
  "egy.pt.602": "The spell is spoken by the Earth, Geb, Osiris, Anubis and Wr-ḥb, asking that the king be made festive at the Feast of Horus and that the one among the falcons hurry to his ka. They ask that his eyes, nose, mouth and ears be opened and his two plumes flourish, and that he be let through by the god. The gods are told that once they have eaten, the king will find their leftovers; they should give him the rest, for he has come.",
  "egy.pt.603": "The father-king is told to rise, put his head back on, gather his limbs, stand on his feet and follow his heart. His messengers hurry, Anubis approaches, Ḥtp gives him his arm and Thot comes to the Two Enneads; he crossed the lake and avoided the Dȝ.t. Parts are lost. He comes to govern the cities and settlements and command those in Nun, and he rests alive in the West among the Followers of Rē‘.",
  "egy.pt.604": "The father-king, the great, is told to rise and sit before them. The windows of heaven are open for him and his steps of light are broad. The spell ends with a cry: \"O! Ho!\"",
  "egy.pt.605": "The son tells his father the king that he has brought him the green eye-paint that Horus put on Osiris, and puts it on him as Horus did for his father when he filled his empty eye with his full one.",
  "egy.pt.606": "Horus tells his father, Osiris the king, to stand up: he has come to purify him, bring him to life, and gather his bones, flesh and scattered limbs. He has struck the one who struck him and avenged him. By the order of Ḥrw, the king is placed on the throne of Rē‘-Atum to lead the blessed dead, will board Rē‘'s boat and command the gods, for he is Rē‘, born of Nut every day. He is to take Geb's inheritance before the Ennead, who ask \"Who is his equal?\" The two great gods set him on the throne as their eldest son, with Shu, Tefnut, Nun and Nnt on his four sides, and lead him to the pure places made for Rē‘. He comes into being as Khepri, rises as Rē‘ and turns back as Atum. The Two Enneads rejoice and greet him again and again as their brother, the eldest son, the one Set wronged, saying they will never let Set stop carrying him, and they tell him to rise, for he lives.",
  "egy.pt.607": "Nun fathered the king on his left hand as a child, and the king has no understanding. He is freed from the evil gods and will not be handed over to them.",
  "egy.pt.608": "The king is told to stand for his father, the Great One, and sit for his mother Nut, and give his hand to his son Horus, who is coming towards him.",
  "egy.pt.609": "The father-king is told that Nut gave birth to him in the West and Isis bore him at Chemmis, and the north wind takes hold of him. The Lake of Reeds and the Winding Watercourse are flooded and his canal is open, so he can cross to the horizon where the gods are born and be born with them; the reed-floats are laid down for Rē‘ and for him. Sothis is his sister and the morning star his mother, and he sits between them on the great throne. Four beings from the eastern sky are to carry his good words to Nḥb-kȝ.w, who will tell the Two Enneads. Hpnti takes his hand as he steps into the boat of Rē‘ with the king's offering.",
  "egy.pt.610": "The king, Geb's eldest son, is told to wake for Horus and stand up against Set; the Two Enneads tremble before him. The year's beginnings and the new moon are celebrated for him, and he is mourned as the tireless one of Abydos. The earth hears what Horus says as he makes his father a spirit, and tells him the doors of Aker and Geb are open. He goes out at Anubis's call, made a spirit like Thot and Anubis, to judge and lean on the Two Enneads. Where he goes, Horus goes; if he is held back, the gods are held back. He passes through Abydos, a ramp is laid to where Śȝḥ is, the ox of heaven takes his arm and he eats the gods' food. Ddwn gives him incense; Rē‘ calls him and makes him the morning star in the Marsh of Reeds. The portal of heaven opens, the gods rejoice, and he sits on his firm throne leading the spirits. He has plenty of the gods' herb, the earth opens his eyes, his limbs are gathered, trees bow to him and Anubis gives him a royal offering.",
  "egy.pt.611": "The father is told he lives, named \"With the gods\", and will rise as Wepwawet, a soul at the head of the living and the mighty one at the head of the spirits. The king is a great one among the spirits and a Thot among the gods. The bolt of the ram-gates that hold people back is drawn for him; he counts his enemies and takes the hand of the imperishable stars. His eyes and ears are open, and he is to enter the guardian's house and let Geb guard him. The waters are gathered for Horus, who will avenge his father, and for the king. A vulture greater than he bows to him three times, and he enjoys the scent of the crown.",
  "egy.pt.612": "The king's going is to be like Horus going to his father Osiris, so that he becomes a spirit, a soul, honoured and mighty. Some lines are lost. He is told to gather his bones and limbs, shake the dust off his body, take his four jars and be dried by Isis like Horus, and rise towards the eye of Rē‘. He is to sit on his firm throne, scratching at the castle door with his nails, and travel the regions of Horus and Set. The end is broken.",
  "egy.pt.613": "The text is badly broken. Hdhd, ferryman of the Winding Watercourse, comes, and Osiris the king arrives on the right side of the Marsh of Offerings behind the two Great Gods to hear what they say. Washing hands and Tefnut are mentioned. It ends: if Tefnut or Shu seize him, Rē‘ will no longer shine on the horizon for every god to see.",
  "egy.pt.614": "Much is lost. The king goes to the gate of the house of Bȝ and gives his hand to those who greet him, but strikes them with something now lost, as befits the honoured lords.",
  "egy.pt.615": "The eye of Horus is set on the wing of his brother Set. The ropes are tied and the boats gathered so that the son of Atum is not without a boat, and the king is with him.",
  "egy.pt.616": "The king calls on the one in the hand of the ferryman of the Marsh of Reeds to bring the boat and ferry him across.",
  "egy.pt.617": "Much of the text is lost. The king is told to hurry and join the gods in Heliopolis. The king makes an offering \"in all thy places\" and \"in all thy dignities\". He goes in his sandals and slaughters an ox; the rest is broken.",
  "egy.pt.618": "Almost all of this is lost. It begins by telling people to be quiet and listen, and mentions the First of the Westerners.",
  "egy.pt.619": "The king is told to rise from his left side onto his right and wash his hands in the fresh water his son has brought. The son has grown barley and reaped spelt for his father's feasts. The king has a jackal's face, and a stairway is built for him to climb to heaven, where he judges between the two great gods. Isis weeps for him, Nephthys calls him and ’Imt.t sits at the foot of his throne. He takes his two oars, one of pine and one of another wood, crosses the lake of his house and takes revenge on whoever wronged him. The spell ends asking the great lake to protect him.",
  "egy.pt.620": "Horus tells Osiris the king that he will not let him fall ill, and tells him to come out and wake up, for he will avenge him.",
  "egy.pt.621": "Osiris the king is told to take the scent of the eye of Horus, like the eye that Horus tracked down by its smell.",
  "egy.pt.622": "The speaker tells Osiris the king that he has adorned him with the eye of Horus, Rnn-wt.t, whom the gods fear, so the gods fear him as they fear the eye.",
  "egy.pt.623": "Osiris the king is told to take to himself the eye of Horus, which made its śtnf (the meaning of this word is unclear).",
  "egy.pt.624": "The king has set out on the sea of the ferryman and gone up with the help of Khepri's wing. Nut takes his hand and prepares his way, and the falcon defends him from the crew of Rē‘'s boat. They are asked to carry him and set him among the imperishable stars, where he will not perish. He is a judge among the great gods. Part is unclear or lost; he rises like Rē‘ and is Osiris come out of the night.",
  "egy.pt.625": "The text has many gaps. The king says he is the sceptre in Grg.w-bȝ, has come down on the perch and gone up among the great ones, climbing the ladder with his foot on Śȝḥ. He took hold of the reins of a god who leads him to the great place, where he takes his throne in the divine boat. He is a prince of heaven with a house among the lords of names; his name is on the horizon and the ‘hm.w fear him. A great game-board is mentioned, and he brings back to life those who fell in a fight. He asks He-who-sees-behind-him for the ladder made by Khmun to climb to heaven and serve Rē‘.",
  "egy.pt.626": "The king has gone up like a swallow and landed like a falcon. Part is lost; all his fortresses and the god's two districts have been given to him.",
  "egy.pt.627": "The text is broken in many places. The king is a well-equipped spirit; heaven shakes and earth quakes. He was born at the feast of the month and conceived at the half-month, coming out with a grasshopper's shell. Conceived at night, he rises to Rē‘ every day and his chapel opens when Rē‘ appears. He rose on a rain-cloud, put truth in place of error so that heaven and earth rejoiced, and his true judgement earned him his post. He is the great falcon who crosses the sky on four geese and perches on the house of the one with the hidden name. His lips, neck, claws and wings are compared to those of various divine beings. He went to the field of the glorified, lit up the earth, and goes to his seat, eating from the Marshes of Offerings and the malachite lakes. He guides Rē‘ into his boats at the year's-end feast.",
  "egy.pt.628": "Nephthys speaks to the king, calling him twice: she has come, taken hold of him and put his heart back in his body.",
  "egy.pt.629": "The speaker tells Osiris the king that she has come, rejoicing out of love for him.",
  "egy.pt.630": "The speaker tells Osiris the king that this spring is in him, and says she is the water-hole and the overflowing.",
  "egy.pt.631": "The speaker says she has gathered up her brother and joined his limbs together.",
  "egy.pt.632": "The speaker says her heart is full of the place where he is, and exclaims how harmful, how bad and how strong his smell is.",
  "egy.pt.633": "This one-line spell says: you are the one who weeps for him.",
  "egy.pt.634": "The text is badly broken. The speaker tells Osiris the king twice that he has brought him something, now lost, in which there are spirits, and that he lives somewhere with a female figure.",
  "egy.pt.635": "The speaker tells Osiris the king he has brought him the eye of Horus from Tȝi.t, Rn(n)-wt.t, whom the gods fear, so they fear him as they fear Horus. Horus has put his eye on the king's forehead as \"Great-in-charms\", so he will rise as king of Upper and Lower Egypt.",
  "egy.pt.636": "The speaker asks the Great Watcher for his arm so he can raise him. He has come to look for him, protect him and avenge him without delay. He lives and is healthier than others (a few words are lost). The father, Osiris the king, lives and has put on the eye of Horus.",
  "egy.pt.637": "Horus came with ointment, looking for his father Osiris, and found him lying on his side in Gḥś.ti; Osiris filled himself with his son's eye. The speaker comes to the king to fill him with the ointment from the eye of Horus, which will gather his bones, join his limbs, collect his flesh and let his bad sweat drain away. Its scent will make him smell sweet like Rē‘ rising on the horizon, and the gods who follow Osiris delight in him. He has taken the white crown in the form of Osiris and is more a spirit than the spirits, as Horus ordered.",
  "egy.pt.638": "Osiris the king is told the gods have fastened his face back on, and Horus has given him his eye and opened it so he can see with it; the eye is called \"She who opens the ways of god\".",
  "egy.pt.639": "Osiris the king is told to take the living eye of Horus and see with it. His face is opened and lit by the light as the earth is lit. The speaker says he has given him the eye as Rē‘ gives light, opened his eye, and given him the ointment.",
  "egy.pt.640": "The speaker tells Geb that Osiris the king is his son and asks him to keep him alive and well so he does not die: if the king lives and is well, so is Geb. Geb is told to be great, honoured, a soul and mighty, to drive out every evil from Osiris the king and serve him so that he does not die or perish.",
  "egy.pt.641": "Horus says he has come to speak for Osiris the king as his son. The king is Geb's eldest son and heir, who rises after him; the Ennead gave him the inheritance and he has power over every god. The rest is broken, apart from a mention of an eye going out from his head.",
  "egy.pt.642": "The text is badly broken. Shu is told he holds all things in his arms and keeps Osiris the king from escaping him. It mentions Atum, who masturbated for him, and a ka, and asks for protection from something now lost.",
  "egy.pt.643": "The text is badly broken. It tells Osiris the king that he lives and is a god with power over the gods, with an eye going out from his head like the white crown. His ka stands among the gods, and he is told to draw his ka, life and joy along after him.",
  "egy.pt.644": "The children of Horus are told to get under the king and carry him so that he does not slip away from them, carrying him like Horus in the ḥnw-boat.",
  "egy.pt.645": "Osiris the king is told that Horus has carried him in the ḥnw-boat as a god named Seker, as he carries his father, and joins with him. He will have power over Upper and Lower Egypt as Horus, and his body will be protected from his enemy.",
  "egy.pt.646": "The Ennead has put the king's enemy under him, as was said to him in his name of Osiris. Horus has made him great in his magic, in his name of \"Great-in-charms\".",
  "egy.pt.647": "Osiris the king is told that Horus has carried him and lifted him into the ḥnw-boat in his name of Seker. A few words are lost; he is equipped with Horus and will have power over Lower Egypt as Horus.",
  "egy.pt.648": "Osiris the king is told that Horus has given him his four children to rule over. Horus told them to get under the king and carry him, with none of them slipping away; they came and carried him, and none escaped.",
  "egy.pt.649": "Osiris the king is told that Geb has given him all the gods to join with him; he rules them and they treat him as a brother and have not rejected him. Horus has gathered all the gods, their lands and goods into his arms to serve him as a bodyguard, and he is a great god, curved like the \"Circle which encircles the nb.wt\". He has been avenged, lives, moves daily and has no disorder in him; he is the ka of all the gods and has become Horus's ka. An eye goes out from his head as the crown of Upper and of Lower Egypt, and he rises as king of both, powerful over the gods and their kas. He is the two souls and will not die; he has spoken and rejoiced, has a ba and life, and destroys his enemies. Horus has made peace with him, and peace is wished on his head.",
  "egy.pt.650": "The opening is lost. Osiris the king, son of Nut, was made to appear by her as king of Upper and Lower Egypt in all his dignity, as Anubis, as Osiris son of Geb, and as ‘nd.ti. The earth produces him to lead the gods of heaven, as Geb leads the Ennead. His mother, heaven, gives birth to him alive every day, beloved of Rē‘, rising with him in the east and setting with him in the west. Rē‘ gives him life and gladdens his heart, settles the South and North for him, destroys the fortresses of Asia and puts all rebellious people under his fingers.",
  "egy.pt.651": "Osiris the king is told to take the eye of Horus to his forehead; the rest is lost.",
  "egy.pt.652": "Osiris the king is told to take the eye of Horus, which the speaker took back from Set after Set had damaged it. The next line is lost.",
  "egy.pt.653": "Osiris the king is told to take the eye of Horus; a gap follows, and the last surviving word is \"a libation\".",
  "egy.pt.654": "Most of the text is lost. What survives mentions braids of hair and says \"so that he may pass when he comes\".",
  "egy.pt.655": "The text is badly broken. Šsmtt says the king comes out as a falcon, as the eye of Horus, with a serpent coming out like a falcon. Birds go to the sky and to the earth; the king reaches heaven like the divine falcons, the great unharmed star. The boat of Seker and the lakes of the jackal are mentioned, and the king makes his way towards \"you\".",
  "egy.pt.656": "Almost all of the text is lost. The surviving words mention the king and \"a message of the Ennead\".",
  "egy.pt.657": "Almost all of the text is lost. What survives says the king came, and mentions the First of the Westerners on his throne.",
  "egy.pt.658": "The text is very broken. The king is told the gods have made him prosper and love him, calling him \"Ceaseless\". Someone quakes beneath him like the earth and cannot escape him; he is higher than that one and is told to spring upon him. Horus has put his enemies under him, given him health and wealth, filled him with his eye called \"The enduring one\", and saved his eye from Set. Most of the rest is lost, apart from mentions of \"Inundation\", power and the eye.",
  "egy.pt.659": "The king's travels are the travels of Horus searching for his father Osiris; his messengers hurry to tell Rē‘ in the east that he is coming as a god and stands in the palace of the horizon. Rē‘ says to him, \"I am your brother, like Sopdu.\" He is announced as Mḫnti-n-’irti. The god does not recognise him, though he spends the night in his arms, kept from decay, like a calf with its herdsman. He has taken white teeth that fly like arrows; his beef is in Abydos and his meat in Nubia. He comes down like Anubis, stands by the lake like Geb at the head of his Ennead, and has his heart and ka, furnishes his house and fastens his door.",
  "egy.pt.660": "Osiris the king is called Shu, the great and eldest son of Atum, whom Atum spat out of his mouth and told to count his children. Shu is told that he has kept Osiris the king alive and lasting: if Shu lives, the king lives, for Shu is lord of the whole earth.",
  "egy.pt.661": "The father-king is told to take the milk from the breasts of his mother Isis, and Nephthys is asked to give him her hand.",
  "egy.pt.662": "The king greets the shining Khepri: each is on the way to the other, and their lives travel towards each other. The papyrus that grows from Wȝd.t has come out as the king and the king as it, and he is strong through it. He has appetite at breakfast and plenty at supper; hunger has no power over him and fire is far from him, for he lives on Rē‘'s plenty every day. The son tells his father to rise and take his first libation from Chemmis; those in their graves are to rise and loosen their bandages. He is told to shake the sand off his face, turn from his left side to his right and see what his son and heir has done: grown wheat and barley for his feasts. The eye of Horus is offered to him, and as lord of the house he has his hand on his property.",
  "egy.pt.663": "The beginning is broken. The king is told his bread is there every day, and a list follows: a red ox, and a thousand each of figs, jars of wine, bread, and ground grain. The father-king is greeted and told his water, flood and the milk in his mother Isis's breasts are his.",
  "egy.pt.664": "This is a group of short sayings, some broken. The speaker greets the king as Osiris and says he has come to take him and give him his heart, as his servant. Isis says she has come to where he lives to put his name in him. Someone avenges him and shines on him as the horizon from which Rē‘ rises, and stays in his arms. The speaker says he has joined the king's limbs and put his heart back in its place in his body. Horus says he has come because of what was done for the king. Nut comes to protect, embrace and raise him, and says she was born of this palace like him. It ends with the title \"Chapter of Osiris N.\"",
  "egy.pt.665": "This long spell, pieced together from two copies, has the son, Horus, telling the king to wake. The king lives \"with the spirits\", rises as Wpi.w, chief of the living and of the spirits, and is Thot in his palace; his hand carries him to the imperishable stars. He is told to rise from sleep, take his four jars from the divine lake and the sceptre Nekhbet gave him, and look over the regions of Horus and Set. He has shed his bonds and been freed from Hr.ti, who lives on human hearts. Thot finds him on his ebony throne like Rē‘; he speaks to the spirits and rules them. Raised up, his bones and limbs gathered, he is purified, commands the gods' houses and smites his enemies; the necropolis doors open and he is offered a thousand of bread, beer, cattle, birds and clothes. He is told to sleep no more but wake, with the sweet smell of the ’Iḫ.t-wt.t; he rises like Geb, and \"his death is gone from him\". His spirit and heart are in his body. He is to take the eye of Horus as his bread, rise to heaven with the stars, strike down those before him and guard against those behind, and protect himself against Nwtknw, who has harmed him. The doors of heaven open; he travels through the lands of Horus and Set like Min, transfigured and not decayed, with bread every day. Some lines are obscure or broken.",
  "egy.pt.666": "The king is told to pass the great lake to the spirits and the water to the dead, and to beware of the people who live in the bush there, who will not take his hand. The pyramid and the east are his protectors. He is to go to Dȝ.w-’ib, Seker's beloved brother, who will make a way for him to eat bread with them and row the boat, while the sky trembles, the earth quakes and the imperishable stars come to him. That god took his hand at a feast in the Marsh of Reeds as he sat on his firm throne judging with the Two Enneads. Some lines are unclear.",
  "egy.pt.667": "This long spell, some of it obscure or broken, tells the king to take back his head, teeth and hair. He goes out facing the sea, sits as chief of the great ones, keeps the sky, shakes the earth and protects the stars. The speaker says he came looking for him in hidden places and up to heaven. Grain, white bread, flat-cakes and drink are set before the gods for him. He is to rise from his left side to his right, sit on the seats of Rē‘, wash his back and enjoy a thousand of bread, beer, cattle, birds and linen, eating meat at the place of slaughter for ever; he must eat it alone, not share it with those beside him. On this third morning he goes to heaven with the imperishable stars,. A second part says it is lovely to see Osiris at the door of the gods: his sanctuary opens heaven's doors so he can live among the gods. He is not like the dead; he is alive among the spirits. At flood time Horus and Set purify each other with their eyes and the king is purified of all evil. He is to gather his bones, take his head, sit for his bread, rise like Min, grow wings and feathers, light the sky and stay chief of heaven. The speaker says he has threshed barley and reaped spelt for his living. Isis and Nephthys stand beside him; he is told again to gather his bones, his mother cleans him, and he is offered a thousand of stone vessels, linen, cattle, birds and sweet things so he can celebrate like a god.",
  "egy.pt.668": "The king is the crying falcon circling the eye of Horus in the Dȝ.t. A few lines are lost. He is a sacrificing falcon who has put \"you\" there, and he goes to the eastern sky where he was conceived and born.",
  "egy.pt.669": "The text has gaps. A prince rises at a great burnt offering inside the horizon and sees the feast and fire-pan prepared at the birth of the gods on the five extra days of the year. The king, born a twin in the nest near Thot, is called the speaker's brother, who parted the two fighting brothers. Isis tells Nun she has borne him and spat him out, but he has no feet or arms, so how can he be put together? The gods say to bring copper and the ḥnw-boat, and ask how to break his egg. Seker of Pdw is to come, cast his bones and build his skeleton, break the egg and loosen the copper, so the two sharp-toothed, long-clawed followers of the gods can draw him out. Now he exists, is assembled and has broken his egg. Asked how he will fly, they bring the ḥnw-boat built by Mw-ḥn, with the south wind as his foster-mother and the north wind as his nurse. He flies and lands on the feathers of his father Geb.",
  "egy.pt.670": "The doors of heaven are open. The gods in Buto, moved by the weeping of Isis and Nephthys, come to Osiris the king as he comes out of the Dȝ.t; the Souls of Buto dance and mourn, beating themselves and tearing their hair, and tell him he has gone and come, slept and woken, died and lives. They tell him to stand and see what his son Horus has done: struck and killed his attacker like a bull, bound him and put him under his great daughter, so the mourning stopped. Osiris speaks to Horus after the evil was ended on the fourth and eighth days. The king, purified, becomes Wepwawet, led by Horus, with his enemies brought by Thot. Isis and Nephthys pour his libation, gathered his flesh and bound his limbs; Atum gives him the evening and morning boats. The four sons of Horus washed his face, dried his tears and opened his mouth with copper fingers. He goes to Atum's hall and the Marsh of Reeds, and Shu and Tefnut, who row him, give him heaven, earth and the Marsh of Reeds. The god wakes and rises for this spirit, Osiris the king. Some words are restored.",
  "egy.pt.671": "The king is told he is the son of a Great One; he is purified in the lake Dȝ.ti and takes his throne in the Marsh of Reeds.",
  "egy.pt.672": "The king is told the truth of Horus is his truth, and he comes clothed and robed. He has inherited the one who is no longer mourned and who comes into being smiling. He is greeted and told he comes in peace.",
  "egy.pt.673": "The father-king has come like a god, and his messengers race up to heaven to tell Rē‘ that he stands in the palace of the horizon and sits on the copper throne of his father Geb, the wonder of the gods. The Two Enneads greet him, he commands people like Min and Horus, and Set has not escaped carrying his weight.",
  "egy.pt.674": "Horus says he has come as the king's son to give him his staff before the spirits and his sceptre before the imperishable stars. He found him whole, with a jackal's face, and Ḳbḥ.wt refreshes his heart in the house of Anubis. He is told to be pure and sit at the head of those greater than he, on the throne of the First of the Westerners. Goddesses greet him like Isis and Nephthys; he stands at the head of the palace like Min, of the Egyptians like Ḥapi, and at Pdw-š like Seker. He has his sceptre and nails; those before Thot are killed with Set's knife. He gives his arm to the dead and the spirits, who lead him to the First of the Westerners.",
  "egy.pt.675": "Osiris and the Great God tell the king, the Great God's messenger, to come in peace. The doors of heaven and the stars are open for him, now that he has gone down as the jackal of Upper Egypt and as Anubis. The great maiden of Heliopolis gives him her arm. He has no human father or mother; his mother is the great wild cow of el-Kâb, with the white crown, long feathers and hanging breasts, who will nurse him and never wean him. He is told to rise, put on his best fringed robe, take his mace and sceptre and stand as chief of the palace, judging the gods' words; he belongs to the stars when Rē‘ shines behind the morning star. No god can go back on his word, and he will give the king a thousand of bread, beer, oxen, geese and everything gods live on.",
  "egy.pt.676": "The king's water, plenty and the fluid from Osiris are his. He is told to gather his bones, arrange his limbs, shake off the dust and untie his bandages; the tomb, the coffin and heaven are open for him. Isis and Nephthys greet their brother at Atum's feast. His libations are in Busiris; his soul is in his body. He is to rise, travel south and north, and take the jackal spirits Horus has given him; Anubis orders him purified with eight jars of each kind. He holds up the sky and beautifies the earth, and mourners lament as he rises as the morning star. Geb is asked to take his hand, seat him on the great seat, cleanse his mouth and nails, and do for him what was done for Osiris on the day his bones were counted; the wise and both palaces come to greet him.",
  "egy.pt.677": "A Great One has fallen on his side but is up like a god, with his sceptre and white crown, and the king likewise, his crown like Rē‘'s as he rises and is greeted by Horus. He is told to rise and take the rank the Two Enneads gave him: he sits on Osiris's throne as First of the Westerners, with his sceptre and great white crown. How fine, it says, is what his father Osiris has done in giving him his throne to rule and lead the hidden ones, followed by all the glorified dead. He is happy and proud, an Atum who will not leave his destiny; Rē‘ calls him \"Him whom all the glorified fear\", and the gods dread him as they dread Rē‘. He is told to take his jackal's face, stand up and sit down to a thousand of bread, beer, oxen, geese and every good thing. Rē‘ finds him standing with his mother Nut, who leads him on the ways of the horizon to his home, where it is good to be with his ka for ever.",
  "egy.pt.678": "The king tells ’Iḥmti and Śdmti not to listen to him or ask him for his magic: they have their magic and he has his. He hopes he will not break their pen or crack their palette, and asks for his offering.",
  "egy.pt.679": "The king is told his water, outflow and flood, which come from Osiris, are his. He handles them like Horus and opens them like Wepwawet, for he is the Great One, the Eldest Son.",
  "egy.pt.680": "Osiris the king is told to take the eye of Horus, for it is his.",
  "egy.pt.681": "The king asks great heaven, great Nut, to give him her hand, for he is her divine falcon. He goes up to heaven, opens the sky and greets his father Rē‘, who crowns him as a Horus, gives him two real crowns and sets his two divine eyes in place. He rises high as Horus of the sky, strikes the northern crowns and commands the Wtn.w. Followers come after him and beings in heaven and on earth greet him. Anointed, clothed and living on offerings, he gives orders, hands out honours and places, makes offerings and leads the presentations; he is the one of heaven, with power before Nut.",
  "egy.pt.682": "Seker greets the king, and Dwȝ-wr washes his face. He flies high like a divine falcon, is cool like a heron and flies low like a goose, with falcon's wings. His bones are joined and he is purified, wearing his headband, bodice and girdle. He goes down into Rē‘'s great boat, which carries him to the horizon to judge the gods with Rē‘ and Horus, for he is one of them.",
  "egy.pt.683": "The gods' word has come upon the king: he is Horus coming out of the Nile, the bull coming out of the fortress, a serpent coming from Rē‘ and another from Set. Whatever happens to him also happens to two daughters of Rē‘, Mdd.t-’it and Mdȝ. He is Wdȝ, son of Wdȝ; as surely as the eye of Horus is whole and alive in Heliopolis, he is whole and alive.",
  "egy.pt.684": "The king rose when Osiris rose and has spoken with his ka in heaven; his bones are firm and his limbs are like the imperishable stars. If he is surrounded, a great one will fall into his hands. Nut, Shu and Tefnut are his mother and father, and take him to heaven on incense smoke. He sits beside Osiris and beside Horus and spits on their hair so it will not sicken or fall out, at every half-month and month feast. He is one of the four sons of Atum and Nut who never rot, decay or fall from heaven to earth; he was looked for and found among them. He rests under the belly of the sky like a star by the Winding Watercourse. When he rises, he is to be given the saying \"Rē‘ is good each day\", and he places himself on Horus's road to the fine ways of heaven and the Marsh of Offerings.",
  "egy.pt.685": "The waters of life in the sky and on earth come; the sky burns and the earth trembles before the god's birth. The two mountains split, a god comes into being and controls his body, and so does the king. His feet will be kissed by the pure waters made through Atum, Shu and Tefnut, which purify him and cense him. He lifts the sky with his hand and treads the earth with his foot. A libation is poured at his gate and every god's face is washed; Osiris and the king wash their arms. The scent of the serpent is on him; bread is in Seker's house and meat in Anubis's. He is whole, the palace stands, the moon is born and the district lives. Barley and spelt are grown for his offerings for ever.",
  "egy.pt.686": "Horus and Set both have ointment. Horus has taken his eye back from his enemies without Set gaining from it; he fills himself with ointment, is satisfied with his eye, and its scent is his while its anger falls on his enemies. The king, too, fills himself with ointment; its scent joins him and its anger falls on his enemies.",
  "egy.pt.687": "The speaker brings the king the hot eye of Horus and tells him its scent is his. Through it he is a ba, mighty and honoured, and wins the crown among the gods. Horus rejoices at his coming as at the coming of his own eye. Equipped as a god with his bones gathered like Osiris, he is greeted by the gods as they greet Rē‘ rising on the horizon.",
  "egy.pt.688": "The four grandsons of Horus of Letopolis, ’Ims.ti, Ḥȝpi, Dwȝ-mu.t.f and Ḳbḥ-śn.w.f, tie and fix a ladder so the king can climb to Khepri in the eastern sky. Its rungs are cut by Šśȝ, its ropes made from the sinews of the bull of heaven, its sides bound like a hide, and a support set under it by Śpḥ-wr.t. They are told to lift his ka to the god and lead him past the two lions to Atum, who has done as he promised and fixed the ladder. So the king is taken away from what people find horrible, and the gods do not find him horrible. He has not eaten a forbidden plant or goose at the new month, nor slept at night. The dwellers in the Dȝ.t listen to his voice as he comes down among them; \"Heavy-is-his-sceptre\" tells them he is one of them. He is mighty among them and honoured in the house of the two lions, for his wrong is driven off by the one who drives away evil.",
  "egy.pt.689": "Geb has raised the eye of Horus, Kȝȝ, which is over his great kas. The eye is given its head so it can see Horus seated, and judgement can take place (some words are lost). Isis comes holding her breasts for her son Horus, who is vindicated. The king has found the eye of Horus, made fierce like Rē‘'s brow and a crocodile, and followed it to the stars, and goes to row Horus with his eye. Shu, who holds up Nut, has carried the eye up to the stars because Horus sits on his firm throne.",
  "egy.pt.690": "Osiris and the king are told to wake: the weary god stands up and controls his body. Horus dresses the king in linen; he is equipped as a god, sitting with the Two Enneads. Rē‘ tells him to rise and come in peace as the great god's messenger, going to heaven through the doors of the horizon, sent by Geb, a soul and powerful like a god. He comes from Heliopolis, avenged, his heart back in his body, his face like a jackal's, his flesh like Atum's, with Isis before him and Nephthys behind, travelling the lands of Horus and Set led by Shu and Tefnut. Horus weaves a tent over his head and Set stretches his canopy. The four sons of Horus bring him the name \"Imperishable\": he will not perish or die. Ḳbḥ.wt purifies him and he appears as a jackal, leading spirits and stars, freed of the evil of Osiris. Horus guides him up to heaven and Nut takes his arm so he lives like the beetle. Sky and earth tremble before him; the earth does not cover him. The flood comes and Geb groans; the speaker says he has pitied him and struck his attacker so he may rise. Horus does for him what he did for Osiris. The speaker ends by saying he has wept and mourned for him, will never forget him, and will bring him offerings at every feast, asking him to clothe his body and come to him.",
  "egy.pt.691": "The king reminds his father Rē‘ that Rē‘ wished for a glorious, crowned, honoured and mighty son with a long reach and a wide stride, and says: here I am, your son. He is purified, takes the rudder and steers happily on the shoulder of the sky and with the Ennead; this is said twice. Another section says the reed-floats are laid down for Rē‘ so he may travel high from east to west beside his brother Śȝḥ and sister Sothis, and the same for the king (here a queen, though the text uses \"he\"). A third section has the son wake his father Osiris, saying he loves him and has brought back what Set carried off; Set had gloated over him beside his mourning sisters Isis and Nephthys. The son asks not to be passed by, says he has rowed him and that his front is like a jackal's, and that he has grown barley and spelt for his feasts. Some lines are obscure. A final section is almost entirely lost; scattered words mention the king's might and throne, the sceptres, the morning boat, Harachte, Khepri and Dwȝ-mw.tf.",
  "egy.pt.692": "Almost all of this spell is lost; only the isolated sounds ’i and ’in survive between gaps, and they cannot be translated.",
  "egy.pt.693": "The text is badly broken. What survives tells the king to wake; the Bows bow their heads to him and the gods rejoice. Geb, prince of the gods, is mentioned, and someone has taken the inheritance. The king is told to put on his white crown and eat bread, with an offering.",
  "egy.pt.694": "The text has many gaps. Isis and Nephthys say they have found Osiris lying on his side on the shore of Ndi.t; one calls him brother, says she searched for him, and tells him to rise as a spirit. Geb says he has struck someone, and something is done for Atum so the king may be among the gods on the ocean. Those in Nun and the blessed dead come to him, and his yearly offerings are made. His way is open. He is Thot, chief of heaven, and Anubis, chief of the house, and the bittern from the marsh. Something in the four tnw is told to leave him; the tongue in his mouth is pure; he is protected so he will not be upside down. He is the bull, with three meals in heaven and two on earth.",
  "egy.pt.695": "The text is badly broken. The diadem comes into being and the king is told to sit on the throne of Rē‘, which Horus moved to the south of the sky. The king sits upon the sky with his hands on Heliopolis, his head above and his legs below. A following of Set is mentioned.",
  "egy.pt.696": "The text is badly broken and the end entirely lost. The speaker calls on the strong jackal Dḳḳ to bring certain things to the king, the messenger of Atum, with linen of Tȝi.t, crying \"O! O! come\", and to lift him up. The eye of Horus and the fingers of Set are mentioned. The king is told to rise, and he rose up in this night.",
  "egy.pt.697": "The earth opens its mouth and Geb tells the king he is great like a king and mighty like Rē‘, purified in the lakes of the jackal and the Dȝt. The Two Enneads say \"Come in peace\", and ’Imn-kȝm opens the eastern door of heaven. Nut, long-haired with hanging breasts, takes him in her arms up to heaven, gives birth to him like Śȝḥ and makes him chief of the palaces. He boards the boat like Rē‘, is carried by the tireless stars, commands the imperishable ones and steers across the ocean. His messengers tell Rē‘ he has come in peace. He is warned not to take the western waterways, for those who go there do not return, but the eastern ones among the Followers of Rē‘. The end is lost.",
  "egy.pt.698": "This is not the beginning of the spell and most of it is lost. What survives mentions the gods and flesh, and tells someone to protect himself and get out of the way behind the king.",
  "egy.pt.699": "The text is broken. Anubis takes the king's arm and Nut gives him his heart. He flies like a falcon and rises out of the water like a bird, going west. He is told he lives and is young, going up beside his father and Śȝḥ to heaven.",
  "egy.pt.700": "The text has gaps. The father-king is told to rise on his right side and lean on his left; his flesh has been gathered and he is pure as a god. Rē‘'s messengers come for him, the imperishable stars take his arm, and he does not die. His bread offering is like the eye of Horus. His enemies are destroyed and perish, foaming against him; they are to be thrown into the lake and the sea. People come to him; the rest is lost.",
  "egy.pt.701": "The text has gaps. The Great One has fallen in Ndi.t, and Isis is freed of her burden; he is told to rise. Horus comes from Chemmis, purifies himself at Buto and comes to avenge his father. Isis and Nephthys say they are his loving sisters, and weep and keep watch for him. The king is to rise and receive a thousand of bread, beer, cattle and geese, roast meat and bread from the hall. He has his crown on his head, taken before the Two Enneads, and is a spirit among his brothers. He is told to stand and sit before his heart like Anubis, First of the Westerners, for he has returned to his proper state.",
  "egy.pt.702": "The king comes to the great and powerful pair of goddesses on the eastern side of heaven, so that they will carry him and set him there.",
  "egy.pt.703": "The king's soul is with him as Osiris; he is told to live and not die. Horus comes, unwraps his bandages, throws off his bonds and drives off his rivals, so the earth-gods cannot seize him. His father and mother are not human: his mother is the great uraeus, the white crown of el-Kâb, with speckled feathers and hanging breasts. The end is lost.",
  "egy.pt.704": "The king is a male who came from Rē‘, born from between the thighs of the Ennead; Šsm.t.t conceived and bore him. He is a falcon from Rē‘ and the living serpent from Rē‘'s eye, flying and hovering over Khepri's throne in the bow of his boat in the sky.",
  "egy.pt.705": "Almost all of this spell is lost; only the words \"thy father\" survive.",
  "egy.pt.706": "Most of the text is lost. What survives mentions the name of Rē‘, says the king was nursed on someone's milk, and mentions \"your arm\".",
  "egy.pt.707": "Most of the text is lost. What survives says the king's mother is Satis, and mentions a guide.",
  "egy.pt.708": "Almost all of the text is lost; only the king and the ‘bȝ-sceptre are mentioned.",
  "egy.pt.709": "Almost all of the text is lost. Surviving words mention the king's name, \"his beloved son, coming forth from\", and \"to exist\".",
  "egy.pt.710": "The text is badly broken. The priest of Rē‘ says something to the king, someone satisfies the spirits, and an incense pellet is broken. Horus puts his arm around the king, natron is mentioned, and the king is told he is Thot who avenges the eye, not Set who took it.",
  "egy.pt.711": "Most of the text is lost. What survives says the king has seen the Delta and sails there to a shore.",
  "egy.pt.712": "Almost all of the text is lost; only the words \"behind N.\" (the king) survive.",
  "egy.pt.713": "Almost all of the text is lost; only the words \"to attack\" and the king's name survive.",
  "egy.pt.714": "Almost all of this spell is lost; only the letter N., standing for the king's name, survives between gaps.",
};
